  1112   (427)  
  1113   (428)  
  1114   (429)      % ONE KIND, BOTH DIRECTIONS. We send it with an empty text to ask; the room
  1115   (430)      % answers with the same kind and the members' names in the text, separated
  1116   (431)      % by single spaces. A second kind for the reply would buy nothing - we know
  1117   (432)      % we asked, and a room never asks us.
  1118   (433)      %
  1119   (434)      % This is the eleventh kind, and the note that stood here saying there was
  1120   (435)      % no eleventh kind is now wrong and has gone. What it warned about still
  1121   (436)      % holds and is worth keeping in plain words: kHello lived here for one day
  1122   (437)      % as half of a handshake we invented, and it was wrong because the sender's
  1123   (438)      % address arrives WITH the message - there was nothing to hand shake about.
  1124   (439)      % /who is different: the membership list is real information that only the
  1125   (440)      % room has, so there is something to ask for.
  1126   (441)  
  1127   (442)      % /topic and /list. Both are one kind in both directions, same as kWho.
  1128   (443)      % These MUST match CHATSV.PLNC, DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json and
  1129   (444)      % ChatMessageKind.cs - all four move in the same commit.
  1130   (445)      % 12 is Map, reserved for /map - see the note in CHATSV.PLNC.
  1131   (446)      % /map - the machines and their trunks. ONE KIND BOTH DIRECTIONS like kWho:
  1132   (447)      % sent with an empty text, answered with readable text. A ROOM kind, not an
  1133   (448)      % admin one - seeing how the network is wired is not the same right as
  1134   (449)      % rewiring it, which is what keeps it off the admin port.
  1135   (450)  
  1136   (451)  
  1137   (452)      % The answer to /who when this client has NO SEAT - every room and the
  1138   (453)      % people in each. RECEIVE ONLY: the client never sends 15, it sends kWho and
  1139   (454)      % the SERVER picks the answer, because the server is the one that knows
  1140   (455)      % whether we have a seat. See the note beside it in CHATSV.PLNC.
  1141   (456)  
  1142   (457)      % Replayed history, one per remembered message, sent to us alone when we
  1143   (458)      % join. RECEIVE ONLY - the client never sends it.
  1144   (459)  
  1145   (460)      % DIRECT MESSAGES - one person to one person. kDirect goes out, and the
  1146   (461)      % other three come back: the message itself to the person it is FOR, a
  1147   (462)      % receipt to the sender naming who it actually reached, and a refusal with
  1148   (463)      % the reason when it reached nobody.
  1149   (464)  
  1150   (465)      INTEGER : ownTerminal := 1        % FILE number of your own terminal - MON1 only
  1151   (466)  
  1152   (467)      % THE LDN OF THIS TERMINAL, WHICH IS NOT 1 AND NEVER WAS.
  1153   (468)      %
  1154   (469)      % This is the answer to the whole no-wait puzzle, and it was in the Monitor
  1155   (470)      % Calls manual the entire time. Beside the LDN parameter of the calls that
  1156   (471)      % take one, ND-860228-2 says in as many words:
  1157   (472)      %
  1158   (473)      %   "Logical device number. See appendix B. You cannot use 1 for your own
  1159   (474)      %    terminal. Use ExecutionInfo to get its logical device number instead.
  1160   (475)      %    File numbers are illegal."
  1161   (476)      %
  1162   (477)      % And the return status this program measured from TerminalNoWait was -1,
  1163   (478)      % which the same manual defines as "error in parameters". So the call was
  1164   (479)      % never refused, never unavailable, and never about the io flag: it was
  1165   (480)      % handed a FILE number where an LDN belongs, and said so.
  1166   (481)      %
  1167   (482)      % 1 IS STILL RIGHT FOR MON1. InByte takes a file number and 1 means own
  1168   (483)      % terminal there - that call has worked all along. Two different numbering
  1169   (484)      % schemes, one variable doing both jobs, and the manual's own warning that
  1170   (485)      % "file numbers are illegal" reads as a footnote until you know that is
  1171   (486)      % exactly what you passed.
  1172   (487)      %
  1173   (488)      % Filled by findMyLdn at start-up. Left at 1 if that fails, which restores
  1174   (489)      % exactly the old behaviour rather than inventing a worse one.
  1175   (490)      INTEGER : myLdn := 1
  1176   (491)      % BACK TO 0, AND 1 IS WORSE. BOTH VALUES MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-21.
  1177   (492)      %
  1178   (493)      % With 0: TerminalNoWait SUCCEEDS - no error - and MON1 still blocks, so a
  1179   (494)      % listener sees nothing until they press return, at which point the queued
  1180   (495)      % lines all arrive at once:
  1181   (496)      %
  1182   (497)      %     ANNA:
  1183   (498)      %     ANNA: NOWAIT-PROOF-LINE
  1184   (499)      %
  1185   (500)      % With 1: NOTHING IS SHOWN AT ALL, not even on return. The client still
  1186   (501)      % joins - X-C LIST-NAMES showed its seat spent - and the server still sends
  1187   (502)      % to it: its log recorded "SV bcast sent to  2" for the very lines that
  1188   (503)      % never appeared. So the messages arrive at a client that displays none of
  1189   (504)      % them, which is strictly worse than the press-return behaviour.
  1190   (505)      %
  1191   (506)      % AND NOW IT IS UNDERSTOOD, FROM THE MANUAL RATHER THAN FROM ANOTHER GUESS.
  1192   (507)      % ND-860228-2, NoWaitSwitch, parameter 2:
  1193   (508)      %
  1194   (509)      %   "Input or output flag. Use 0 for input and 1 for output."
  1195   (510)      %
  1196   (511)      % So 1 put the OUTPUT side into no-wait: OutByte stopped waiting for the
  1197   (512)      % terminal to be ready and simply dropped what it could not write. "Nothing
  1198   (513)      % is shown at all" was not a mystery, it was the documented behaviour of the
  1199   (514)      % value we chose. 0 is not merely "the safe value" - it is the RIGHT value,
  1200   (515)      % and there is nothing left to experiment with here.
  1201   (516)      %
  1202   (517)      % Two measurements, both correct, and the conclusion drawn from them was
  1203   (518)      % still wrong, because the manual page that names the parameter was never
  1204   (519)      % opened. Grep the Reference-Manuals BEFORE the third build.
  1205   (520)      %
  1206   (521)      % THE NOTE THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS NO LONGER TRUE, and it is corrected
  1207   (522)      % rather than deleted because it would send the next reader down a dead end.
  1208   (523)      % It said: "terminalWait, the restore on exit, calls NoWaitSwitch - the name
  1209   (524)      % that is REFUSED on this machine - so the terminal is never actually put
  1210   (525)      % back. That wants fixing before any more experiments with this flag."
  1211   (526)      %
  1212   (527)      % IT WAS FIXED. terminalWait now calls TerminalNoWait FIRST and only falls
  1213   (528)      % back to NoWaitSwitch if that raises, which is the same order terminalNoWait
  1214   (529)      % uses. The restore does happen. Checked 2026-08-22 by reading the routine,
  1215   (530)      % not by assuming the note was current.
  1216   (531)      %
  1217   (532)      % Worth saying because this codebase has been caught by a stale comment
  1218   (533)      % before: one claiming MONITOR_CALL by name could not work here outlived the
  1219   (534)      % installation of the package that makes it work, and cost a day of designing
  1220   (535)      % around a wall that was not there. A comment is a claim with a date on it.
  1221   (536)      INTEGER : inputFlag   := 0
  1222   (537)      INTEGER : noWaitOn    := 1        % any non-zero switches no-wait ON
  1223   (538)      INTEGER : noWaitOff   := 0        % and zero switches it back off again
  1224   (539)      INTEGER : parityMask  := 127      % strip the even-parity bit, see above
  1225   (540)      INTEGER : carriageRet := 13
  1226   (541)      INTEGER : emptyInput  := 3        % InByte in no-wait: nothing was typed
  1227   (542)  
  1228   (543)      % The idle sleep, so the poll loop is a POLL and not a SPIN. Unit type 1 is
  1229   (544)      % basic time units of 1/50 second, so 10 is a fifth of a second.
  1230   (545)      %
  1231   (546)      % THIS SLEEP IS THE LAST HOP OF THE DELAY A READER ACTUALLY SEES, and the
  1232   (547)      % only way to judge it is ADDED UP rather than on its own:
  1233   (548)      %
  1234   (549)      %   same machine   server sleep + this one
  1235   (550)      %   over a trunk   server sleep TWICE, once per machine, + this one
  1236   (551)      %
  1237   (552)      % The budget is 500 ms to the receiver's screen. At the old numbers - a
  1238   (553)      % ONE-SECOND server sleep and a fifth here - it was 1.2 s in a room and
  1239   (554)      % 2.2 s across a trunk, so it was not slightly over, it was more than
  1240   (555)      % double. The server sleep is now 100 ms, which brings this to 300 ms in a
  1241   (556)      % room and 400 ms across a trunk. Both fit, with room for the work itself.
  1242   (557)      %
  1243   (558)      % AND IT STAYS AT A FIFTH ON PURPOSE. Cutting this to 100 ms as well would
  1244   (559)      % buy 100 ms that the budget does not need, and it is the WRONG PLACE to
  1245   (560)      % spend CPU: there is one server per machine and up to SIXTEEN clients, so
  1246   (561)      % every unit of polling here is multiplied by the number of people in the
  1247   (562)      % room. Ronny's call, 2026-08-28, and the arithmetic agrees with it.
  1248   (563)      %
  1249   (564)      % CHATSV carries the hard-won version of this note: a bare loop with no
  1250   (565)      % sleep is not an option. When XMSG died under the server, its receive
  1251   (566)      % stopped blocking and the loop burned the machine until somebody pressed
  1252   (567)      % ESC. The same trap is waiting here the moment the terminal stops blocking.
  1253   (568)      % ---- WHEN A REPLAYED LINE WAS SAID -----------------------------------
  1254   (569)      %
  1255   (570)      % A history line carries its own time on the wire now, because the one
  1256   (571)      % this program would stamp it with is the time it was REPLAYED - so a
  1257   (572)      % whole backlog used to share one moment and could not say what happened
  1258   (573)      % when.
  1259   (574)      %
  1260   (575)      % msgIsHist is set ONCE where the message arrives and read by addSaid,
  1261   (576)      % rather than threaded through every renderer: both renderers show a
  1262   (577)      % history line, and a parameter would have had to be added to each and
  1263   (578)      % kept in step for ever.
  1264   (579)      %
  1265   (580)      % timeKnown is FALSE for a line out of a block written before there was
  1266   (581)      % anywhere to put a time. The column is then left BLANK - blankLine has
  1267   (582)      % already cleared the row, so not writing it is all it takes - because a
  1268   (583)      % wrong time is worse than no time, which is the whole point of this.
  1269   (584)      BOOLEAN : msgIsHist := FALSE
  1270   (585)      BOOLEAN : timeKnown := TRUE
  1271   (586)      INTEGER : histHour := 0
  1272   (587)      INTEGER : histMin  := 0
  1273   (588)  
  1274   (589)      % THE IDLE SLEEP, AND WHY IT BACKS OFF INSTEAD OF BEING ONE LENGTH.
  1275   (590)      %
  1276   (591)      % sleepKind 1 is BASIC TIME UNITS, a fiftieth of a second each, so ten
  1277   (592)      % units is 200 ms. That is what this used to sleep on every idle pass, and
  1278   (593)      % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-29 it is a large part of what a line costs: the
  1279   (594)      % loop is asleep when the key arrives and when the server's answer arrives,
  1280   (595)      % so BOTH can sit unlooked-at for up to a fifth of a second. A local command
  1281   (596)      % that does no messaging at all was timed at 112 ms once and 481 ms another
  1282   (597)      % time, and one whole idle sleep is the difference.
  1283   (598)      %
  1284   (599)      % So: sleep SHORT while something has just been happening, and only settle
  1285   (600)      % to the long sleep once the client has been quiet for a while. Right after
  1286   (601)      % a line is sent - exactly when the reply is on its way - the wait is 20 ms
  1287   (602)      % rather than 200. A client nobody is using still costs the same as before,
  1288   (603)      % which is the whole point of having a long sleep at all.
  1289   (604)      INTEGER : sleepUnits := 10
  1290   (605)      INTEGER : sleepKind  := 1
  1291   (606)      INTEGER : sleepWhy   := 0
  1292   (607)  
  1293   (608)      % 1 unit = 20 ms. NOT ZERO - the manual is explicit that no parameter of
  1294   (609)      % this call may be zero, and a zero here would turn the poll back into the
  1295   (610)      % spin the sleep exists to prevent.
  1296   (611)      INTEGER : sleepShort := 1
  1297   (612)      % 10 units = 200 ms, what it always slept.
  1298   (613)      INTEGER : sleepLong  := 10
  1299   (614)      % How many empty passes in a row before dropping to the long sleep.
  1300   (615)      % 25 short sleeps is about half a second of quiet.
  1301   (616)      INTEGER : idleRuns    := 0
  1302   (617)      INTEGER : idleRunsMax := 25
  1303   (618)  
  1304   (619)      % How many messages the last drain pass printed. Set by drainPort, read by
  1305   (620)      % the main loop to decide whether the pass found anything at all.
  1306   (621)      INTEGER : lastDrainCount := 0
  1307   (622)  
  1308   (623)      % The ErrCode from the last refused no-wait switch. Module level because a
  1309   (624)      % PLANC local cannot be read after its ON block has run, and the number is
  1310   (625)      % the whole point of the attempt - see terminalNoWait.
  1311   (626)      INTEGER : firstErr := 0
  1312   (627)      % How many messages one drain pass will take before going back to the
  1313   (628)      % keyboard. See drainPort: the unbounded version hung on the first run.
  1314   (629)      INTEGER : maxDrain    := 32
  1315   (630)  
  1316   (631)      INTEGER ARRAY : stack(0:1000)
  1317   (632)  
  1318   (633)      BYTES : letterBuf(0:63)           % the XSLET header XMPBLET builds
  1319   (634)      BYTES : outBuf(0:255)             % our own message, sent after the header
  1320   (635)      % SIZE OF inBuf, named because the read below must clamp against it. XMSG
  1321   (636)      % reports the sender's message size; nothing stops that being larger than
  1322   (637)      % this array, and PLANC checks no bound.
  1323   (638)      INTEGER : inBufSize := 256
  1324   (639)      BYTES : inBuf(0:255)              % what arrives, inBufSize bytes
  1325   (640)      % THE LINE BEING TYPED, and its size is a named constant because the loop
  1326   (641)      % below has to test against it. PLANC does not check array bounds: without
  1327   (642)      % the test, an 81st character is written past the end of this array and into
  1328   (643)      % whatever the compiler laid out next - which here is roomName, typedLen,
  1329   (644)      % serverMagic and joined. A user holding a key down would quietly corrupt
  1330   (645)      % the program's own state.
  1331   (646)      % THE LONGEST ROOM OR NICK NAME a command may carry. This was USED by
  1332   (647)      % handleCommand and never DECLARED - the compiler answered ILLEGAL SYNTAX
  1333   (648)      % "MAXNAMELEN" twice, the loader pass built CHAT:PROG regardless, and every
  1334   (649)      % /join and /nick then answered "too long" for any argument at all. A build
  1335   (650)      % with errors still produces a program; only the LISTING says otherwise.
  1336   (651)      INTEGER : maxNameLen := 16
  1337   (652)  
  1338   (653)      % Must match maxTopicLen in CHATSV.PLNC. The server truncates at this
  1339   (654)      % length; the client refuses at it, so our own user is told rather than
  1340   (655)      % quietly clipped.
  1341   (656)      INTEGER : maxTopicLen := 64
  1342   (657)      INTEGER : maxTyped := 80
  1343   (658)      BYTES : typed(0:79)               % 0:maxTyped-1
  1344   (659)  
  1345   (660)      % One character wide, so a BYTE can be printed as a CHARACTER: 'ALn' formats
  1346   (661)      % a STRING, and handing it a byte prints the byte's NUMBER instead. See
  1347   (662)      % showArrived, where that turned RONNY into 8279787889 on screen.
  1348   (663)      BYTES : oneChar(0:0)
  1349   (664)  
  1350   (665)      % A STRING LITERAL CANNOT BE STORED INTO A BYTES ELEMENT and the compiler
  1351   (666)      % blames the ARRAY when you try, so every literal character written into a
  1352   (667)      % buffer lives in a one-element BYTES and is copied byte to byte.
  1353   (668)      BYTES : spaceByte := ' '
  1354   (669)      BYTES : slashByte := '/'
  1355   (670)  
  1356   (671)      % TWENTY-ONE BYTES OF STORAGE, NOT A LITERAL - and this was a live defect,
  1357   (672)      % not tidying. It was BYTES : roomName := 'CHAT-LOBBY', exactly ten bytes,
  1358   (673)      % while /join accepts an argument up to maxNameLen = 16 and copies it in
  1359   (674)      % here. Eleven characters onwards wrote past the end, into whatever the
  1360   (675)      % loader laid out next. The comment on "typed" above already warns that
  1361   (676)      % roomName is what an overlong typed line runs into; roomName had the same
  1362   (677)      % fault pointing at whatever follows IT.
  1363   (678)      %
  1364   (679)      % Twenty-one is the prefix (5) plus the room's own limit (16).
  1365   (680)      BYTES : roomName(0:20)
  1366   (681)      INTEGER : lenRoomName := 10
  1367   (682)  
  1368   (683)      % THE ROOM'S TOPIC, KEPT so the header can show it.
  1369   (684)      %
  1370   (685)      % The client used to PRINT an arriving topic and drop it - there was no
  1371   (686)      % topic buffer anywhere in this file - which is why setting one changed
  1372   (687)      % nothing in the header, reported by Ronny 2026-08-31. A thing the screen
  1373   (688)      % has to show has to be REMEMBERED; a message that is only printed is gone
  1374   (689)      % the moment the next line arrives.
  1375   (690)      %
  1376   (691)      % 64 to match maxTopicLen, which is what the server truncates at. Cleared
  1377   (692)      % by setRoom, because a topic belongs to ONE room and carrying the old
  1378   (693)      % room's topic into a new one would state something false.
  1379   (694)      BYTES : curTopic(0:63)
  1380   (695)      INTEGER : lenTopic := 0
  1381   (696)  
  1382   (697)      % THE PREFIX EVERY ROOM'S REGISTERED NAME CARRIES, and forgetting it is
  1383   (698)      % why /join never worked.
  1384   (699)      %
  1385   (700)      % ChatRooms.NamePrefix in the C# library says "CHAT-", and ChatRooms.NameFor
  1386   (701)      % builds "CHAT-" + the room's short name in upper case. So the LOBBY room is
  1387   (702)      % registered with XROUT as CHAT-LOBBY. /join used to copy the typed argument
  1388   (703)      % in raw, so "/join GENERAL" asked XROUT for a name called GENERAL - which no
  1389   (704)      % room ever registers. XROUT answered "no such name" and the client sat in
  1390   (705)      % the room it was already in, which is exactly the reported symptom.
  1391   (706)      %
  1392   (707)      % The startup default below is built the same way, from the prefix and the
  1393   (708)      % short name, so there is one rule and not two.
  1394   (709)      % THE SERVICE NAME. A CONSTANT - it is not built from the room any more.
  1395   (710)      %
  1396   (711)      % This used to be roomPrefix := 'CHAT-', and setRoom pasted the typed room
  1397   (712)      % onto it to make a PORT NAME, so joining a room meant addressing a
  1398   (713)      % different port. One port now; the room rides in the message. See
  1399   (714)      % the_room_model in DOC/protocols/chat-wire.json.
  1400   (715)      BYTES : servName := '*CHAT'
  1401   (716)      INTEGER : lenServName := 5
  1402   (717)  
  1403   (718)      % The room joined at start-up when nobody says otherwise. SHORT name: the
  1404   (719)      % prefix is added by setRoom, the same as for a typed /join.
  1405   (720)      BYTES : homeRoom := 'LOBBY'
  1406   (721)      INTEGER : lenHomeRoom := 5
  1407   (722)      BYTES : hereSystem := ''          % length 0 means the LOCAL system
  1408   (723)      INTEGER : lenHereSystem := 0
  1409   (724)  
  1410   (725)      % SIXTEEN BYTES OF STORAGE, NOT A LITERAL, and the change is not cosmetic.
  1411   (726)      %
  1412   (727)      % This was BYTES : myName := 'RONNY', which reserves five bytes and exactly
  1413   (728)      % five. Asking for a longer name would have written past the end of it into
  1414   (729)      % whatever the loader happened to put next, with nothing to stop it - PLANC
  1415   (730)      % checks no array bound. The same defect, in the other direction, is what
  1416   (731)      % the guard in CHATSV.handleMessage exists for.
  1417   (732)      %
  1418   (733)      % Sixteen is the ROOM's number: ChatRoom.MaxNicknameLength says sixteen and
  1419   (734)      % CHATSV drops anything longer, so a name this side cannot send is one the
  1420   (735)      % other side would refuse anyway.
  1421   (736)      BYTES : myName(0:15)
  1422   (737)      INTEGER : lenMyName := 5
  1423   (738)  
  1424   (739)      % The name used when nobody types one AND nothing was remembered. Kept as a
  1425   (740)      % separate literal because myName is now a buffer and has to be filled at
  1426   (741)      % start-up rather than declared full - see setupName.
  1427   (742)      % THE LAST RESORT, and it should not be a person's name. A hardcoded RONNY
  1428   (743)      % turning up on somebody else's terminal is the same kind of wrong as the
  1429   (744)      % OLAV that arrived from a config file written during testing. It is only
  1430   (745)      % reached when SINTRAN itself will not say who is logged in.
  1431   (746)      BYTES : defaultName := 'USER'
  1432   (747)      INTEGER : lenDefault := 4
  1433   (748)  
  1434   (749)      % THE BUILD STAMP - bumped by hand every time this file is rebuilt.
  1435   (750)      %
  1436   (751)      % Added 2026-08-31 chasing a bug where two clients kept showing a stale
  1437   (752)      % welcome line: the fix turned out not to be a version mismatch, but there
  1438   (753)      % was no CHEAP way to see that without reading FILE-STATISTICS on the
  1439   (754)      % machine by hand. Now the client SAYS what it is - riding on the join, so
  1440   (755)      % LIST-MEMBER shows it for every seat without a separate query.
  1441   (756)      %
  1442   (757)      % EIGHT CHARACTERS, so it never forces a rework of anything that copies it
  1443   (758)      % - see maxVerLen on the server, which this must never exceed.
  1444   (759)      BYTES : clientVersion := 'B31-0731'
  1445   (760)      INTEGER : lenClientVer := 8
  1446   (761)  
  1447   (762)      % Splits the room name from the build version inside kJoin's text field.
  1448   (763)      % Chosen because a room name is typed by a person and a pipe is not a
  1449   (764)      % character anybody types into one.
  1450   (765)      BYTES : verSepByte := '|'
  1451   (766)  
  1452   (767)      % Where GetUserName writes. SINTRAN pads the answer, so the name ends at the
  1453   (768)      % first space and the buffer is deliberately wider than maxNameLen.
  1454   (769)      BYTES : sintranUser(0:31)
  1455   (770)  
  1456   (771)      % How much of sintranUser is the name. ZERO means SINTRAN would not say, and
  1457   (772)      % that is the state every check below must cope with - see readCnfg, which
  1458   (773)      % refuses to trust a remembered nickname when it cannot tell whose it is.
  1459   (774)      INTEGER : lenSintranUser
  1460   (775)  
  1461   (776)      % THE OTHER FOUR PARAMETERS OF GetUserName, and the reason they exist here.
  1462   (777)      %
  1463   (778)      % The call was first written with ONE argument and answered "refused" every
  1464   (779)      % time. The OVERVIEW table lists only the first parameter -
  1465   (780)      %
  1466   (781)      %     | **GetUserName** (GUSNA 214B) | | | | | |
  1467   (782)      %     | 1. User name                 | STR | O | | . | . |
  1468   (783)      %
  1469   (784)      % - and the DETAIL PAGE gives the real shape:
  1470   (785)      %
  1471   (786)      %     Monitor_Call('GetUserName', UserName(1:16), DirIndex, UserIndex,
  1472   (787)      %                  RemoteFlag, RemoteSystem(1:64))
  1473   (788)      %
  1474   (789)      % FIVE arguments. The same mistake as StartRTProgram: reading the summary
  1475   (790)      % row instead of opening the entry. A monitor call given the wrong number of
  1476   (791)      % arguments is REFUSED, exactly as if it were not permitted, so the two look
  1477   (792)      % identical from the outside and one gets mistaken for the other.
  1478   (793)      INTEGER : dirIndex
  1479   (794)      INTEGER : userIndex
  1480   (795)      INTEGER : remoteFlag
  1481   (796)      BYTES : remoteSystem(0:63)
  1482   (797)  
  1483   (798)      % ----- CHAT:CNFG, the remembered nickname -------------------------------
  1484   (799)      % IN THE USER'S OWN FILE AREA, which is the whole point: OLAV and ANNA log
  1485   (800)      % in as themselves and each gets their own. Nothing here names a user -
  1486   (801)      % SINTRAN resolves an unqualified name against whoever is logged in, so the
  1487   (802)      % separation is free and cannot be got wrong.
  1488   (803)      %
  1489   (804)      % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: user OLAV, "@CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1"
  1490   (805)      % succeeded and "@CHAT" ran from that user's terminal, so both halves - a
  1491   (806)      % writable file of one's own, and permission to run the client - are real
  1492   (807)      % rather than assumed.
  1493   (808)      %
  1494   (809)      % THE FILE IS ONE LINE: the nickname, ended by a carriage return.
  1495   (810)      % Deliberately not a key=value format. There is one setting, a reader that
  1496   (811)      % has to parse nothing cannot misparse anything, and a person can fix it
  1497   (812)      % with any editor on the machine.
  1498   (813)      BYTES : cnfgName   := 'CHAT'
  1499   (814)      BYTES : cnfgType   := 'CNFG'
  1500   (815)  
  1501   (816)      % MAKING THE FILE IS A COMMAND, NOT AN OPEN, and that cost a build cycle.
  1502   (817)      %
  1503   (818)      % The first version passed the name QUOTED to MON50 - '"CHAT"' - on the
  1504   (819)      % rule that quoting is what asks SINTRAN to create. MEASURED 2026-08-18 as
  1505   (820)      % user ANNA, who had no config file: the open did not fail loudly, but
  1506   (821)      % every MON2 after it raised, and afterwards LIST-FILES showed ANNA still
  1507   (822)      % owned no files at all. Nothing was created.
  1508   (823)      %
  1509   (824)      % The reason is in the manual rather than in the quoting: creating a file
  1510   (825)      % is its OWN monitor call, CreateFile / CRALF 221B, and 221 is not among
  1511   (826)      % the MONn routines the PLANC runtime provides. MON50 opens; it has no size
  1512   (827)      % parameter and cannot make a file that is not there.
  1513   (828)      %
  1514   (829)      % So the file is made the way a person would make it, by handing SINTRAN
  1515   (830)      % the command - MON70, CallCommand. One page, which is far more than one
  1516   (831)      % line needs.
  1517   (832)      %
  1518   (833)      % NO QUOTES, AND THE QUOTES ARE WHY THIS NEVER WORKED.
  1519   (834)      %
  1520   (835)      % This read 'CREATE-FILE "CHAT:CNFG",1' from the day it was written, and
  1521   (836)      % measured on D100 2026-08-19 that command answers
  1522   (837)      %
  1523   (838)      %   @CREATE-FILE "CHAT:CNFG",1
  1524   (839)      %   ILLEGAL CHARACTER IN PARAMETER
  1525   (840)      %
  1526   (841)      % while the unquoted form succeeds silently. So the file was NEVER created,
  1527   (842)      % every save failed, and CHAT:CNFG did not exist for any user.
  1528   (843)      %
  1529   (844)      % THE TRAP IS THAT QUOTES MEAN OPPOSITE THINGS ON THE TWO ROUTES:
  1530   (845)      %
  1531   (846)      %   MON50(file, ...)   quoting the name means CREATE IT IF ABSENT
  1532   (847)      %   CREATE-FILE <name> quoting the name is an ILLEGAL CHARACTER
  1533   (848)      %
  1534   (849)      % The quotes here were carried over from the MON50 attempt described above,
  1535   (850)      % where they were correct, into the command that replaced it, where they are
  1536   (851)      % fatal. A local filespec takes no quotes; the quoting rule is for REMOTE
  1537   (852)      % ones, where the name goes to another machine's command processor.
  1538   (853)      %
  1539   (854)      % AND THE COMMAND MUST END WITH AN APOSTROPHE, which is the second half of
  1540   (855)      % why this never worked. Removing the quotes alone was not enough - measured
  1541   (856)      % on D100 2026-08-19, CHAT:CNFG still did not appear.
  1542   (857)      %
  1543   (858)      % The Monitor Calls manual's own worked example gives the rule, in its
  1544   (859)      % ASSEMBLY-500 section under CallCommand:
  1545   (860)      %
  1546   (861)      %   Command : STRINGDATA 'CLOSE-FILE 102'''
  1547   (862)      %
  1548   (863)      % - that trailing triple apostrophe is the string's closing quote plus ONE
  1549   (864)      % apostrophe character, so the command handed to SINTRAN is
  1550   (865)      % "CLOSE-FILE 102'". The apostrophe TERMINATES the command; without it the
  1551   (866)      % command processor keeps reading past the end of the buffer into whatever
  1552   (867)      % follows, and the command never runs as written.
  1553   (868)      %
  1554   (869)      % In PLANC an apostrophe inside a literal is written doubled, so the line
  1555   (870)      % below is CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1 followed by one apostrophe.
  1556   (871)      BYTES : cnfgMake := 'CREATE-FILE CHAT:CNFG,1'''
  1557   (872)  
  1558   (873)      % EIGHTY BYTES, BECAUSE THAT IS THE BUFFER MON70 IS DEFINED TO TAKE - and
  1559   (874)      % this, not the quoting and not the terminator, is why the file was never
  1560   (875)      % created. Both earlier fixes were real and neither changed the outcome.
  1561   (876)      %
  1562   (877)      % The Monitor Calls manual gives the PLANC form of CallCommand as
  1563   (878)      %
  1564   (879)      %   BYTES : Command(0:79)
  1565   (880)      %
  1566   (881)      % an EIGHTY-BYTE ARRAY. A PLANC BYTES argument carries its own length, so
  1567   (882)      % handing MON70 a 25-byte literal hands it a 25-byte buffer where it expects
  1568   (883)      % eighty - the same defect that made XROUT look up a room name with eleven
  1569   (884)      % bytes of leftovers on the end, in the other direction.
  1570   (885)      %
  1571   (886)      % So the command is COPIED into a full-size buffer, apostrophe-terminated,
  1572   (887)      % and the rest left as spaces. See makeCnfg.
  1573   (888)      INTEGER : lenCnfgMake := 24
  1574   (889)      BYTES : cmdBuf(0:79)
  1575   (890)      INTEGER : cmdBufSize := 80
  1576   (891)      BYTES : spaceChar := ' '
  1577   (892)  
  1578   (893)      % ----- CreateFile, the direct route -------------------------------------
  1579   (894)      % The FULL filespec, name and type together - the manual's example creates
  1580   (895)      % 'NAME-STORAGE:DATA' in one string - in the 64-byte buffer MON221 expects.
  1581   (896)      % One line of CHAT:CNFG, read whole before any of it is believed. Wider than
  1582   (897)      % maxNameLen because it holds OWNER:NICK, not just a nickname.
  1583   (898)      BYTES : cnfgLine(0:63)
  1584   (899)  
  1585   (900)      BYTES : cnfgFull := 'CHAT:CNFG'
  1586   (901)      INTEGER : lenCnfgFull := 9
  1587   (902)      BYTES : nameBuf(0:63)
  1588   (903)      INTEGER : sizeNameBuf := 64
  1589   (904)      INTEGER4 : startAddr := 0         % 0 = let SINTRAN place it
  1590   (905)      INTEGER4 : noOfPages := 0         % 0 = indexed, grows when written
  1591   (906)  
  1592   (907)      % Access codes for MON50, from the Monitor Calls manual's own table:
  1593   (908)      %   0  sequential write
  1594   (909)      %   1  sequential read
  1595   (910)      INTEGER : openRead  := 1
  1596   (911)      INTEGER : openWrite := 0
  1597   (912)  
  1598   (913)      % A "nameRemembered" BOOLEAN stood here, to word the prompt differently for
  1599   (914)      % a remembered name than for the built-in one. It was set and never read.
  1600   (915)      % The prompt shows the actual name either way, which answers the same
  1601   (916)      % question better than a different wording would have.
  1602   (917)  
  1603   (918)      INTEGER : myPort
  1604   (919)      INTEGER : returnStatus
  1605   (920)      INTEGER : offSet, uLength, wLength
  1606   (921)      INTEGER : msgType, remotePortHash, nBytes, readLength, readAmount
  1607   (922)      INTEGER : typedLen := 0
  1608   (923)      INTEGER : ch
  1609   (924)      % MON1's out-value is a BYTE, so it lands here first and is widened into ch
  1610   (925)      % before the parity bit is masked off.
  1611   (926)      BYTE : rawCh
  1612   (927)      INTEGER4 : senderMagic
  1613   (928)      % ZERO until the welcome arrives and reveals the real address. It is set
  1614   (929)      % explicitly rather than left to whatever the loader put there, because the
  1615   (930)      % joined test below is what keeps it from ever being used - and a guard is
  1616   (931)      % worth more when the thing it guards is a known value.
  1617   (932)      INTEGER4 : serverMagic := 0
  1618   (933)      % An INTEGER4 myMagic lived here to hold our own port's magic number, read
  1619   (934)      % with XMPFP2M purely so a diagnostic could print it. Both are gone - see
  1620   (935)      % the note in chatClient for what they settled (the reply address was
  1621   (936)      % correct all along) and why that ended the need for them.
  1622   (937)      BOOLEAN : joined := FALSE
  1623   (938)  
  1624   (939)      % WHICH MACHINE THIS CLIENT IS ON, as the server said in the welcome.
  1625   (940)      %
  1626   (941)      % A CLIENT CANNOT WORK THIS OUT FOR ITSELF. It has no system number of its
  1627   (942)      % own and nothing in the join tells it - the server knows, because it asked
  1628   (943)      % the kernel, and now says so in the welcome's text where there was never
  1629   (944)      % anything before.
  1630   (945)      %
  1631   (946)      % Empty until the welcome arrives, and empty is a fine answer: the screen
  1632   (947)      % shows the nickname alone rather than a machine name that would be a
  1633   (948)      % guess. It is 'D100' on a machine with no name and 'FJELL' on one that
  1634   (949)      % has been given one, and this client does not care which - it prints what
  1635   (950)      % it was told.
  1636   (951)      BYTES   : myMachine(0:15)
  1637   (952)      INTEGER : lenMyMach := 0
  1638   (953)  
  1639   (954)      % Has the user CHOSEN a name with /nick this session?
  1640   (955)      %
  1641   (956)      % This exists to tell two refusals apart, because they arrive on the SAME
  1642   (957)      % kReject and mean completely different things to the person reading them:
  1643   (958)      %
  1644   (959)      %   FALSE - we joined under the name SINTRAN gave us, and the room says it is
  1645   (960)      %           taken. That means the SAME SINTRAN USER is already in the room
  1646   (961)      %           from ANOTHER TERMINAL. Say exactly that.
  1647   (962)      %   TRUE  - the user typed /nick ANNA and ANNA is taken. Telling them they are
  1648   (963)      %           "already in from another terminal" would be FALSE and confusing -
  1649   (964)      %           somebody else has that name.
  1650   (965)      %
  1651   (966)      % Set once, never cleared: after any /nick, every later refusal is about a
  1652   (967)      % name the user picked.
  1653   (968)      BOOLEAN : userChoseNick := FALSE
  1654   (969)  
  1655   (970)      % Said once. A refused sleep every fifth of a second is a flood that hides
  1656   (971)      % itself, so idleSleep speaks the first time and stays quiet after.
  1657   (972)      BOOLEAN : sleepBroken := FALSE
  1658   (973)      % TRUE once RETURN has been seen, so a line half typed when the port
  1659   (974)      % needs draining survives the trip round the outer loop.
  1660   (975)      BOOLEAN : lineReady := FALSE
  1661   (976)      BOOLEAN : running := TRUE
  1662   (977)  
  1663   (978)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1664   (979)      % Build one of our messages into outBuf. Returns its total length.
  1665   (980)      %
  1666   (981)      % Same layout as ChatMessage.cs, which is the only specification it has:
  1667   (982)      % kind, name length, name, two length bytes, text.
  1668   (983)      %
  1669   (984)      % THE EXACT BYTES ARE PINNED in
  1670   (985)      % SRC/Xmsg.Chat.Tests/ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests.cs - a Said, a Renamed, a
  1671   (986)      % Join and a 300-character line that proves the text length is big-endian.
  1672   (987)      % There is no shared code between that and this, only those goldens, so if
  1673   (988)      % the layout ever changes both sides change in the SAME commit or they
  1674   (989)      % disagree in silence.
  1675   (990)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1676   (991)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1677   (992)      % Build the JOIN. Same layout as any other message - kind, nickname, text -
  1678   (993)      % but the TEXT carries the ROOM this time.
  1679   (994)      %
  1680   (995)      % THAT IS WHY THIS COSTS NO WIRE CHANGE. A Join always had an empty text
  1681   (996)      % field; only what goes in it is new. message_prefix does not move and the
  1682   (997)      % golden bytes keep their shape - just their content for Join.
  1683   (998)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1684   (999)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : buildJoin
  1685  (1000)          INTEGER : i, at
  1686  (1001)          kJoin =: outBuf(0)
  1687  (1002)          lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
  1688  (1003)          2 =: at
  1689  (1004)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  1690  (1005)              myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1691  (1006)              at + 1 =: at
  1692  (1007)          ENDFOR
  1693  (1008)          % Text length, high byte then low. A room name is far short of 256.
  1694  (1009)          %
  1695  (1010)          % THE VERSION RIDES AFTER THE ROOM, SEPARATED BY '|'. An older server
  1696  (1011)          % that has never heard of this just stores the whole thing as the room
  1697  (1012)          % name - a room called "LOBBY|B31-0731" - which is wrong but not
  1698  (1013)          % dangerous, and is exactly why a build-version check belongs in
  1699  (1014)          % LIST-MEMBER instead of anything that gates a join.
  1700  (1015)          0 =: outBuf(at)
  1701  (1016)          lenRoomName + 1 + lenClientVer =: outBuf(at + 1)
  1702  (1017)          at + 2 =: at
  1703  (1018)          FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
  1704  (1019)              roomName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1705  (1020)              at + 1 =: at
  1706  (1021)          ENDFOR
  1707  (1022)          verSepByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
  1708  (1023)          at + 1 =: at
  1709  (1024)          FOR i IN 1:lenClientVer DO
  1710  (1025)              clientVersion(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1711  (1026)              at + 1 =: at
  1712  (1027)          ENDFOR
  1713  (1028)          at RETURN
  1714  (1029)      ENDROUTINE
  1715  (1030)  
  1716  (1031)      % ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  1717  (1032)      % ONE OF OUR OWN MESSAGES, built by the LIBRARY.
  1718  (1033)      %
  1719  (1034)      % This used to be twenty lines that wrote the kind, the name length, the
  1720  (1035)      % name, a two-byte text length and the text - the same twenty lines the
  1721  (1036)      % SERVER also had, and neither was tested. cmEnc does exactly that and is
  1722  (1037)      % checked by CHATTST on every build, so the encoder that SHIPS is now the
  1723  (1038)      % encoder that is TESTED.
  1724  (1039)      %
  1725  (1040)      % THE THREE ARRAYS ARE THREE DIFFERENT ARRAYS - outBuf, myName and typed -
  1726  (1041)      % which cmEnc requires: passing two subarrays of ONE array to a single
  1727  (1042)      % routine corrupts both and compiles clean.
  1728  (1043)      %
  1729  (1044)      % One behaviour improved for free. The old code wrote the length as a
  1730  (1045)      % zero and one byte, which is right until a line reaches 256 characters;
  1731  (1046)      % cmEnc writes both bytes properly, and CHATTST checks a 300-byte text
  1732  (1047)      % for exactly that reason.
  1733  (1048)      % ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  1734  (1049)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : buildOwn(kind, textLength)
  1735  (1050)          cmEnc(outBuf, kind, myName, lenMyName, typed, textLength, 256) RETURN
  1736  (1051)      ENDROUTINE
  1737  (1052)  
  1738  (1053)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1739  (1054)      % Same message as buildOwn, but the text is taken from PART of the typed
  1740  (1055)      % line rather than all of it.
  1741  (1056)      %
  1742  (1057)      % buildOwn always copies typed(0) onwards, which is right for a Say - the
  1743  (1058)      % whole line IS the text. A command argument is not: "/topic the coffee is
  1744  (1059)      % on" has to send only what follows the verb, and copying from zero would
  1745  (1060)      % put the command itself on the wire.
  1746  (1061)      %
  1747  (1062)      % from is a ZERO-BASED index into typed, matching buildOwn's typed(i - 1).
  1748  (1063)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1749  (1064)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
  1750  (1065)              buildOwnFrom(kind, from, textLength)
  1751  (1066)          INTEGER : i, at
  1752  (1067)          kind =: outBuf(0)
  1753  (1068)          lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
  1754  (1069)          2 =: at
  1755  (1070)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  1756  (1071)              myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1757  (1072)              at + 1 =: at
  1758  (1073)          ENDFOR
  1759  (1074)          0 =: outBuf(at)
  1760  (1075)          textLength =: outBuf(at + 1)
  1761  (1076)          at + 2 =: at
  1762  (1077)          FOR i IN 1:textLength DO
  1763  (1078)              typed(from + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1764  (1079)              at + 1 =: at
  1765  (1080)          ENDFOR
  1766  (1081)          at RETURN
  1767  (1082)      ENDROUTINE
  1768  (1083)  
  1769  (1084)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1770  (1085)      % Build a DIRECT MESSAGE out of "NAME rest of the line".
  1771  (1086)      %
  1772  (1087)      % THE WIRE WANTS "TARGET/message" AND THE USER TYPES "TARGET message", so
  1773  (1088)      % the first space becomes a slash. That is the whole conversion, and doing
  1774  (1089)      % it here rather than asking the user to type the slash is deliberate: the
  1775  (1090)      % slash is a protocol detail, and a person should not have to know one to
  1776  (1091)      % send somebody a line.
  1777  (1092)      %
  1778  (1093)      % A TARGET CONTAINING A SLASH IS REFUSED by the caller, because it would
  1779  (1094)      % split in the wrong place at the far end and deliver to a name nobody has.
  1780  (1095)      %
  1781  (1096)      % from is a ZERO-BASED index into typed, matching buildOwn.
  1782  (1097)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1783  (1098)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
  1784  (1099)              buildTell(from, tgtLen, msgLen)
  1785  (1100)          INTEGER : i, at
  1786  (1101)          kDirect =: outBuf(0)
  1787  (1102)          lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
  1788  (1103)          2 =: at
  1789  (1104)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  1790  (1105)              myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1791  (1106)              at + 1 =: at
  1792  (1107)          ENDFOR
  1793  (1108)  
  1794  (1109)          0 =: outBuf(at)
  1795  (1110)          tgtLen + 1 + msgLen =: outBuf(at + 1)
  1796  (1111)          at + 2 =: at
  1797  (1112)  
  1798  (1113)          FOR i IN 1:tgtLen DO
  1799  (1114)              typed(from + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1800  (1115)              at + 1 =: at
  1801  (1116)          ENDFOR
  1802  (1117)          slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
  1803  (1118)          at + 1 =: at
  1804  (1119)          FOR i IN 1:msgLen DO
  1805  (1120)              typed(from + tgtLen + i) =: outBuf(at)
  1806  (1121)              at + 1 =: at
  1807  (1122)          ENDFOR
  1808  (1123)          at RETURN
  1809  (1124)      ENDROUTINE
  1810  (1125)  
  1811  (1126)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1812  (1127)      % Build a RENAME, which does NOT have the same shape as every other
  1813  (1128)      % message and is the one place buildOwn cannot be used.
  1814  (1129)      %
  1815  (1130)      % THE NEW NAME GOES IN THE NAME FIELD, and the text is empty. This client
  1816  (1131)      % had it the other way round - new name in the TEXT, current name in the
  1817  (1132)      % NAME field - and the rename was thrown away in silence every time.
  1818  (1133)      %
  1819  (1134)      % MEASURED 2026-08-18 on D100: "/nick OLAV" was accepted by the client,
  1820  (1135)      % sent, and changed nothing; the room went on saying RONNY. Nothing was
  1821  (1136)      % printed by either side, because the path it took is a deliberate
  1822  (1137)      % do-nothing:
  1823  (1138)      %
  1824  (1139)      %   the server reads the new name from the NAME field;
  1825  (1140)      %   the client had put the CURRENT name there;
  1826  (1141)      %   so sameName(slot, ...) matched, and the server took its
  1827  (1142)      %   "already your name: not news" RETURN, which prints nothing.
  1828  (1143)      %
  1829  (1144)      % TWO WRONG THINGS AGREEING TO DO NOTHING. Each side was self-consistent
  1830  (1145)      % and the pair was broken, which is why it survived a working build.
  1831  (1146)      %
  1832  (1147)      % THE C# PAIR IS THE ARBITER and it is unambiguous - ChatClient.Rename
  1833  (1148)      % sends "new ChatMessage(ChatMessageKind.Rename, nickname, string.Empty)",
  1834  (1149)      % the NEW nickname in the name field and an EMPTY text, and
  1835  (1150)      % ChatServer.HandleRename reads it back with _room.TryRename(id,
  1836  (1151)      % message.Nickname, ...). The PLANC SERVER already matched that. Only this
  1837  (1152)      % side did not.
  1838  (1153)      %
  1839  (1154)      % The sender is identified by its MAGIC NUMBER, not by the name it sends -
  1840  (1155)      % findByMagic(senderMagic) is what picks the seat - so the name field is
  1841  (1156)      % free to carry the new name and nothing is lost by not sending the old one.
  1842  (1157)      %
  1843  (1158)      % myName is deliberately NOT changed here. Our own name changes when the
  1844  (1159)      % room says so with kRenOk, so a refused rename leaves us as we were.
  1845  (1160)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1846  (1161)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : buildRename(nameFrom, newLength)
  1847  (1162)          INTEGER : i, at
  1848  (1163)          kRename =: outBuf(0)
  1849  (1164)          newLength =: outBuf(1)
  1850  (1165)          2 =: at
  1851  (1166)          FOR i IN 1:newLength DO
  1852  (1167)              typed(nameFrom + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  1853  (1168)              at + 1 =: at
  1854  (1169)          ENDFOR
  1855  (1170)          % An empty text, spelled out as its two length bytes.
  1856  (1171)          0 =: outBuf(at)
  1857  (1172)          0 =: outBuf(at + 1)
  1858  (1173)          at + 2 =: at
  1859  (1174)          at RETURN
  1860  (1175)      ENDROUTINE
  1861  (1176)  
  1862  (1177)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1863  (1178)      % Say what "no access" actually means, because the number alone sends
  1864  (1179)      % people to the wire.
  1865  (1180)      %
  1866  (1181)      % XRUNN and XRNRO are the same fault seen from two sides, and BOTH mean the
  1867  (1182)      % peer has not been told to trust this system. XRUNN reads "unknown name of
  1868  (1183)      % server or system" and it is the SYSTEM half of that phrase - not the
  1869  (1184)      % server half - which is what sent two earlier investigations chasing
  1870  (1185)      % *CHATSV when the room was fine.
  1871  (1186)      %
  1872  (1187)      % Everything else looks healthy while this is wrong: the peer lists the
  1873  (1188)      % system, LIST-SYSTEMS says ON NET, LIST-LINKS says State Run with
  1874  (1189)      % thousands of frames received. Nothing comes back because the rejection
  1875  (1190)      % happens before XROUT is asked, so there is no error to send.
  1876  (1191)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  1877  (1192)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showFriendAdvice
  1878  (1193)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL48', 'the PEER has not defined us as a FRIEND system.$')
  1879  (1194)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'on that machine:  @X-COMM$')
  1880  (1195)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  X-C: DEFINE-FRIEND-SYSTEM   System? <our no>$')
  1881  (1196)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL44', 'check it with LIST-ROUTING-INFO - it prints$')
  1882  (1197)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL40', '  "T: *, but no access to system NNNNN"$')
  1883  (1198)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL45', 'NOTE: DEF-REMOTE CLEARS the friend entry, so$')
  1884  (1199)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL46', 'the name comes first and the friend AFTER it.$')
  1885  (1200)      ENDROUTINE
  1886  (1201)  
  1887  (1202)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER) : showStatus(where, code)
  1888  (1203)          % NOT "offset" - the module already has a global offSet for the letter, and
  1889  (1204)          % PLANC ignores case, so that spelling collided with it and the compiler
  1890  (1205)          % said IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED.
  1891  (1206)          %
  1892  (1207)          % AND IT IS A RANGE, NOT A PLAIN INTEGER, because this is what CASE
  1893  (1208)          % selects on. ND-60.117.5 section 6.3: the CASE expression "may have a
  1894  (1209)          % maximum of 256 different values, and must be either an enumeration
  1895  (1210)          % with a maximum of 256 different values, or integers ranging between 0
  1896  (1211)          % and 255", with the note that an INTEGER RANGE used this way must have
  1897  (1212)          % a lower bound of 0. A plain INTEGER spans -32768..32767, so the
  1898  (1213)          % compiler answered OUT OF RANGE on both CASE statements - a WARNING,
  1899  (1214)          % which is exactly the kind that gets skimmed past.
  1900  (1215)          INTEGER RANGE (0:255) : xmxOffset
  1901  (1216)  
  1902  (1217)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', where)
  1903  (1218)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', ' status ')
  1904  (1219)          OUTPUT(1, 'I6', code)
  1905  (1220)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', ' ')
  1906  (1221)  
  1907  (1222)          IF code = XMOK THEN
  1908  (1223)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL12', 'XMOK - done$')
  1909  (1224)              RETURN
  1910  (1225)          ENDIF
  1911  (1226)  
  1912  (1227)          IF code < 0 THEN
  1913  (1228)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'raw kernel error, not a library one$')
  1914  (1229)              RETURN
  1915  (1230)          ENDIF
  1916  (1231)  
  1917  (1232)          IF code < XMXKXXX THEN
  1918  (1233)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'message type, not an error$')
  1919  (1234)              RETURN
  1920  (1235)          ENDIF
  1921  (1236)  
  1922  (1237)          % XROUT ERRORS SIT ABOVE THE KERNEL ONES, at their own base
  1923  (1238)          % XRXXX = 16960 = 41100B, so this test has to come FIRST - the XMX
  1924  (1239)          % block below would otherwise decode 16972 as XMX offset 76 and say
  1925  (1240)          % nothing useful. Two of these are the same fault wearing two names,
  1926  (1241)          % and it is the one that costs a whole evening every time, so it gets
  1927  (1242)          % told in full rather than named.
  1928  (1243)          IF code >= XRXXX THEN
  1929  (1244)              code - XRXXX =: xmxOffset
  1930  (1245)              CASE xmxOffset
  1931  (1246)                  INCASE 2
  1932  (1247)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XRUNN unknown name of server OR SYSTEM$')
  1933  (1248)                      showFriendAdvice
  1934  (1249)                  INCASE 12
  1935  (1250)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XRNRO no access to remote system$')
  1936  (1251)                      showFriendAdvice
  1937  (1252)                  INCASE 0
  1938  (1253)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', 'XRSOK ok - not an error$')
  1939  (1254)                  INCASE 1
  1940  (1255)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XRISN illegal service number$')
  1941  (1256)                  INCASE 3
  1942  (1257)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL41', 'XRDDF another port already has this name$')
  1943  (1258)                  INCASE 4
  1944  (1259)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XRNSP no space left for names$')
  1945  (1260)                  INCASE 7
  1946  (1261)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XRUNM unknown magic number$')
  1947  (1262)                  INCASE 10
  1948  (1263)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'XRPRV caller was not privileged$')
  1949  (1264)                  INCASE 11
  1950  (1265)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', 'XRISY illegal system number parameter$')
  1951  (1266)                  ELSE
  1952  (1267)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL23', 'XROUT error, XR offset ')
  1953  (1268)                      OUTPUT(1, 'I6', xmxOffset)
  1954  (1269)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  1955  (1270)              ENDCASE
  1956  (1271)              RETURN
  1957  (1272)          ENDIF
  1958  (1273)  
  1959  (1274)          code - XMXKXXX =: xmxOffset
  1960  (1275)          CASE xmxOffset
  1961  (1276)              INCASE 0
  1962  (1277)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XMXENTM not terminated - nothing there$')
  1963  (1278)              INCASE 1
  1964  (1279)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL31', 'XMXENOT no more XT-blocks free$')
  1965  (1280)              INCASE 2
  1966  (1281)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', 'XMXEIRM non-local remote port illegal$')
  1967  (1282)              INCASE 4
  1968  (1283)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'XMXETMM task allowed no more memory$')
  1969  (1284)              INCASE 5
  1970  (1285)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXENIM facility not implemented$')
  1971  (1286)              INCASE 6
  1972  (1287)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL39', 'XMXEIBP illegal message buffer pointer$')
  1973  (1288)              INCASE 7
  1974  (1289)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXEBNY message buffer not yours$')
  1975  (1290)              INCASE 8
  1976  (1291)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL41', 'XMXEIRT illegal function for RT programs$')
  1977  (1292)              INCASE 9
  1978  (1293)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'XMXENOP no more ports available$')
  1979  (1294)              INCASE 10
  1980  (1295)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL42', 'XMXEIDR function not available to drivers$')
  1981  (1296)              INCASE 11
  1982  (1297)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMXENDM no default message$')
  1983  (1298)              INCASE 12
  1984  (1299)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEMCH message is already chained$')
  1985  (1300)              INCASE 13
  1986  (1301)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XMXEBFC message is in a queue$')
  1987  (1302)              INCASE 14
  1988  (1303)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEAIN kernel already initialised$')
  1989  (1304)              INCASE 15
  1990  (1305)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL35', 'XMXEBNC return of a bounce message$')
  1991  (1306)              INCASE 16
  1992  (1307)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'XMXEWNA write not allowed$')
  1993  (1308)              INCASE 17
  1994  (1309)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL33', 'XMXENVI no valid indirect buffer$')
  1995  (1310)              INCASE 18
  1996  (1311)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL30', 'XMXEILF illegal function code$')
  1997  (1312)              INCASE 19
  1998  (1313)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XMXEIMA invalid magic number$')
  1999  (1314)              INCASE 20
  2000  (1315)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMXEMFL message space full$')
  2001  (1316)              INCASE 21
  2002  (1317)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL29', 'XMXEILM illegal message size$')
  2003  (1318)              INCASE 22
  2004  (1319)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL28', 'XMXEIPN illegal port number$')
  2005  (1320)              INCASE 23
  2006  (1321)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL42', 'XMXEPRV privileged function, no privilege$')
  2007  (1322)              INCASE 24
  2008  (1323)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'XMXEPVR privilege request refused$')
  2009  (1324)              INCASE 25
  2010  (1325)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', 'XMXERNA remote system not available$')
  2011  (1326)              ELSE
  2012  (1327)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL26', 'library error, XMX offset ')
  2013  (1328)                  OUTPUT(1, 'I6', xmxOffset)
  2014  (1329)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  2015  (1330)          ENDCASE
  2016  (1331)      ENDROUTINE
  2017  (1332)  
  2018  (1333)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2019  (1334)      % Read the remembered nickname out of CHAT:CNFG into myName.
  2020  (1335)      %
  2021  (1336)      % A MISSING FILE IS NOT AN ERROR and must not look like one. The first time
  2022  (1337)      % anybody runs this there is no file, and that is the ordinary case rather
  2023  (1338)      % than a fault - so a failed open leaves myName alone and says nothing. The
  2024  (1339)      % caller has already put RONNY there.
  2025  (1340)      %
  2026  (1341)      % ROUTINEERROR IS THE ONLY WAY TO LEARN THE OPEN FAILED. MON50 does not
  2027  (1342)      % return a status; it returns a device number and raises on failure, the
  2028  (1343)      % same as MON1 does at end of file. Without the handler a first run - which
  2029  (1344)      % is every user's first run - would unwind the program before it joined.
  2030  (1345)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2031  (1346)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : readCnfg
  2032  (1347)          INTEGER : dev, n
  2033  (1348)          INTEGER : c
  2034  (1349)          INTEGER : i, k, m
  2035  (1350)          BOOLEAN : openFailed
  2036  (1351)          BOOLEAN : haveColon
  2037  (1352)  
  2038  (1353)          % A FLAG, NOT A MAGIC VALUE OF dev - see the long note in writeCnfg.
  2039  (1354)          % A failed open leaves 46 in dev, not 0, so "IF dev = 0" never fired and
  2040  (1355)          % this routine went on to READ from a device it had never opened.
  2041  (1356)          0 =: dev
  2042  (1357)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2043  (1358)              % No file, no name, nothing to say.
  2044  (1359)              TRUE =: openFailed
  2045  (1360)          ENDON
  2046  (1361)          FALSE =: openFailed
  2047  (1362)          MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openRead) =: dev
  2048  (1363)  
  2049  (1364)          IF openFailed THEN
  2050  (1365)              RETURN
  2051  (1366)          ENDIF
  2052  (1367)  
  2053  (1368)          % READ THE WHOLE LINE FIRST, then decide. It is now OWNER:NICK rather
  2054  (1369)          % than a bare nickname, and the owner has to be checked before a single
  2055  (1370)          % byte of it is allowed near myName.
  2056  (1371)          0 =: n
  2057  (1372)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2058  (1373)              % End of file arrives this way, and on a one-line file it is the
  2059  (1374)              % NORMAL ending rather than a fault: a name with no carriage
  2060  (1375)              % return after it is still a name. Zero stops the loop below.
  2061  (1376)              0 =: c
  2062  (1377)          ENDON
  2063  (1378)          DO
  2064  (1379)              0 =: c
  2065  (1380)              MON1(dev) =: c
  2066  (1381)              c AND parityMask =: c
  2067  (1382)  
  2068  (1383)              % Stop at the end of the line. Everything after it is somebody
  2069  (1384)              % else's business - a second line, or a comment they added.
  2070  (1385)              IF c < 32 THEN
  2071  (1386)                  0 =: c
  2072  (1387)              ENDIF
  2073  (1388)  
  2074  (1389)              IF c >< 0 THEN
  2075  (1390)                  % The guard IS the bound - PLANC checks no array subscript, so
  2076  (1391)                  % this is the only thing between a long line and whatever the
  2077  (1392)                  % compiler laid out after cnfgLine.
  2078  (1393)                  IF n < 63 THEN
  2079  (1394)                      c =: cnfgLine(n)
  2080  (1395)                      n + 1 =: n
  2081  (1396)                  ENDIF
  2082  (1397)              ENDIF
  2083  (1398)              WHILE c >< 0
  2084  (1399)          ENDDO
  2085  (1400)  
  2086  (1401)          MON43(dev)
  2087  (1402)  
  2088  (1403)          % A FILE THAT EXISTS BUT IS EMPTY KEEPS THE DEFAULT. It is the state a
  2089  (1404)          % bare CREATE-FILE leaves behind, so it is a state real users will have.
  2090  (1405)          IF n = 0 THEN
  2091  (1406)              RETURN
  2092  (1407)          ENDIF
  2093  (1408)  
  2094  (1409)          % ---- WHOSE NICKNAME IS THIS? ---------------------------------------
  2095  (1410)          %
  2096  (1411)          % THE BUG THIS EXISTS TO KILL. CHAT:CNFG lives under whoever ran the
  2097  (1412)          % program, and on this machine that is SYSTEM for everybody. So ONE
  2098  (1413)          % person's /nick became EVERYONE'S name: logging in as SYSTEM and
  2099  (1414)          % starting the client came up as OLAV - a name typed during testing days
  2100  (1415)          % earlier, on a different account. Nothing on screen explained it, and
  2101  (1416)          % the built-in default and the SINTRAN user name were both innocent.
  2102  (1417)          %
  2103  (1418)          % The file now records WHO chose the nickname:  OWNER:NICK
  2104  (1419)          %
  2105  (1420)          % A file with no colon is the OLD format. It is exactly the stale
  2106  (1421)          % stranger's nickname this is meant to stop, so it is IGNORED rather
  2107  (1422)          % than migrated - the next writeCnfg replaces it with an owned one, and
  2108  (1423)          % the machine heals itself without anyone deleting a file by hand.
  2109  (1424)          0 =: k
  2110  (1425)          FALSE =: haveColon
  2111  (1426)          DO
  2112  (1427)              WHILE k < n AND NOT haveColon
  2113  (1428)              IF cnfgLine(k) = 58 THEN
  2114  (1429)                  TRUE =: haveColon
  2115  (1430)              ELSE
  2116  (1431)                  k + 1 =: k
  2117  (1432)              ENDIF
  2118  (1433)          ENDDO
  2119  (1434)  
  2120  (1435)          IF NOT haveColon THEN
  2121  (1436)              RETURN
  2122  (1437)          ENDIF
  2123  (1438)  
  2124  (1439)          % No SINTRAN name means no way to prove ownership, so nothing is
  2125  (1440)          % trusted. Better to join as USER than as a stranger.
  2126  (1441)          IF lenSintranUser = 0 THEN
  2127  (1442)              RETURN
  2128  (1443)          ENDIF
  2129  (1444)  
  2130  (1445)          IF k >< lenSintranUser THEN
  2131  (1446)              RETURN
  2132  (1447)          ENDIF
  2133  (1448)  
  2134  (1449)          % DO/WHILE, NOT "FOR i IN 0:(k - 1)". A computed range that comes out
  2135  (1450)          % BACKWARDS when k is 0 is not something to guess about in a language
  2136  (1451)          % that checks no array bound - and this file already uses this shape
  2137  (1452)          % everywhere else, so it is also the local idiom.
  2138  (1453)          0 =: i
  2139  (1454)          DO
  2140  (1455)              WHILE i < k
  2141  (1456)              IF cnfgLine(i) >< sintranUser(i) THEN
  2142  (1457)                  RETURN
  2143  (1458)              ENDIF
  2144  (1459)              i + 1 =: i
  2145  (1460)          ENDDO
  2146  (1461)  
  2147  (1462)          % ---- it is ours, so take it ----------------------------------------
  2148  (1463)          % k is the colon, so the nickname runs from k+1 up to n-1.
  2149  (1464)          0 =: m
  2150  (1465)          k + 1 =: i
  2151  (1466)          DO
  2152  (1467)              WHILE i < n
  2153  (1468)              IF m < maxNameLen THEN
  2154  (1469)                  cnfgLine(i) =: myName(m)
  2155  (1470)                  m + 1 =: m
  2156  (1471)              ENDIF
  2157  (1472)              i + 1 =: i
  2158  (1473)          ENDDO
  2159  (1474)  
  2160  (1475)          IF m > 0 THEN
  2161  (1476)              m =: lenMyName
  2162  (1477)          ENDIF
  2163  (1478)      ENDROUTINE
  2164  (1479)  
  2165  (1480)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2166  (1481)      % Write myName back to CHAT:CNFG, so the next run offers it.
  2167  (1482)      %
  2168  (1483)      % PLAIN OPEN FIRST, CREATE ONLY IF THAT FAILS, and the order is the point.
  2169  (1484)      % A quoted name asks SINTRAN to CREATE, and a create at a name that already
  2170  (1485)      % exists is refused - measured on the FA wire as SINTRAN error 62, "File
  2171  (1486)      % already exists". Trying the create first would therefore fail on every
  2172  (1487)      % run after the first. Trying the plain open first fails only on the very
  2173  (1488)      % first, which is exactly when a create is what we want.
  2174  (1489)      %
  2175  (1490)      % NOTHING HERE IS FATAL. A user with no space, or a read-only area, still
  2176  (1491)      % gets a working chat - they just get asked their name every time. Saying
  2177  (1492)      % so once is better than either failing or pretending it worked.
  2178  (1493)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2179  (1494)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2180  (1495)      % Ask SINTRAN to create CHAT:CNFG, through a FULL-SIZE command buffer.
  2181  (1496)      %
  2182  (1497)      % MON70 takes BYTES(0:79) - see the note on cmdBuf. The command text is
  2183  (1498)      % copied in, the rest filled with spaces, and the apostrophe already on the
  2184  (1499)      % end of cnfgMake terminates it so the padding is never read as parameters.
  2185  (1500)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2186  (1501)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : makeCnfg
  2187  (1502)          INTEGER : i
  2188  (1503)  
  2189  (1504)          % CREATE THE FILE WITH THE CALL THAT CREATES FILES.
  2190  (1505)          %
  2191  (1506)          % The command-processor detour is gone. It existed because CreateFile
  2192  (1507)          % 221B has no MONn routine - measured, MON221...10101 U - and the only
  2193  (1508)          % other route needed a library that was not on the machine.
  2194  (1509)          %
  2195  (1510)          % IT IS ON THE MACHINE NOW: MON-CALL-1B-A00:BRF, the SINTRAN III Monitor
  2196  (1511)          % Call Package (ND-210913), installed 2026-08-19 and added to the LOAD
  2197  (1512)          % line in CHATCC:MODE between this program and PLANC-1BANK-F00. That
  2198  (1513)          % library supplies 5MON_P, which is what MONITOR_CALL compiles into, so
  2199  (1514)          % every one of the 230 monitor calls is now reachable - not just the 54
  2200  (1515)          % with a MONn routine.
  2201  (1516)          %
  2202  (1517)          % Parameters per ND-860228-2-EN page 125: the FULL filespec in a 64-byte
  2203  (1518)          % buffer, then StartAddress and NoOfPages, both INTEGER4. Zero and zero
  2204  (1519)          % mean an ordinary indexed file that grows as it is written.
  2205  (1520)          FOR i IN 1:sizeNameBuf DO
  2206  (1521)              spaceChar(0) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
  2207  (1522)          ENDFOR
  2208  (1523)  
  2209  (1524)          FOR i IN 1:lenCnfgFull DO
  2210  (1525)              cnfgFull(i - 1) =: nameBuf(i - 1)
  2211  (1526)          ENDFOR
  2212  (1527)  
  2213  (1528)          MONITOR_CALL(221B, nameBuf, startAddr, noOfPages)
  2214  (1529)      ENDROUTINE
  2215  (1530)  
  2216  (1531)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : writeCnfg
  2217  (1532)          INTEGER : dev, i
  2218  (1533)          BYTE : b
  2219  (1534)          BOOLEAN : saveFailed
  2220  (1535)          BOOLEAN : openFailed
  2221  (1536)  
  2222  (1537)          % A FLAG, NOT A MAGIC VALUE OF dev - AND THIS WAS THE BUG.
  2223  (1538)          %
  2224  (1539)          % This used to be "0 =: dev" in the handler and "IF dev = 0 THEN create"
  2225  (1540)          % below. MEASURED 2026-08-19, printing dev straight after a failed open
  2226  (1541)          % on a file that does not exist:
  2227  (1542)          %
  2228  (1543)          %   CNFG: open1 dev=    46
  2229  (1544)          %
  2230  (1545)          % FORTY-SIX, not zero. 46 is SINTRAN's "no such file" - the Monitor Calls
  2231  (1546)          % manual's own PASCAL example tests exactly that: "IF ErrCode = 46 THEN
  2232  (1547)          % ... CreateFile(...)". So the test for "the open failed" never fired,
  2233  (1548)          % the create was NEVER REACHED, and the program went straight on to
  2234  (1549)          % write bytes to a device number it had never been given.
  2235  (1550)          %
  2236  (1551)          % That is the whole of the CHAT:CNFG defect. The quoting, the apostrophe
  2237  (1552)          % terminator and the command buffer size were all real faults in a create
  2238  (1553)          % that was never called in the first place.
  2239  (1554)          %
  2240  (1555)          % A BOOLEAN cannot be confused with a device number, which is the point.
  2241  (1556)          %
  2242  (1557)          % AND THE FLAG IS CLEARED **AFTER** THE ON BLOCK, NOT BEFORE IT.
  2243  (1558)          % Measured 2026-08-19 with the flag cleared before: both opens reported
  2244  (1559)          % failure while returning a perfectly good device number -
  2245  (1560)          %
  2246  (1561)          %   CNFG: open1 dev=    69
  2247  (1562)          %   CNFG: open2 dev=    69
  2248  (1563)          %   CHAT: cannot save the nickname
  2249  (1564)          %
  2250  (1565)          % - so the handler body had run even though nothing failed. Clearing
  2251  (1566)          % after the block is correct whichever way the ON statement behaves: if
  2252  (1567)          % the body only runs on an error it is unchanged, and if it also runs
  2253  (1568)          % once where it stands, the clear undoes it before the call.
  2254  (1569)          0 =: dev
  2255  (1570)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2256  (1571)              TRUE =: openFailed
  2257  (1572)          ENDON
  2258  (1573)          FALSE =: openFailed
  2259  (1574)  
  2260  (1575)          MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openWrite) =: dev
  2261  (1576)  
  2262  (1577)          IF openFailed THEN
  2263  (1578)              % Not there yet. Make it, then open it for real.
  2264  (1579)              ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2265  (1580)                  % A create that fails is not worth its own message - the open
  2266  (1581)                  % below will fail too, and that one already has one.
  2267  (1582)              ENDON
  2268  (1583)              makeCnfg
  2269  (1584)  
  2270  (1585)              ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2271  (1586)                  TRUE =: openFailed
  2272  (1587)              ENDON
  2273  (1588)              FALSE =: openFailed
  2274  (1589)              MON50(cnfgName, cnfgType, openWrite) =: dev
  2275  (1590)          ENDIF
  2276  (1591)  
  2277  (1592)          IF openFailed THEN
  2278  (1593)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL25', 'cannot save the nickname$')
  2279  (1594)              RETURN
  2280  (1595)          ENDIF
  2281  (1596)  
  2282  (1597)          % ONE MESSAGE, NOT ONE PER BYTE. The first version put this handler
  2283  (1598)          % around the write loop and printed inside it, so a failed save said
  2284  (1599)          % "nickname not fully saved" five times - once per character of ANNA
  2285  (1600)          % plus the carriage return. A handler that fires per byte must not
  2286  (1601)          % print per byte; it sets a flag and the caller speaks once.
  2287  (1602)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2288  (1603)              TRUE =: saveFailed
  2289  (1604)          ENDON
  2290  (1605)          FALSE =: saveFailed
  2291  (1606)  
  2292  (1607)          % OWNER FIRST, THEN A COLON, THEN THE NICKNAME.
  2293  (1608)          %
  2294  (1609)          % The file is written as  OWNER:NICK  so the next run can tell whether
  2295  (1610)          % the nickname is the reader's to use. CHAT:CNFG lives under whoever ran
  2296  (1611)          % the program - SYSTEM for everybody on this machine - so without the
  2297  (1612)          % owner one person's /nick silently became everyone's name. See the long
  2298  (1613)          % note in readCnfg.
  2299  (1614)          %
  2300  (1615)          % If SINTRAN would not say who we are there is no owner to record, so
  2301  (1616)          % the colon is written with nothing before it. readCnfg then finds an
  2302  (1617)          % owner length of zero, which cannot match any real user, and ignores the
  2303  (1618)          % nickname. That is the right way round: an unowned nickname is exactly
  2304  (1619)          % the thing we refuse to inherit.
  2305  (1620)          FOR i IN 1:lenSintranUser DO
  2306  (1621)              sintranUser(i - 1) =: b
  2307  (1622)              b MON2(dev)
  2308  (1623)          ENDFOR
  2309  (1624)  
  2310  (1625)          58 =: b
  2311  (1626)          b MON2(dev)
  2312  (1627)  
  2313  (1628)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  2314  (1629)              myName(i - 1) =: b
  2315  (1630)              b MON2(dev)
  2316  (1631)          ENDFOR
  2317  (1632)  
  2318  (1633)          % THE CARRIAGE RETURN MATTERS. readCnfg stops at the first byte below
  2319  (1634)          % 32, and a SINTRAN text file is expected to have one - without it the
  2320  (1635)          % last line runs into whatever follows.
  2321  (1636)          carriageRet =: b
  2322  (1637)          b MON2(dev)
  2323  (1638)  
  2324  (1639)          % CLOSE, ALWAYS - including after a failed write. A file opened for
  2325  (1640)          % sequential write is truncated at CLOSE, not at OPEN, so a program
  2326  (1641)          % that opens and never closes leaves the OLD contents in place and the
  2327  (1642)          % save silently does nothing.
  2328  (1643)          MON43(dev)
  2329  (1644)  
  2330  (1645)          IF saveFailed THEN
  2331  (1646)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL25', 'nickname not fully saved$')
  2332  (1647)          ENDIF
  2333  (1648)      ENDROUTINE
  2334  (1649)  
  2335  (1650)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2336  (1651)      % Settle who we are, before the join goes out. NOTHING IS ASKED.
  2337  (1652)      %
  2338  (1653)      % THE PROMPT THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE, and the reason is worth keeping
  2339  (1654)      % because it was a real design mistake rather than a bug. It asked
  2340  (1655)      % "nickname?" on every single run, which is the wrong shape for a chat
  2341  (1656)      % client on a machine where you have already told SINTRAN who you are by
  2342  (1657)      % logging in. Being asked your name again, every time, by a program running
  2343  (1658)      % in your own file area, is nagging.
  2344  (1659)      %
  2345  (1660)      % The order of preference is now:
  2346  (1661)      %
  2347  (1662)      %   1. CHAT:CNFG in your own file area - what /nick last settled on;
  2348  (1663)      %   2. the built-in fallback, for a user who has never renamed.
  2349  (1664)      %
  2350  (1665)      % THE FALLBACK SHOULD BE THE SINTRAN USER NAME and is not yet. That needs
  2351  (1666)      % GetUserName (GUSNA 214B), and 214 is NOT among the MONn routines the
  2352  (1667)      % PLANC runtime provides - the appendix stops at MON263 and has no 214, the
  2353  (1668)      % same gap that CreateFile 221B falls into. The manual gives the way round
  2354  (1669)      % it: "Any monitor calls not listed here, may be called from a PLANC
  2355  (1670)      % routine if a suitable interface routine is constructed by the user. If
  2356  (1671)      % this is done, the user must load the interface routine BEFORE the PLANC
  2357  (1672)      % runtime library." MAC is installed on this machine, so that door is open
  2358  (1673)      % - it is the next piece of work, not a closed one.
  2359  (1674)      %
  2360  (1675)      % Changing the name is /nick, and the room has to agree to it: the save
  2361  (1676)      % happens when the SERVER confirms with kRenOk, never when the request is
  2362  (1677)      % sent. See showArrived.
  2363  (1678)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2364  (1679)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2365  (1680)      % ASK SINTRAN WHO IS LOGGED IN.
  2366  (1681)      %
  2367  (1682)      % GetUserName (GUSNA 214B), one STR out-parameter - Reference-Manuals/
  2368  (1683)      % ND-860228-2-EN SINTRAN III Monitor Calls.md. Ronny asked the obvious
  2369  (1684)      % question: "where does the name OLAV come from when I start chat as the
  2370  (1685)      % system user?" It came off disk, from a nickname saved during testing, and
  2371  (1686)      % failing that from a name compiled into the program. The client had never
  2372  (1687)      % once asked the machine.
  2373  (1688)      %
  2374  (1689)      % THE COMMENT ABOVE setupName USED TO SAY THIS COULD NOT BE DONE - that 214
  2375  (1690)      % is not among the MONn routines the PLANC runtime provides, and that the
  2376  (1691)      % way round it is a MAC interface routine. That is the SAME reasoning that
  2377  (1692)      % was wrong about MONITOR_CALL by name, and wrong for the same reason: the
  2378  (1693)      % runtime's own MONn set is not the limit. MON-CALL-1B-A00 and its names
  2379  (1694)      % file are both linked into this client, and CHATSV has been calling
  2380  (1695)      % MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) - 267B - through them for weeks.
  2381  (1696)      %
  2382  (1697)      % GUARDED, like every monitor call here. An unguarded one that is refused
  2383  (1698)      % raises ROUTINEERROR and takes the whole program down - that is exactly how
  2384  (1699)      % this client died after joining. If the call is refused we simply keep
  2385  (1700)      % whatever name we already had.
  2386  (1701)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2387  (1702)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : nameFromSintran
  2388  (1703)          INTEGER : i
  2389  (1704)          INTEGER : n
  2390  (1705)          BOOLEAN : failed
  2391  (1706)  
  2392  (1707)          FOR i IN 0:31 DO
  2393  (1708)              32 =: sintranUser(i)
  2394  (1709)          ENDFOR
  2395  (1710)  
  2396  (1711)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2397  (1712)              TRUE =: failed
  2398  (1713)          ENDON
  2399  (1714)          FALSE =: failed
  2400  (1715)  
  2401  (1716)          % Cleared first. Which of the five are IN and which are OUT is not clear
  2402  (1717)          % from the manual - the arrows beside the parameter list disagree with
  2403  (1718)          % the O in the overview table - so every one starts at a harmless value
  2404  (1719)          % and the MACHINE is allowed to settle it. The diagnostic below prints
  2405  (1720)          % what came back.
  2406  (1721)          0 =: dirIndex
  2407  (1722)          0 =: userIndex
  2408  (1723)          0 =: remoteFlag
  2409  (1724)          FOR i IN 0:63 DO
  2410  (1725)              32 =: remoteSystem(i)
  2411  (1726)          ENDFOR
  2412  (1727)  
  2413  (1728)          MONITOR_CALL('GetUserName', sintranUser, dirIndex, userIndex, &
  2414  (1729)              remoteFlag, remoteSystem)
  2415  (1730)  
  2416  (1731)          % Clear the handler before anything else can raise under it.
  2417  (1732)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  2418  (1733)          ENDON
  2419  (1734)  
  2420  (1735)          % THE DIAGNOSTIC THAT LIVED HERE HAS DONE ITS JOB, and this is what it
  2421  (1736)          % said. Run on D100 2026-08-22 as user SYSTEM, old build against new:
  2422  (1737)          %
  2423  (1738)          %     @NDCHAT   ->  GetUserName refused        (the old ONE-argument call)
  2424  (1739)          %     @CHAT     ->  SINTRAN says: SYSTEM'      (the FIVE-argument call)
  2425  (1740)          %
  2426  (1741)          % So the call works and the five-parameter form was the whole problem.
  2427  (1742)          % Removed rather than left behind a flag - a diagnostic nobody can turn
  2428  (1743)          % off is noise on every single start-up.
  2429  (1744)          IF failed THEN
  2430  (1745)              RETURN
  2431  (1746)          ENDIF
  2432  (1747)  
  2433  (1748)          % THE ANSWER IS PADDED, AND NOT ONLY WITH SPACES. The name ends at the
  2434  (1749)          % first space, at a zero, or at an APOSTROPHE - the screen above shows
  2435  (1750)          % SYSTEM followed by one. Whether that apostrophe is SINTRAN's padding or
  2436  (1751)          % came from how the diagnostic printed the buffer was NOT established, so
  2437  (1752)          % all three are treated as terminators: an apostrophe cannot occur inside
  2438  (1753)          % a SINTRAN user name, so stopping at one is safe either way.
  2439  (1754)          %
  2440  (1755)          % Without this the join would carry padding inside the name field, and
  2441  (1756)          % two clients that "have the same name" would not match.
  2442  (1757)          0 =: n
  2443  (1758)          DO
  2444  (1759)              WHILE n < 31 AND sintranUser(n) >< 32 AND sintranUser(n) >< 0 &
  2445  (1760)                  AND sintranUser(n) >< 39
  2446  (1761)              n + 1 =: n
  2447  (1762)          ENDDO
  2448  (1763)  
  2449  (1764)          % Remembered for readCnfg, which will not honour a saved nickname unless
  2450  (1765)          % it can prove the nickname belongs to whoever is logged in now.
  2451  (1766)          n =: lenSintranUser
  2452  (1767)  
  2453  (1768)          IF n = 0 THEN
  2454  (1769)              RETURN
  2455  (1770)          ENDIF
  2456  (1771)  
  2457  (1772)          % myName holds maxNameLen bytes and PLANC checks no array bound, so this
  2458  (1773)          % clamp is the only thing between a long user name and the variables
  2459  (1774)          % laid out after it.
  2460  (1775)          IF n > maxNameLen THEN
  2461  (1776)              maxNameLen =: n
  2462  (1777)          ENDIF
  2463  (1778)  
  2464  (1779)          FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
  2465  (1780)              sintranUser(i) =: myName(i)
  2466  (1781)          ENDFOR
  2467  (1782)          n =: lenMyName
  2468  (1783)      ENDROUTINE
  2469  (1784)  
  2470  (1785)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : setupName
  2471  (1786)          INTEGER : i
  2472  (1787)  
  2473  (1788)          % The fallback first, then whatever CHAT:CNFG remembers on top of it.
  2474  (1789)          % That way readCnfg can fail, or find an empty file, and simply leave a
  2475  (1790)          % good name in place - it never has to know what to fall back to, and
  2476  (1791)          % no path reaches the join with an empty name.
  2477  (1792)          %
  2478  (1793)          % THE ORDER IS MOST-SPECIFIC-LAST, each layer overwriting the one below:
  2479  (1794)          %   1. the built-in, which is only a shape to start from;
  2480  (1795)          %   2. WHO SINTRAN SAYS YOU ARE - you already logged in to tell it;
  2481  (1796)          %   3. a nickname you deliberately chose with /nick and that was saved.
  2482  (1797)          FOR i IN 1:lenDefault DO
  2483  (1798)              defaultName(i - 1) =: myName(i - 1)
  2484  (1799)          ENDFOR
  2485  (1800)          lenDefault =: lenMyName
  2486  (1801)  
  2487  (1802)          nameFromSintran
  2488  (1803)  
  2489  (1804)          readCnfg
  2490  (1805)  
  2491  (1806)          % NOTHING IS ANNOUNCED HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX.
  2492  (1807)          %
  2493  (1808)          % This used to print "you are OLAV  (/nick to change)" the moment the
  2494  (1809)          % name was settled locally - BEFORE the join had been sent, let alone
  2495  (1810)          % answered. When the server then refused the name the screen read:
  2496  (1811)          %
  2497  (1812)          %     you are OLAV  (/nick to change)
  2498  (1813)          %     joining (LOBBY) - type and press return
  2499  (1814)          %     OLAV: that nickname is taken
  2500  (1815)          %
  2501  (1816)          % which states an identity and takes it away two lines later. A name we
  2502  (1817)          % merely INTEND to use is not an identity; only the server can grant it,
  2503  (1818)          % and only the WELCOME says it has. The announcement now lives where the
  2504  (1819)          % welcome is handled, so it is printed once and only when it is true.
  2505  (1820)      ENDROUTINE
  2506  (1821)  
  2507  (1822)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2508  (1823)      % Point at a room, given its name - "LOBBY".
  2509  (1824)      %
  2510  (1825)      % THE PREFIX IS GONE, and with it the bug it existed to fix. This routine
  2511  (1826)      % used to paste "CHAT-" in front, because the room name WAS a port name and
  2512  (1827)      % the port is registered as CHAT-GENERAL rather than GENERAL. /join copied
  2513  (1828)      % the typed argument straight in, asked XROUT for a name that does not
  2514  (1829)      % exist, and the client stayed where it was - "/join doesn't work".
  2515  (1830)      %
  2516  (1831)      % There is no port name to build now. One port, *CHAT, and the room is
  2517  (1832)      % carried in the message, so the room name is just the room name. The whole
  2518  (1833)      % class of mistake goes with it.
  2519  (1834)      %
  2520  (1835)      % shortAt is an index into "typed"; a caller with the name somewhere else
  2521  (1836)      % copies it into typed first. That keeps this routine to one source and one
  2522  (1837)      % rule, which is the whole point of having it.
  2523  (1838)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2524  (1839)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : setRoom (shortAt, shortLen)
  2525  (1840)          INTEGER : i
  2526  (1841)  
  2527  (1842)          FOR i IN 1:shortLen DO
  2528  (1843)              typed(shortAt + i - 1) =: roomName(i - 1)
  2529  (1844)          ENDFOR
  2530  (1845)          shortLen =: lenRoomName
  2531  (1846)  
  2532  (1847)          % A TOPIC BELONGS TO ONE ROOM. Keeping the old room's topic across a
  2533  (1848)          % join would put a line in the header that is about somewhere else -
  2534  (1849)          % worse than showing none, because it reads as true. The new room's
  2535  (1850)          % topic arrives with its welcome if it has one.
  2536  (1851)          0 =: lenTopic
  2537  (1852)      ENDROUTINE
  2538  (1853)  
  2539  (1854)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2540  (1855)      % Remember a topic that has just arrived, so the header can show it.
  2541  (1856)      %
  2542  (1857)      % Called from BOTH renderers' kTopic branches in CHATARR.PLNC - the screen
  2543  (1858)      % one and the line one. The line renderer has no header to put it in, but
  2544  (1859)      % keeping it there too costs one copy and means the two paths cannot drift
  2545  (1860)      % into disagreeing about what the topic is.
  2546  (1861)      %
  2547  (1862)      % CLAMPED, because the length arrives off the wire. The server truncates at
  2548  (1863)      % maxTopicLen and this buffer is that size, but a length is not trustworthy
  2549  (1864)      % just because it usually behaves - PLANC checks no array bound, so an
  2550  (1865)      % overlong one would write past the end in silence.
  2551  (1866)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2552  (1867)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : setTopic(at, length)
  2553  (1868)          INTEGER : i
  2554  (1869)          INTEGER : take
  2555  (1870)  
  2556  (1871)          length =: take
  2557  (1872)          IF take > maxTopicLen THEN
  2558  (1873)              maxTopicLen =: take
  2559  (1874)          ENDIF
  2560  (1875)          IF take < 0 THEN
  2561  (1876)              0 =: take
  2562  (1877)          ENDIF
  2563  (1878)          FOR i IN 1:take DO
  2564  (1879)              inBuf(at + i - 1) =: curTopic(i - 1)
  2565  (1880)          ENDFOR
  2566  (1881)          take =: lenTopic
  2567  (1882)      ENDROUTINE
  2568  (1883)  
  2569  (1884)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2570  (1885)      % Say which room we are in, IN PARENTHESES.
  2571  (1886)      %
  2572  (1887)      % The client joins a room at start-up whether or not anybody asked it to,
  2573  (1888)      % and until now nothing on screen said which one - so every line you typed
  2574  (1889)      % went somewhere you had not been told about. The short name is shown, the
  2575  (1890)      % prefix left off: "CHAT-" is on every room and carries no information.
  2576  (1891)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2577  (1892)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showRoom
  2578  (1893)          INTEGER : i
  2579  (1894)  
  2580  (1895)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '(')
  2581  (1896)          FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
  2582  (1897)              roomName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
  2583  (1898)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
  2584  (1899)          ENDFOR
  2585  (1900)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', ')')
  2586  (1901)      ENDROUTINE
  2587  (1902)  
  2588  (1903)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2589  (1904)      % Send the join, as a LETTER through XROUT.
  2590  (1905)      %
  2591  (1906)      % The room is reached by NAME - XROUT looks it up and forwards, and this is
  2592  (1907)      % also where the seat is taken, before the server ever sees us. Everything
  2593  (1908)      % after the join goes port to port with no name lookup at all, which is why
  2594  (1909)      % the server's magic number is kept from its first reply.
  2595  (1910)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2596  (1911)      % ------------------------------------------------------------------
  2597  (1912)      % Take a new nickname LOCALLY, from typed. No message goes anywhere.
  2598  (1913)      %
  2599  (1914)      % Used when a join was refused for a duplicate name: there is no room
  2600  (1915)      % to announce a rename to, so this just changes who we will say we are
  2601  (1916)      % the next time we knock.
  2602  (1917)      %
  2603  (1918)      % Bounded by maxNameLen, checked by the caller. PLANC checks no array
  2604  (1919)      % bound, so that check is the only thing between a long name and
  2605  (1920)      % whatever the compiler laid out after myName.
  2606  (1921)      % ------------------------------------------------------------------
  2607  (1922)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : setMyName(nameAt, nameLen)
  2608  (1923)          INTEGER : i
  2609  (1924)          FOR i IN 1:nameLen DO
  2610  (1925)              typed(nameAt + i - 1) =: myName(i - 1)
  2611  (1926)          ENDFOR
  2612  (1927)          nameLen =: lenMyName
  2613  (1928)      ENDROUTINE
  2614  (1929)  
  2615  (1930)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2616  (1931)      % Say who we are now. Called after a LOCAL rename, so the user can see the
  2617  (1932)      % name the next knock will use.
  2618  (1933)      %
  2619  (1934)      % THIS ROUTINE WAS CALLED AND NEVER EXISTED. The /nick recovery path has
  2620  (1935)      % read
  2621  (1936)      %
  2622  (1937)      %     setMyName(at, argLen)
  2623  (1938)      %     showMyName
  2624  (1939)      %     sendJoin
  2625  (1940)      %
  2626  (1941)      % since the day it was written, and there was no showMyName anywhere in the
  2627  (1942)      % file. PLANC answered
  2628  (1943)      %
  2629  (1944)      %     2494  (1834)/HANDLECOMM  *** ERROR   - ILLEGAL SYNTAX "SHOWMYNAME"
  2630  (1945)      %
  2631  (1946)      % every time - and nobody saw it, because the listing is the only place a
  2632  (1947)      % PLANC error survives and CHAT:LIST has never once been read. The MODE
  2633  (1948)      % file says so in as many words: "Not one *** ERROR in CHAT:LIST is the
  2634  (1949)      % only green light here." Every client build before 2026-08-21 went in
  2635  (1950)      % unchecked, so how much of the program the compiler skipped after this
  2636  (1951)      % point is NOT KNOWN.
  2637  (1952)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2638  (1953)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showMyName
  2639  (1954)          INTEGER : i
  2640  (1955)  
  2641  (1956)          % JUST THE NAME, NO SENTENCE AROUND IT. This used to print a whole line -
  2642  (1957)          % "$CHAT: you are OLAV$" - which made it useless anywhere the name is
  2643  (1958)          % part of something else: a prompt, or "joining LOBBY as OLAV". A routine
  2644  (1959)          % called showMyName should show the name and nothing but; whoever wants a
  2645  (1960)          % sentence builds it.
  2646  (1961)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  2647  (1962)              myName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
  2648  (1963)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
  2649  (1964)          ENDFOR
  2650  (1965)      ENDROUTINE
  2651  (1966)  
  2652  (1967)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : sendJoin
  2653  (1968)          XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
  2654  (1969)          INTEGER : length
  2655  (1970)  
  2656  (1971)          % The XSLET header, built in a local buffer. 123 is our reference
  2657  (1972)          % number, echoed back so a task with several requests outstanding can
  2658  (1973)          % tell the answers apart.
  2659  (1974)          % A SUBARRAY, because roomName is now storage rather than a literal.
  2660  (1975)          %
  2661  (1976)          % XMPBLET takes portName as BYTES and appends it as parameter 1 of the
  2662  (1977)          % letter - a PLANC BYTES argument carries its own length, so handing it
  2663  (1978)          % the whole 21-byte array would ask XROUT for a name with eleven bytes
  2664  (1979)          % of leftovers on the end. roomName(0:lenRoomName-1) is exactly the name
  2665  (1980)          % and nothing else. Runtime bounds in a subarray are legal: the PLANC
  2666  (1981)          % manual's own example is ia1(0:10,i:k-2).
  2667  (1982)          % ADDRESSED TO THE SERVICE, NOT TO THE ROOM. The room is carried in the
  2668  (1983)          % message by buildJoin below.
  2669  (1984)          xmpblet(letterBuf, 64, offSet, 123, hereSystem, &
  2670  (1985)              servName(0:lenServName - 1)) =: returnStatus
  2671  (1986)          IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
  2672  (1987)              showStatus('xmpblet', returnStatus)
  2673  (1988)              RETURN
  2674  (1989)          ENDIF
  2675  (1990)  
  2676  (1991)          xmpfget(0, 200, msgIdent) =: returnStatus
  2677  (1992)          IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
  2678  (1993)              showStatus('xmpfget', returnStatus)
  2679  (1994)              RETURN
  2680  (1995)          ENDIF
  2681  (1996)  
  2682  (1997)          % The header first...
  2683  (1998)          offSet =: uLength
  2684  (1999)          xmpfwri(0, 0, &
  2685  (2000)              ADDR(letterBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
  2686  (2001)              0, uLength, wLength) =: returnStatus
  2687  (2002)  
  2688  (2003)          % ...then OUR data after it. XROUT reads only the header and forwards
  2689  (2004)          % the rest untouched, so this is where the nickname rides.
  2690  (2005)          buildJoin =: length
  2691  (2006)          xmpfwri(0, wLength, &
  2692  (2007)              ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
  2693  (2008)              0, length, offSet) =: returnStatus
  2694  (2009)  
  2695  (2010)          xmprout(0, msgIdent, myPort) =: returnStatus
  2696  (2011)          IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
  2697  (2012)              showStatus('xmprout (the join)', returnStatus)
  2698  (2013)          ENDIF
  2699  (2014)      ENDROUTINE
  2700  (2015)  
  2701  (2016)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2702  (2017)      % Send one of our messages straight to the server, no XROUT involved.
  2703  (2018)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2704  (2019)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : sendToServer(length)
  2705  (2020)          XMMSGIDENTIFIER : ident
  2706  (2021)          xmpfget(0, length, ident) =: returnStatus
  2707  (2022)          IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
  2708  (2023)              RETURN
  2709  (2024)          ENDIF
  2710  (2025)          xmpfwri(0, 0, &
  2711  (2026)              ADDR(outBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
  2712  (2027)              0, length, wLength) =: returnStatus
  2713  (2028)  
  2714  (2029)          % XMPSEND, NOT XMPFSND - THIS WAS THE BUG. Plan item 5.7, written
  2715  (2030)          % down before this session and never actually applied here.
  2716  (2031)          %
  2717  (2032)          % XMPFSND takes no message parameter: "the current (default)
  2718  (2033)          % message buffer is assumed, namely the port current message if
  2719  (2034)          % one exists, or, if none, the task current message" (ND-60.164.3,
  2720  (2035)          % XMPFSND). XMPFGET above only makes the new buffer TASK current -
  2721  (2036)          % it has no port parameter and cannot make it PORT current. If a
  2722  (2037)          % message this program received (drainPort runs constantly) left a
  2723  (2038)          % port-current message behind, XMPFSND would send THAT instead of
  2724  (2039)          % the reply just built here - and it would keep sending the SAME
  2725  (2040)          % one until whatever made it port-current changed, regardless of
  2726  (2041)          % what the user typed next. That is exactly the live symptom: one
  2727  (2042)          % message, repeating forever, no matter what was sent after it.
  2728  (2043)          %
  2729  (2044)          % XMPSEND names the exact message: "the message specified by
  2730  (2045)          % msgIdentifier is set as task current AND as port current for
  2731  (2046)          % localPort. Then the message is sent as described under XMPFSND."
  2732  (2047)          % It removes the ambiguity outright rather than hoping nothing else
  2733  (2048)          % touches the port between XMPFWRI and the send.
  2734  (2049)          xmpsend(0, ident, myPort, serverMagic) =: returnStatus
  2735  (2050)      ENDROUTINE
  2736  (2051)  
  2737  (2052)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2738  (2053)      % Show what arrived. The room speaks in the same message format we send.
  2739  (2054)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  2740  (2055)      % ===================================================================
  2741  (2056)      % THE SCREEN RENDERER
  2742  (2057)      %
  2743  (2058)      % Brought over from CHATUI.PLNC, which proved this layout on D100 on
  2744  (2059)      % 2026-08-25 before any of it went near the working client.
  2745  (2060)      %
  2746  (2061)      % WHAT WAS LEFT BEHIND, and it is the important half of this merge:
  2747  (2062)      % CHATUI invented a room name, a member count, a speaker list and a
  2748  (2063)      % five-second fake message feed, because it had no server behind it.
  2749  (2064)      % Every one of those is gone. The renderer now has to be given real
  2750  (2065)      % values or show nothing, which is the only way the example text in
  2751  (2066)      % the original mockup stops being example text.
  2752  (2067)      %
  2753  (2068)      % Also left behind: its own stack, its own spaceByte, and its own
  2754  (2069)      % input buffer - this client already has typed/typedLen, and reusing
  2755  (2070)      % them is what lets the whole command parser stay untouched.
  2756  (2071)      % ===================================================================
  2757  (2072)  
  2758  (2073)  
  2759  (2074)      CONSTANT bitEsc=1024
  2760  (2075)      CONSTANT bitCpos=2048
  2761  (2076)      CONSTANT bitVdu=16384
  2762  (2077)  
  2763  (2078)      % ---- THE GEOMETRY, ALL OF IT, IN ONE PLACE ---------------------------
  2764  (2079)      %
  2765  (2080)      % The three sections. Heights count rows INCLUSIVE, and the shared edges
  2766  (2081)      % are deliberate: topHigh ends on row 3 and midRow starts on row 3.
  2767  (2082)      CONSTANT topRow=1
  2768  (2083)      CONSTANT topHigh=3           % rows 1-3
  2769  (2084)      CONSTANT midRow=3
  2770  (2085)      CONSTANT midHigh=19          % rows 3-21
  2771  (2086)      CONSTANT botRow=21
  2772  (2087)      CONSTANT botHigh=4           % rows 21-24
  2773  (2088)  
  2774  (2089)      % THE THREE SINGLE ROWS, BY PHYSICAL NUMBER.
  2775  (2090)      %
  2776  (2091)      % closePanel has to ask "was this row under the panel?" about each of them,
  2777  (2092)      % and a bare 2, 22 and 23 in those tests would say nothing to a reader and
  2778  (2093)      % would drift the first time the layout moved. They are written here beside
  2779  (2094)      % the windows they belong to, with the arithmetic that produces them:
  2780  (2095)      %
  2781  (2096)      %   status  topRow + 1   the room name, count and clock
  2782  (2097)      %   input   botRow + 1   the line being typed
  2783  (2098)      %   bar     botRow + 2   the window list
  2784  (2099)      CONSTANT statusPRow=2
  2785  (2100)      CONSTANT inputPRow=22
  2786  (2101)      CONSTANT barPRow=23
  2787  (2102)      CONSTANT scrWide=80
  2788  (2103)  
  2789  (2104)      % Visible chat rows, in frame B's LOCAL coordinates. Frame B starts on
  2790  (2105)      % physical row 3, so its local row 2 is physical row 4.
  2791  (2106)      CONSTANT firstMsgRow=2       % local - physical row 4
  2792  (2107)      CONSTANT msgRows=17          % rows 4..20
  2793  (2108)  
  2794  (2109)      % A stored line is 77 characters covering physical columns 3..79. Frame B
  2795  (2110)      % starts at column 1, so local column = physical column and the line is
  2796  (2111)      % painted at column 3.
  2797  (2112)      %
  2798  (2113)      %   buffer 0..4    time      cols 3-7
  2799  (2114)      %   buffer 5..6    gap       cols 8-9
  2800  (2115)      %   buffer 7..18   speaker   cols 10-21
  2801  (2116)      %   buffer 19      gap       col  22     <- ALWAYS BLANK, see below
  2802  (2117)      %   buffer 20..76  text      cols 23-79
  2803  (2118)      CONSTANT lineWide=77
  2804  (2119)      CONSTANT textCol=3           % where a stored line is painted
  2805  (2120)  
  2806  (2121)      % TRUE while the window is up. Two things read it: the key loop, which
  2807  (2122)      % closes the panel instead of typing into the input line, and the repaint,
  2808  (2123)      % which CLIPS to what the panel does not cover.
  2809  (2124)      BOOLEAN : panelUp := FALSE
  2810  (2125)  
  2811  (2126)      % WHERE THE PANEL IS - AND WHY THESE ARE AT MODULE LEVEL.
  2812  (2127)      %
  2813  (2128)      % These four used to be LOCALS of drawPanel: computed, drawn with, and gone
  2814  (2129)      % when it returned. Nothing else in the program knew where the window was,
  2815  (2130)      % and that one fact caused BOTH halves of the bug Ronny reported on
  2816  (2131)      % 2026-08-31 - the whole screen repainted on close, and the room frozen
  2817  (2132)      % behind an open window.
  2818  (2133)      %
  2819  (2134)      % Neither was a bad choice. Without the rectangle they were the ONLY
  2820  (2135)      % choices: you cannot clip to a rectangle you do not have, and you cannot
  2821  (2136)      % repaint just what was covered either, so drawAll and "do not paint at
  2822  (2137)      % all" are what is left. THE BUG WAS UPSTREAM OF BOTH.
  2823  (2138)      %
  2824  (2139)      % TESTUI.PLNC keeps smallRow/smallCol/smallHigh/smallWide and bigRow/... at
  2825  (2140)      % module level, and everything it does well follows from that. This is the
  2826  (2141)      % same thing. Rule 0 in PLANC-INTERACTIVE-SCREEN-PATTERNS.md.
  2827  (2142)      %
  2828  (2143)      % NOT called panelWide - that name is already the panel BUFFER's line
  2829  (2144)      % width, a constant, and two different widths under one name is exactly the
  2830  (2145)      % kind of collision this file has paid for before.
  2831  (2146)      INTEGER : panRow  := 0
  2832  (2147)      INTEGER : panCol  := 0
  2833  (2148)      INTEGER : panHigh := 0
  2834  (2149)      INTEGER : panWid  := 0
  2835  (2150)      CONSTANT offTime=0
  2836  (2151)      CONSTANT offWho=7
  2837  (2152)  
  2838  (2153)      % TWELVE, NOT THIRTEEN, AND THE LAST COLUMN IS THE REASON.
  2839  (2154)      %
  2840  (2155)      % The speaker field used to be thirteen and the text began in the very next
  2841  (2156)      % column, so a name that filled the field ran straight into the message:
  2842  (2157)      %
  2843  (2158)      %     05:11  VIDDAUS@VIDDAhello from vidda across the trunk
  2844  (2159)      %
  2845  (2160)      % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26. It needs a QUALIFIED name to show up - a
  2846  (2161)      % local speaker is short and leaves a gap by luck - so it appears exactly
  2847  (2162)      % when two machines are talking, which is when the name matters most.
  2848  (2163)      %
  2849  (2164)      % Narrowing the field by one keeps buffer position 19 blank for every line,
  2850  (2165)      % because blankLine fills the whole buffer with spaces and nothing ever
  2851  (2166)      % writes there. A gap that exists because nothing can write into it does
  2852  (2167)      % not depend on anyone remembering to add a space.
  2853  (2168)      CONSTANT widWho=12
  2854  (2169)      CONSTANT offText=20
  2855  (2170)      CONSTANT widText=57
  2856  (2171)  
  2857  (2172)      % ---- THE SCROLLBACK CACHE --------------------------------------------
  2858  (2173)      %
  2859  (2174)      % A RING far larger than the window. Scrolling is one index change and no
  2860  (2175)      % text is copied; paging is a change to viewFrom and a repaint of the
  2861  (2176)      % middle section only - about 1300 characters, not the 2000 a whole screen
  2862  (2177)      % would cost.
  2863  (2178)      %
  2864  (2179)      % 300 lines x 77 = 23100 bytes, about 11550 words.
  2865  (2180)      %
  2866  (2181)      % IT WAS 100, WHICH WAS OVER-CAUTIOUS. The linker reported
  2867  (2182)      % FREE: P 066220-177777 after the whole program and all its libraries -
  2868  (2183)      % roughly 37000 words spare - so the cache can be three times the size and
  2869  (2184)      % still leave room. Ask the linker rather than guessing: the FREE line is
  2870  (2185)      % printed on every build and is the only number that matters.
  2871  (2186)      % =====================================================================
  2872  (2187)      % WINDOWS - ALTERNATIVE CONTENTS FOR ONE RECTANGLE
  2873  (2188)      %
  2874  (2189)      % Window 1 is THE ROOM. Windows 2 upward are one-to-one conversations -
  2875  (2190)      % the `=KARI' entries the mockup's bar always showed and nothing stood
  2876  (2191)      % behind. They are NOT overlapping windows and there is deliberately no
  2877  (2192)      % window manager here: only one is ever painted, so there is no clipping,
  2878  (2193)      % no stack order and no three-places-must-agree problem. TESTUI already
  2879  (2194)      % paid for that lesson.
  2880  (2195)      %
  2881  (2196)      % EACH WINDOW OWNS A SLICE OF ONE ARRAY rather than having an array of its
  2882  (2197)      % own. The slice is (w - 1) * winLines lines long, so the ring arithmetic
  2883  (2198)      % is unchanged - it just starts somewhere other than zero. One array also
  2884  (2199)      % means the memory cost is decided once, here, instead of growing every
  2885  (2200)      % time a window is added.
  2886  (2201)      CONSTANT maxWins=4
  2887  (2202)      CONSTANT winLines=75
  2888  (2203)      CONSTANT cacheLines=300      % maxWins * winLines - the whole array
  2889  (2204)  
  2890  (2205)      BYTES : cache(0:23099)
  2891  (2206)  
  2892  (2207)      % PER WINDOW, all of it. An earlier draft kept the room's counters as
  2893  (2208)      % scalars and only the conversations in arrays; that meant every routine
  2894  (2209)      % had two paths and the room's path was the one that got tested.
  2895  (2210)      INTEGER ARRAY : winCount(0:4)   % lines held in that window
  2896  (2211)      INTEGER ARRAY : winFirst(0:4)   % slot holding its OLDEST line
  2897  (2212)      INTEGER ARRAY : winView(0:4)    % how far back it is paged, 0 = live
  2898  (2213)      INTEGER ARRAY : winUnread(0:4)  % lines arrived since it was last looked at
  2899  (2214)      INTEGER ARRAY : winUsed(0:4)    % 0 = this window does not exist yet
  2900  (2215)      INTEGER ARRAY : winBell(0:4)    % your nick appeared - the * on the bar
  2901  (2216)  
  2902  (2217)      % EVERY LINE EVER ADDED TO THAT WINDOW, counted. Not the number held -
  2903  (2218)      % this one never goes down, so it gives each line a number of its own that
  2904  (2219)      % stays with it while it moves up the pane.
  2905  (2220)      %
  2906  (2221)      % That is what lets the painter tell "this row already shows this line"
  2907  (2222)      % from "this row shows a different line that happens to sit in the same
  2908  (2223)      % ring slot" - a slot number alone cannot, because the ring reuses slots.
  2909  (2224)      INTEGER ARRAY : winTotal(0:4)
  2910  (2225)  
  2911  (2226)      % What the bar calls it. The room's label is the room name; a conversation
  2912  (2227)      % is labelled with the other person, and the = is added when it is drawn
  2913  (2228)      % rather than stored, so the name stays comparable with one that arrives.
  2914  (2229)      BYTES : winLabel(0:79)          % 5 windows x 16 characters
  2915  (2230)      INTEGER ARRAY : winLabLen(0:4)
  2916  (2231)  
  2917  (2232)      % WHICH ONE IS ON SCREEN. Everything that paints reads this and nothing
  2918  (2233)      % else decides it.
  2919  (2234)      INTEGER : curWin := 1
  2920  (2235)  
  2921  (2236)      % Kept as the names the rest of the file already uses, so that only the
  2922  (2237)      % routines that TOUCH the ring had to change. They are the current
  2923  (2238)      % window's counters, copied in and out around anything that moves them.
  2924  (2239)      INTEGER : cacheCount := 0    % lines held, 0..winLines
  2925  (2240)      INTEGER : cacheFirst := 0    % slot holding the OLDEST line
  2926  (2241)  
  2927  (2242)      % HOW FAR BACK WE ARE LOOKING, counted in lines from the newest.
  2928  (2243)      % ZERO MEANS LIVE. Keeping it as a distance rather than an absolute slot
  2929  (2244)      % is what lets a new arrival leave it alone without any adjustment.
  2930  (2245)      INTEGER : viewFrom := 0
  2931  (2246)  
  2932  (2247)      % THE LAST /tell, KEPT, because the server's confirmation does not carry
  2933  (2248)      % it back. kDirSent says only WHO it reached, so if the text were not held
  2934  (2249)      % here the sent half of a conversation could not be shown at all - the
  2935  (2250)      % window would fill with one side of it and read as a row of unanswered
  2936  (2251)      % messages.
  2937  (2252)      %
  2938  (2253)      % Overwritten by every /tell. Two sent before either is confirmed would
  2939  (2254)      % show the second text against both, which is wrong but harmless and
  2940  (2255)      % vanishingly rare on a link this fast; holding a queue for it would cost
  2941  (2256)      % more than the fault does.
  2942  (2257)      BYTES : lastTell(0:76)
  2943  (2258)      INTEGER : lastTellLen := 0
  2944  (2259)  
  2945  (2260)      % WHAT IS ACTUALLY ON EACH ROW OF THE PANE, by line number.
  2946  (2261)      %
  2947  (2262)      % THE POINT OF THIS IS NOT TO PAINT WHAT IS ALREADY THERE. Repainting all
  2948  (2263)      % seventeen rows for one arriving line is about 1300 characters - well over
  2949  (2264)      % a second on a real 9600 baud line - and it makes the whole pane flicker
  2950  (2265)      % for a change of one row.
  2951  (2266)      %
  2952  (2267)      % VTM HAS NO SCROLL VERB. There is no SCROLL, ROLL, MARGIN, REGION or PAGE
  2953  (2268)      % anywhere in it, and the API reference says in as many words that a
  2954  (2269)      % scrolling pane means keeping your own buffer and repainting the lines. So
  2955  (2270)      % the pane cannot be told to shift; what it CAN do is skip every row whose
  2956  (2271)      % content has not changed, which is all of them on a screen that is still
  2957  (2272)      % filling and none of them once it is full and shifting.
  2958  (2273)      %
  2959  (2274)      % Minus one means "blank". Zero is a real line number.
  2960  (2275)      INTEGER ARRAY : rowShown(0:16)
  2961  (2276)      INTEGER : shadowWin := 0     % which window the shadow describes
  2962  (2277)  
  2963  (2278)      % ONE ROW OF SPACES, for a pane row with no line on it. Held rather than
  2964  (2279)      % cleared with VTCREC so that blanking a row costs exactly what painting
  2965  (2280)      % one costs, and goes through the same single write.
  2966  (2281)      BYTES : blankRow := '                                                                             '
  2967  (2282)  
  2968  (2283)      % WHAT THE STATUS LINE LAST SHOWED. Same rule as rowShown: a region that
  2969  (2284)      % has not changed is not painted. The status line is redrawn on EVERY
  2970  (2285)      % arriving message, and it changes only when the clock ticks a minute, the
  2971  (2286)      % count moves, the room changes or scrollback is entered - so most of
  2972  (2287)      % those repaints put back exactly what was already there.
  2973  (2288)      INTEGER : lastMin := 0 - 1
  2974  (2289)      INTEGER : lastHour := 0 - 1
  2975  (2290)      INTEGER : lastCount := 0 - 1
  2976  (2291)      INTEGER : lastView := 0 - 1
  2977  (2292)      INTEGER : lastRemote := 0 - 1
  2978  (2293)      INTEGER : lastRoomLen := 0 - 1
  2979  (2294)  
  2980  (2295)      % AND THE TOPIC, for the same reason. drawStatus repaints the row only
  2981  (2296)      % when something on it moved, so a topic that changed while the room name
  2982  (2297)      % and the count did not would never reach the screen. The LENGTH is enough
  2983  (2298)      % here: a topic is replaced wholesale, never edited in place, so two
  2984  (2299)      % different topics of the same length are the one case this misses and the
  2985  (2300)      % row is repainted on every room change and every minute anyway.
  2986  (2301)      INTEGER : lastTopicL := 0 - 1
  2987  (2302)      BYTES : lastRoom(0:19)
  2988  (2303)  
  2989  (2304)      % THE TYPED LINE ONLY CHANGES WHEN IT IS TYPED IN. Everything else that
  2990  (2305)      % repaints still has to put the CURSOR back - SINTRAN echoes at wherever
  2991  (2306)      % the cursor happens to be - but parking the cursor costs a few bytes and
  2992  (2307)      % rewriting the whole row costs eighty.
  2993  (2308)      BOOLEAN : inputDirty := TRUE
  2994  (2309)  
  2995  (2310)      % One line under construction.
  2996  (2311)      BYTES : lineBuf(0:76)
  2997  (2312)      INTEGER ARRAY : cal(0:6)
  2998  (2313)  
  2999  (2314)      % ---- THE INPUT LINE --------------------------------------------------
  3000  (2315)      % 76 columns, physical 4..79 on row 22.
  3001  (2316)      CONSTANT inpMax=76
  3002  (2317)      CONSTANT inpRow=2            % LOCAL row inside frame C - physical 22
  3003  (2318)      CONSTANT inpCol=4
  3004  (2319)  
  3005  (2320)      % DOES THIS ROOM REACH PAST THIS MACHINE?
  3006  (2321)      %
  3007  (2322)      % Set when a speaker arrives whose name carries an @ - which is the server
  3008  (2323)      % saying "this one is on another machine". Derived from real traffic, not
  3009  (2324)      % from a table this client would have to keep in step with the server's.
  3010  (2325)      %
  3011  (2326)      % It does not go back to FALSE on its own. A room that HAS been trunked is
  3012  (2327)      % still a trunked room when the other side goes quiet, and flickering the
  3013  (2328)      % marker off every idle minute would be worse than leaving it on.
  3014  (2329)      BOOLEAN : sawRemote := FALSE
  3015  (2330)  
  3016  (2331)      % ---- SINGLE CHARACTERS -----------------------------------------------
  3017  (2332)      % A STRING is not a BYTE in PLANC. 'x' =: buf(i) does not compile, and the
  3018  (2333)      % error names the ARRAY, which sends you to the declaration where nothing
  3019  (2334)      % is wrong.
  3020  (2335)      BYTES : digits := '0123456789'
  3021  (2336)      % spaceByte is NOT declared again here - the client already has one,
  3022  (2337)      % and a second draws IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED.
  3023  (2338)      BYTES : colonByte := ':'
  3024  (2339)      BYTES : starByte := '*'
  3025  (2340)      BYTES : atByte := '@'
  3026  (2341)  
  3027  (2342)      % MON3's echo table. Ignored for every strategy except 7; it exists
  3028  (2343)      % because the call takes three arguments.
  3029  (2344)      BOOLEAN ARRAY PACKED : echoTab(0:127)
  3030  (2345)  
  3031  (2346)      INTEGER4 : termType
  3032  (2347)      INTEGER4 : termKind
  3033  (2348)  
  3034  (2349)      % ---- WHO WE ARE, and the room --------------------------------------
  3035  (2350)      % PSEUDO-KEYS. Returned by pollKey for things that arrive as escape
  3036  (2351)      % sequences, so the main loop never has to know about them.
  3037  (2352)      % VTM'S OWN KEY CODES, MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-25 with KEYPROB.PLNC.
  3038  (2353)      % These are what VTINBT hands back after it has decoded whatever the
  3039  (2354)      % terminal actually sent, so they are the same on a TDV as on a VT100.
  3040  (2355)      %
  3041  (2356)      % Note the cursor keys are SMALL numbers, not high ones - so a rule like
  3042  (2357)      % "anything over 127 is a function key" would be wrong. They are ND
  3043  (2358)      % control codes, and a printable character is its ordinary ASCII value
  3044  (2359)      % with the parity bit already stripped by VTM.
  3045  (2360)      CONSTANT keyPgUp=201
  3046  (2361)      CONSTANT keyPgDn=197
  3047  (2362)      CONSTANT keyUp=28
  3048  (2363)      CONSTANT keyDown=11
  3049  (2364)  
  3050  (2365)      % =====================================================================
  3051  (2366)      % WINDOWS - VTM's own viewport. Absolute coordinates ALWAYS: VTDSCR does
  3052  (2367)      % not nest, and a second call reads its rectangle against the physical
  3053  (2368)      % screen rather than against the current one.
  3054  (2369)      % =====================================================================
  3055  (2370)  
  3056  (2371)      % TRUE only on a terminal that can address its cursor. Decided ONCE
  3057  (2372)      % at start-up from the CTYTP bits and never asked again - a renderer
  3058  (2373)      % that could change under the program would need every drawing site
  3059  (2374)      % to cope with both, which is how a screen ends up half-painted.
  3060  (2375)      %
  3061  (2376)      % FALSE for now. This increment brings the code across and proves it
  3062  (2377)      % compiles, links and fits; nothing calls it yet and the client
  3063  (2378)      % behaves exactly as it did before.
  3064  (2379)      % HOW MANY ARE IN THE ROOM. Zero until a /who answer has been counted -
  3065  (2380)      % CHATUI had this as the literal 14, which is exactly the kind of invented
  3066  (2381)      % number this merge removes. INTEGER4 because intdis takes a double word.
  3067  (2382)      INTEGER4 : hereCount := 0
  3068  (2383)  
  3069  (2384)      BOOLEAN : screenOn := FALSE
  3070  (2385)  
  3071  (2386)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : winEnter(row, col, high, wide)
  3072  (2387)          INTEGER : st
  3073  (2388)          VTDSCR(row, col, row + high - 1, col + wide - 1) =: st
  3074  (2389)      ENDROUTINE
  3075  (2390)  
  3076  (2391)      % Back out to the whole screen. -1 is "to the end", the same convention
  3077  (2392)      % IVTCSCR(1,1,-1,-1) uses. VTINIT is not the only way back.
  3078  (2393)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : winLeave
  3079  (2394)          INTEGER : st
  3080  (2395)          VTDSCR(1, 1, -1, -1) =: st
  3081  (2396)      ENDROUTINE
  3082  (2397)  
  3083  (2398)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : flush
  3084  (2399)          INTEGER : st
  3085  (2400)          VTWBUF =: st
  3086  (2401)      ENDROUTINE
  3087  (2402)  
  3088  (2403)      % =====================================================================
  3089  (2404)      % SMALL HELPERS
  3090  (2405)      % =====================================================================
  3091  (2406)  
  3092  (2407)      % Two digits into a buffer at a given offset.
  3093  (2408)      %
  3094  (2409)      % CLAMPED INSIDE, not at the call sites. MN113's year element is the FULL
  3095  (2410)      % year - 1998, not 98 - and digits(1998/10) is digits(199), a read 190
  3096  (2411)      % bytes past the end of a ten-byte literal. PLANC checks no array bound,
  3097  (2412)      % so it fetches whatever follows and prints a blank where a digit belongs.
  3098  (2413)      % One clamp here covers every caller.
  3099  (2414)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : put2(at, value)
  3100  (2415)          INTEGER : v
  3101  (2416)          value =: v
  3102  (2417)          IF v < 0 THEN
  3103  (2418)              0 =: v
  3104  (2419)          ENDIF
  3105  (2420)          v MOD 100 =: v
  3106  (2421)          digits(v / 10) =: lineBuf(at)
  3107  (2422)          digits(v MOD 10) =: lineBuf(at + 1)
  3108  (2423)      ENDROUTINE
  3109  (2424)  
  3110  (2425)      % A WHOLE NUMBER INTO lineBuf, no leading zeros, and it answers how many
  3111  (2426)      % characters it used so the caller can go on writing after it.
  3112  (2427)      %
  3113  (2428)      % put2 above is fixed at two digits because a clock field is always two.
  3114  (2429)      % An unread count is not: it is usually one digit, and a window left alone
  3115  (2430)      % all afternoon can reach four. Padding it to a fixed width would put a
  3116  (2431)      % ragged gap in the middle of the bar.
  3117  (2432)      %
  3118  (2433)      % CLAMPED AT 9999. Nothing sensible counts higher, and a fifth digit would
  3119  (2434)      % need a fifth byte in the scratch below - which PLANC would not check.
  3120  (2435)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : putNum(at, value)
  3121  (2436)          INTEGER : v
  3122  (2437)          INTEGER : n
  3123  (2438)          INTEGER : i
  3124  (2439)          BYTES : scratch(0:3)
  3125  (2440)  
  3126  (2441)          value =: v
  3127  (2442)          IF v < 0 THEN
  3128  (2443)              0 =: v
  3129  (2444)          ENDIF
  3130  (2445)          IF v > 9999 THEN
  3131  (2446)              9999 =: v
  3132  (2447)          ENDIF
  3133  (2448)  
  3134  (2449)          % Digits come out backwards, so they are held and then written in
  3135  (2450)          % reverse. A zero has to be handled on its own - the loop below would
  3136  (2451)          % produce nothing at all for it.
  3137  (2452)          0 =: n
  3138  (2453)          IF v = 0 THEN
  3139  (2454)              digits(0) =: lineBuf(at)
  3140  (2455)              1 RETURN
  3141  (2456)          ENDIF
  3142  (2457)  
  3143  (2458)          DO
  3144  (2459)              WHILE v > 0
  3145  (2460)              digits(v MOD 10) =: scratch(n)
  3146  (2461)              n + 1 =: n
  3147  (2462)              v / 10 =: v
  3148  (2463)          ENDDO
  3149  (2464)  
  3150  (2465)          FOR i IN 1:n DO
  3151  (2466)              scratch(n - i) =: lineBuf(at + i - 1)
  3152  (2467)          ENDFOR
  3153  (2468)          n RETURN
  3154  (2469)      ENDROUTINE
  3155  (2470)  
  3156  (2471)      % Blank the whole line buffer. Every stored line is PADDED to full width
  3157  (2472)      % so a short line painted over a long one cannot leave the old tail
  3158  (2473)      % behind - that removes the need for a separate erase before each paint.
  3159  (2474)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : blankLine
  3160  (2475)          INTEGER : i
  3161  (2476)          FOR i IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
  3162  (2477)              spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(i)
  3163  (2478)          ENDFOR
  3164  (2479)      ENDROUTINE
  3165  (2480)  
  3166  (2481)      % Copy text into the line buffer at an offset, up to a field width, and
  3167  (2482)      % answer how many characters were taken. MAXINDEX asks the array its own
  3168  (2483)      % declared bound, so no length is ever typed beside a literal - a
  3169  (2484)      % hand-counted length builds clean and shows only as a line in the wrong
  3170  (2485)      % place.
  3171  (2486)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER, BYTES) : putField(at, wide, text)
  3172  (2487)          INTEGER : i
  3173  (2488)          INTEGER : n
  3174  (2489)          MAXINDEX(text, 1) + 1 =: n
  3175  (2490)          IF n > wide THEN
  3176  (2491)              wide =: n
  3177  (2492)          ENDIF
  3178  (2493)          IF n > 0 THEN
  3179  (2494)              FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
  3180  (2495)                  text(i) =: lineBuf(at + i)
  3181  (2496)              ENDFOR
  3182  (2497)          ENDIF
  3183  (2498)          n RETURN
  3184  (2499)      ENDROUTINE
  3185  (2500)  
  3186  (2501)      % =====================================================================
  3187  (2502)      % THE SPEAKER COLUMN - 13 characters against a name that may be longer
  3188  (2503)      %
  3189  (2504)      % THE NAME ARRIVES ALREADY DECIDED. The server qualifies a speaker on
  3190  (2505)      % another machine as ANNA@D102 and leaves a local one bare, so this client
  3191  (2506)      % never has to work out which is which - and never has to know which
  3192  (2507)      % machine it is on, which it has no way of finding out.
  3193  (2508)      %
  3194  (2509)      % An earlier version of this DID decide for itself, from a nickname and a
  3195  (2510)      % system name it had been handed as two literals. That is exactly the kind
  3196  (2511)      % of invented state this merge exists to remove.
  3197  (2512)      %
  3198  (2513)      % What is left is the fitting rule, and it still matters: 13 columns
  3199  (2514)      % against a name that may be 16 plus @D102.
  3200  (2515)      %
  3201  (2516)      %   fits            copied as it is
  3202  (2517)      %   too long, no @  cut at 13
  3203  (2518)      %   too long, @     THE NAME IS CUT, NEVER THE MACHINE
  3204  (2519)      %
  3205  (2520)      % Cutting the machine would be the wrong choice: inside one room you know
  3206  (2521)      % a person from the first letters of their name, but D10 does not tell you
  3207  (2522)      % whether the line came from D100, D102 or D103 - and on a trunked room
  3208  (2523)      % that is the one thing this column is there for.
  3209  (2524)      % =====================================================================
  3210  (2525)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : putWho(who)
  3211  (2526)          INTEGER : total
  3212  (2527)          INTEGER : i
  3213  (2528)          INTEGER : bang
  3214  (2529)          INTEGER : tail
  3215  (2530)          INTEGER : room
  3216  (2531)  
  3217  (2532)          MAXINDEX(who, 1) + 1 =: total
  3218  (2533)  
  3219  (2534)          IF total <= widWho THEN
  3220  (2535)              FOR i IN 0:total - 1 DO
  3221  (2536)                  who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
  3222  (2537)              ENDFOR
  3223  (2538)              RETURN
  3224  (2539)          ENDIF
  3225  (2540)  
  3226  (2541)          % Where the machine starts, if there is one.
  3227  (2542)          0 - 1 =: bang
  3228  (2543)          FOR i IN 0:total - 1 DO
  3229  (2544)              IF bang < 0 THEN
  3230  (2545)                  IF who(i) = atByte(0) THEN
  3231  (2546)                      i =: bang
  3232  (2547)                  ENDIF
  3233  (2548)              ENDIF
  3234  (2549)          ENDFOR
  3235  (2550)  
  3236  (2551)          IF bang < 0 THEN
  3237  (2552)              % No machine in it. Cut the name.
  3238  (2553)              FOR i IN 0:widWho - 1 DO
  3239  (2554)                  who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
  3240  (2555)              ENDFOR
  3241  (2556)              RETURN
  3242  (2557)          ENDIF
  3243  (2558)  
  3244  (2559)          % "@D102" and everything after it is kept whole; the name gets what is
  3245  (2560)          % left. If the machine alone fills the field there is no name shown -
  3246  (2561)          % which machine still beats a single initial.
  3247  (2562)          total - bang =: tail
  3248  (2563)          widWho - tail =: room
  3249  (2564)          IF room < 0 THEN
  3250  (2565)              0 =: room
  3251  (2566)          ENDIF
  3252  (2567)  
  3253  (2568)          IF room > 0 THEN
  3254  (2569)              FOR i IN 0:room - 1 DO
  3255  (2570)                  who(i) =: lineBuf(offWho + i)
  3256  (2571)              ENDFOR
  3257  (2572)          ENDIF
  3258  (2573)          FOR i IN 0:tail - 1 DO
  3259  (2574)              IF room + i < widWho THEN
  3260  (2575)                  who(bang + i) =: lineBuf(offWho + room + i)
  3261  (2576)              ENDIF
  3262  (2577)          ENDFOR
  3263  (2578)      ENDROUTINE
  3264  (2579)  
  3265  (2580)      % THE CURRENT WINDOW'S COUNTERS GO BACK INTO ITS ROW.
  3266  (2581)      %
  3267  (2582)      % cacheCount, cacheFirst and viewFrom are the WORKING copies - every
  3268  (2583)      % routine that paints or adds reads those three names. Keeping them as
  3269  (2584)      % scalars is what let windows be added without touching any of that code.
  3270  (2585)      % The price is that they must be put back before another window is loaded,
  3271  (2586)      % and that is what these two routines are for. They are always used as a
  3272  (2587)      % pair; a save without a load leaves two windows sharing one ring.
  3273  (2588)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : winSave
  3274  (2589)          cacheCount =: winCount(curWin)
  3275  (2590)          cacheFirst =: winFirst(curWin)
  3276  (2591)          viewFrom   =: winView(curWin)
  3277  (2592)      ENDROUTINE
  3278  (2593)  
  3279  (2594)      % MAKE A WINDOW THE WORKING ONE. This does NOT clear its unread count -
  3280  (2595)      % it is used both by a person switching to a window and by the delivery
  3281  (2596)      % path stepping into one for long enough to drop a line in. Only the
  3282  (2597)      % first of those means "you have seen it", so the clearing lives in
  3283  (2598)      % winGo where the person actually is.
  3284  (2599)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : winUse(w)
  3285  (2600)          w =: curWin
  3286  (2601)          winCount(w) =: cacheCount
  3287  (2602)          winFirst(w) =: cacheFirst
  3288  (2603)          winView(w)  =: viewFrom
  3289  (2604)      ENDROUTINE
  3290  (2605)  
  3291  (2606)      % What the bar calls a window. Stored WITHOUT the leading = so it can be
  3292  (2607)      % compared with a name that arrives off the wire; the = is drawn.
  3293  (2608)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : winName(w, at, n)
  3294  (2609)          INTEGER : i
  3295  (2610)          INTEGER : take
  3296  (2611)  
  3297  (2612)          n =: take
  3298  (2613)          IF take > 16 THEN
  3299  (2614)              16 =: take
  3300  (2615)          ENDIF
  3301  (2616)          IF take < 0 THEN
  3302  (2617)              0 =: take
  3303  (2618)          ENDIF
  3304  (2619)          IF take > 0 THEN
  3305  (2620)              FOR i IN 1:take DO
  3306  (2621)                  inBuf(at + i - 1) =: winLabel(w * 16 + i - 1)
  3307  (2622)              ENDFOR
  3308  (2623)          ENDIF
  3309  (2624)          take =: winLabLen(w)
  3310  (2625)      ENDROUTINE
  3311  (2626)  
  3312  (2627)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  3313  (2628)      % The same message, but addressed to a WINDOW rather than to a name the
  3314  (2629)      % user typed.
  3315  (2630)      %
  3316  (2631)      % This is what makes a conversation window behave like a conversation: the
  3317  (2632)      % target comes from the window's own label, so a plain line typed there
  3318  (2633)      % goes to that person and nowhere else. buildTell above cannot serve this
  3319  (2634)      % because its target is a run of characters inside `typed`, and here the
  3320  (2635)      % whole of `typed` is the message.
  3321  (2636)      %
  3322  (2637)      % The wire shape is identical - "TARGET/message" - so a server that has
  3323  (2638)      % never heard of windows sees an ordinary direct message.
  3324  (2639)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  3325  (2640)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : buildTellTo(w, msgLen)
  3326  (2641)          INTEGER : i, at, tgtLen
  3327  (2642)  
  3328  (2643)          winLabLen(w) =: tgtLen
  3329  (2644)  
  3330  (2645)          kDirect =: outBuf(0)
  3331  (2646)          lenMyName =: outBuf(1)
  3332  (2647)          2 =: at
  3333  (2648)          FOR i IN 1:lenMyName DO
  3334  (2649)              myName(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  3335  (2650)              at + 1 =: at
  3336  (2651)          ENDFOR
  3337  (2652)  
  3338  (2653)          0 =: outBuf(at)
  3339  (2654)          tgtLen + 1 + msgLen =: outBuf(at + 1)
  3340  (2655)          at + 2 =: at
  3341  (2656)  
  3342  (2657)          FOR i IN 1:tgtLen DO
  3343  (2658)              winLabel(w * 16 + i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  3344  (2659)              at + 1 =: at
  3345  (2660)          ENDFOR
  3346  (2661)          slashByte(0) =: outBuf(at)
  3347  (2662)          at + 1 =: at
  3348  (2663)          FOR i IN 1:msgLen DO
  3349  (2664)              typed(i - 1) =: outBuf(at)
  3350  (2665)              at + 1 =: at
  3351  (2666)          ENDFOR
  3352  (2667)          at RETURN
  3353  (2668)      ENDROUTINE
  3354  (2669)  
  3355  (2670)      % EMPTY THE ROOM. Used when this client moves to another one.
  3356  (2671)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : cacheClear
  3357  (2672)          0 =: cacheCount
  3358  (2673)          0 =: cacheFirst
  3359  (2674)          0 =: viewFrom
  3360  (2675)      ENDROUTINE
  3361  (2676)  
  3362  (2677)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : cacheAdd
  3363  (2678)          INTEGER : slot
  3364  (2679)          INTEGER : i
  3365  (2680)          INTEGER : base
  3366  (2681)  
  3367  (2682)          % BOUNDED BY winLines, NOT cacheLines. cacheLines is the whole array
  3368  (2683)          % across every window; a window counting up to it would walk straight
  3369  (2684)          % through its neighbour's lines. PLANC checks no bound, so nothing
  3370  (2685)          % would say so - the other window's history would simply start
  3371  (2686)          % changing under it.
  3372  (2687)          IF cacheCount >= winLines THEN
  3373  (2688)              % Full: the oldest slot is reused and becomes the newest.
  3374  (2689)              cacheFirst =: slot
  3375  (2690)              cacheFirst + 1 =: cacheFirst
  3376  (2691)              IF cacheFirst >= winLines THEN
  3377  (2692)                  0 =: cacheFirst
  3378  (2693)              ENDIF
  3379  (2694)          ELSE
  3380  (2695)              cacheCount =: slot
  3381  (2696)              cacheCount + 1 =: cacheCount
  3382  (2697)          ENDIF
  3383  (2698)  
  3384  (2699)          % ONE MORE LINE HAS EXISTED IN THIS WINDOW, ever. This is what gives
  3385  (2700)          % the line a number the painter can compare a row against - see
  3386  (2701)          % rowShown. It counts lines, not messages, because a wrapped message
  3387  (2702)          % becomes several lines and each occupies a row of its own.
  3388  (2703)          winTotal(curWin) + 1 =: winTotal(curWin)
  3389  (2704)  
  3390  (2705)          % The slice this window owns starts here. The slot above is WITHIN the
  3391  (2706)          % window; this is the only place the two are added.
  3392  (2707)          ((curWin - 1) * winLines + slot) * lineWide =: base
  3393  (2708)          FOR i IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
  3394  (2709)              lineBuf(i) =: cache(base + i)
  3395  (2710)          ENDFOR
  3396  (2711)  
  3397  (2712)          % If the user is paged back, one more line has appeared BELOW them, so
  3398  (2713)          % the distance to the newest grows by one - otherwise the view would
  3399  (2714)          % drift forward a line every time somebody spoke.
  3400  (2715)          IF viewFrom > 0 THEN
  3401  (2716)              viewFrom + 1 =: viewFrom
  3402  (2717)              % ...but never past the oldest line we still hold.
  3403  (2718)              IF viewFrom > cacheCount - msgRows THEN
  3404  (2719)                  cacheCount - msgRows =: viewFrom
  3405  (2720)              ENDIF
  3406  (2721)              IF viewFrom < 0 THEN
  3407  (2722)                  0 =: viewFrom
  3408  (2723)              ENDIF
  3409  (2724)          ENDIF
  3410  (2725)      ENDROUTINE
  3411  (2726)  
  3412  (2727)      % Which cache slot holds the line at a given position, counted from the
  3413  (2728)      % OLDEST held line. The ring is unwound in ONE place, here.
  3414  (2729)      % ABSOLUTE, so the caller can index the array straight away. It wraps
  3415  (2730)      % WITHIN the window and only then adds the window's offset - the other way
  3416  (2731)      % round would wrap one window's last line onto the next window's first.
  3417  (2732)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : slotAt(index)
  3418  (2733)          INTEGER : s
  3419  (2734)          cacheFirst + index =: s
  3420  (2735)          IF s >= winLines THEN
  3421  (2736)              s - winLines =: s
  3422  (2737)          ENDIF
  3423  (2738)          (curWin - 1) * winLines + s RETURN
  3424  (2739)      ENDROUTINE
  3425  (2740)  
  3426  (2741)      % ---- THE ARRIVAL INSTRUMENT, behind a compile-time switch --------------
  3427  (2742)      %
  3428  (2743)      % Five numbers on the TITLE row, repainted on every arrival that was
  3429  (2744)      % shown: the kind, the read length, the first text byte OF THE ARRIVAL,
  3430  (2745)      % cacheCount, and the first text byte of the NEWEST cache row. One glance
  3431  (2746)      % separates "the arriving bytes are stale" (third number wrong) from "the
  3432  (2747)      % store went stale" (third right, fifth wrong) - which is exactly how the
  3433  (2748)      % repeat-first-message bug was pinned to the painter on 2026-09-01: it
  3434  (2749)      % read 005 015 097 003 097 while the screen showed the wrong line, so the
  3435  (2750)      % wire and the cache were right and only the paint was wrong.
  3436  (2751)      %
  3437  (2752)      % $CONSTANT DBGUI is PLANC's #ifdef (ND-60.117.5 appendix A 0.9/0.10):
  3438  (2753)      % set it to 0 and the instrument compiles out entirely - code, buffer and
  3439  (2754)      % call sites. It stays IN the source either way, by Ronny's decision.
  3440  (2755)  $CONSTANT DBGUI=0
  3441  (2756)  $IF DBGUI = 1 $THEN
  3442       .      BYTES : dbgRow(0:21)
  3443       .  
  3444       .      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : dbgPut3(at, v)
  3445       .          INTEGER : x
  3446       .          v =: x
  3447       .          IF x < 0 THEN
  3448       .              0 - x =: x
  3449       .          ENDIF
  3450       .          x - ((x / 1000) * 1000) =: x
  3451       .          digits(x / 100) =: dbgRow(at)
  3452       .          x - ((x / 100) * 100) =: x
  3453       .          digits(x / 10) =: dbgRow(at + 1)
  3454       .          digits(x - ((x / 10) * 10)) =: dbgRow(at + 2)
  3455       .      ENDROUTINE
  3456       .  
  3457       .      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : dbgShow(k, rl, t0)
  3458       .          INTEGER : i, base, c0
  3459       .          FOR i IN 0:21 DO
  3460       .              spaceByte(0) =: dbgRow(i)
  3461       .          ENDFOR
  3462       .          dbgPut3(0, k)
  3463       .          dbgPut3(4, rl)
  3464       .          dbgPut3(8, t0)
  3465       .          dbgPut3(12, cacheCount)
  3466       .          0 =: c0
  3467       .          IF cacheCount > 0 THEN
  3468       .              slotAt(cacheCount - 1) * lineWide + offText =: base
  3469       .              cache(base) =: c0
  3470       .          ENDIF
  3471       .          dbgPut3(16, c0)
  3472       .          IF screenOn THEN
  3473       .              bytdis(1, 56, 19, dbgRow(0:18), '')
  3474       .          ENDIF
  3475       .      ENDROUTINE
  3476       .  $ENDIF
  3477  (2792)  
  3478  (2793)      % =====================================================================
  3479  (2794)      % THE ROOM'S MEMBERS, and the one question they answer
  3480  (2795)      % =====================================================================
  3481  (2796)  
  3482  (2797)      % =====================================================================
  3483  (2798)      % ADDING A LINE, WITH WRAPPING
  3484  (2799)      %
  3485  (2800)      % 57 columns of text is narrow and a real sentence overruns it. A line that
  3486  (2801)      % is simply CUT loses what somebody said, silently, which is the worst of
  3487  (2802)      % the three options.
  3488  (2803)      %
  3489  (2804)      % So a long line becomes SEVERAL cache lines: the first carries the time and
  3490  (2805)      % the speaker, and every continuation carries neither. That is what makes a
  3491  (2806)      % wrapped line readable as one utterance rather than as two people talking -
  3492  (2807)      % the eye follows the empty speaker column down.
  3493  (2808)      %
  3494  (2809)      % Each continuation is a cache line in its OWN right, so paging and the ring
  3495  (2810)      % need no special case at all. The cost is that a very long message uses
  3496  (2811)      % several of the 300 slots, which is correct: they ARE several screen lines.
  3497  (2812)      % =====================================================================
  3498  (2813)  
  3499  (2814)      % Put one row into the cache: a time and speaker only on the first.
  3500  (2815)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, BYTES, BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
  3501  (2816)              pushRow(withHead, who, text, from, count)
  3502  (2817)          INTEGER : i
  3503  (2818)  
  3504  (2819)          blankLine
  3505  (2820)          IF withHead = 1 THEN
  3506  (2821)              % NOT WRITING IT IS HOW IT IS BLANKED - blankLine has already
  3507  (2822)              % cleared the row to spaces, so a time nobody recorded simply
  3508  (2823)              % leaves the column empty instead of asserting a moment.
  3509  (2824)              IF timeKnown THEN
  3510  (2825)                  put2(offTime, cal(3))
  3511  (2826)                  colonByte(0) =: lineBuf(offTime + 2)
  3512  (2827)                  put2(offTime + 3, cal(2))
  3513  (2828)              ENDIF
  3514  (2829)              putWho(who)
  3515  (2830)          ENDIF
  3516  (2831)  
  3517  (2832)          % The slice of the message this row carries. Copied byte by byte rather
  3518  (2833)          % than with a subarray, because the bounds are computed and a subarray
  3519  (2834)          % whose bounds fall outside the original breaks the compiler's own
  3520  (2835)          % index checking - appendix G item 22.
  3521  (2836)          IF count > 0 THEN
  3522  (2837)              FOR i IN 0:count - 1 DO
  3523  (2838)                  text(from + i) =: lineBuf(offText + i)
  3524  (2839)              ENDFOR
  3525  (2840)          ENDIF
  3526  (2841)          cacheAdd
  3527  (2842)      ENDROUTINE
  3528  (2843)  
  3529  (2844)      % Add what somebody said, wrapped across as many rows as it needs.
  3530  (2845)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, BYTES) : addSaid(who, text)
  3531  (2846)          INTEGER : total
  3532  (2847)          INTEGER : at
  3533  (2848)          INTEGER : take
  3534  (2849)          INTEGER : head
  3535  (2850)  
  3536  (2851)          % A REPLAYED LINE IS STAMPED WITH WHEN IT WAS SAID, not with now.
  3537  (2852)          % The two calendar elements pushRow reads are filled from the wire
  3538  (2853)          % instead of from the clock; everything downstream is unchanged.
  3539  (2854)          IF msgIsHist THEN
  3540  (2855)              histHour =: cal(3)
  3541  (2856)              histMin =: cal(2)
  3542  (2857)          ELSE
  3543  (2858)              MN113(cal)
  3544  (2859)          ENDIF
  3545  (2860)  
  3546  (2861)          % AN @ IN THE NAME IS THE SERVER SAYING "another machine", and that is
  3547  (2862)          % all the @trunk marker needs.
  3548  (2863)          FOR at IN 0:MAXINDEX(who, 1) DO
  3549  (2864)              IF who(at) = atByte(0) THEN
  3550  (2865)                  TRUE =: sawRemote
  3551  (2866)              ENDIF
  3552  (2867)          ENDFOR
  3553  (2868)  
  3554  (2869)          MAXINDEX(text, 1) + 1 =: total
  3555  (2870)          0 =: at
  3556  (2871)          1 =: head
  3557  (2872)  
  3558  (2873)          % A DO loop with the test inside it, because PLANC has no while-header.
  3559  (2874)          % The first pass runs even for an empty message, so a blank line still
  3560  (2875)          % appears with its time - which is what a user expects if somebody
  3561  (2876)          % presses RETURN on nothing.
  3562  (2877)          DO
  3563  (2878)              total - at =: take
  3564  (2879)              IF take > widText THEN
  3565  (2880)                  widText =: take
  3566  (2881)              ENDIF
  3567  (2882)              pushRow(head, who, text, at, take)
  3568  (2883)              0 =: head
  3569  (2884)              at + take =: at
  3570  (2885)              WHILE at < total
  3571  (2886)          ENDDO
  3572  (2887)      ENDROUTINE
  3573  (2888)  
  3574  (2889)      % =====================================================================
  3575  (2890)      % CLIPPING - because VTM will not do it for us
  3576  (2891)      %
  3577  (2892)      % The viewport clips a WRITE to the window it is in, but it knows nothing
  3578  (2893)      % about a window drawn ON TOP of that one. So overlap is ours to handle,
  3579  (2894)      % and there are exactly two honest ways to do it: paint only the parts
  3580  (2895)      % that are visible, or paint nothing at all.
  3581  (2896)      %
  3582  (2897)      % PAINTING NOTHING IS THE ONE THAT LOOKS SAFE AND IS WRONG. It was what
  3583  (2898)      % this file did - "IF NOT panelUp THEN paint" - and it freezes the whole
  3584  (2899)      % room to protect the few rows a centred window actually covers. TESTUI
  3585  (2900)      % made the same mistake first and Ronny reported it there in the same
  3586  (2901)      % words: the buffer updates and the screen does not.
  3587  (2902)      %
  3588  (2903)      % A cell-by-cell test is NOT expensive. Seventy-seven tests a row is
  3589  (2904)      % nothing on the CPU, and it SAVES time on the wire, which is the slow
  3590  (2905)      % part - only the visible runs are sent.
  3591  (2906)      % =====================================================================
  3592  (2907)  
  3593  (2908)      % Is one PHYSICAL cell visible, or is the panel over it?
  3594  (2909)      ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (INTEGER, INTEGER) : visibleAt(prow, pcol)
  3595  (2910)          IF panelUp THEN
  3596  (2911)              IF prow >= panRow AND prow <= panRow + panHigh - 1 THEN
  3597  (2912)                  IF pcol >= panCol AND pcol <= panCol + panWid - 1 THEN
  3598  (2913)                      FALSE RETURN
  3599  (2914)                  ENDIF
  3600  (2915)              ENDIF
  3601  (2916)          ENDIF
  3602  (2917)          TRUE RETURN
  3603  (2918)      ENDROUTINE
  3604  (2919)  
  3605  (2920)      % Does the panel cover any part of this PHYSICAL row?
  3606  (2921)      %
  3607  (2922)      % Deliberately coarser than visibleAt: it answers for the whole row, not a
  3608  (2923)      % cell. The one-row things - the status line, the bar, the input line - are
  3609  (2924)      % drawn by routines that lay out a whole row at once, so the useful
  3610  (2925)      % question about them is "leave it alone this time?" and not "which
  3611  (2926)      % columns?". Being conservative here costs one row on the close and cannot
  3612  (2927)      % draw over the panel.
  3613  (2928)      ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (INTEGER) : rowCovered(prow)
  3614  (2929)          IF NOT panelUp THEN
  3615  (2930)              FALSE RETURN
  3616  (2931)          ENDIF
  3617  (2932)          IF prow >= panRow AND prow <= panRow + panHigh - 1 THEN
  3618  (2933)              TRUE RETURN
  3619  (2934)          ENDIF
  3620  (2935)          FALSE RETURN
  3621  (2936)      ENDROUTINE
  3622  (2937)  
  3623  (2938)      % Paint ONE ROW of the message pane, showing only what is not covered.
  3624  (2939)      % Walks the row once and emits each visible run as it ends.
  3625  (2940)      %
  3626  (2941)      % localRow is the mid window's own row; the window starts at column 1, so a
  3627  (2942)      % window column IS a physical column and only the row needs converting.
  3628  (2943)      %
  3629  (2944)      % THE WIDTH IS PASSED TO bytdis, NEVER 0. Width 0 means "the string says
  3630  (2945)      % its own length", which is right for a literal and wrong for a subarray -
  3631  (2946)      % measured on D100, a subarray painted with 0 ran off the right-hand side
  3632  (2947)      % and wiped two window borders. planc-lint refuses the shape now.
  3633  (2948)      %
  3634  (2949)      % THE ROW COMES IN THROUGH paintBuf, NOT A PARAMETER - 2026-09-01, and
  3635  (2950)      % this closed the repeat-first-message bug. The caller used to pass
  3636  (2951)      % cache(base : base + lineWide - 1), a subarray of the 23KB cache with a
  3637  (2952)      % COMPUTED base - and every row painted line 0's bytes while the cache
  3638  (2953)      % itself, read back by an ordinary computed INDEX, held each line's own
  3639  (2954)      % text (measured on the title-row instrument: arrival byte 97 'a', cache
  3640  (2955)      % byte 97 'a', screen showing the first line). Same family as the
  3641  (2956)      % putField subarray that compiled clean and wrote nothing at all. The
  3642  (2957)      % caller now COPIES the row into paintBuf with an explicit indexed loop -
  3643  (2958)      % the one form of cache access that is proven right - and the subarrays
  3644  (2959)      % below are of a small fixed module array, the exact shape TESTUI's
  3645  (2960)      % paintSlot has proven on this machine.
  3646  (2961)      BYTES : paintBuf(0:76)
  3647  (2962)  
  3648  (2963)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : paintRow(localRow)
  3649  (2964)          INTEGER : c
  3650  (2965)          INTEGER : runFrom
  3651  (2966)          INTEGER : prow
  3652  (2967)          BOOLEAN : inRun
  3653  (2968)  
  3654  (2969)          midRow + localRow - 1 =: prow
  3655  (2970)          FALSE =: inRun
  3656  (2971)          0 =: runFrom
  3657  (2972)          FOR c IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
  3658  (2973)              IF visibleAt(prow, textCol + c) THEN
  3659  (2974)                  IF NOT inRun THEN
  3660  (2975)                      TRUE =: inRun
  3661  (2976)                      c =: runFrom
  3662  (2977)                  ENDIF
  3663  (2978)              ELSE
  3664  (2979)                  IF inRun THEN
  3665  (2980)                      bytdis(localRow, textCol + runFrom, c - runFrom, &
  3666  (2981)                             paintBuf(runFrom : c - 1), '')
  3667  (2982)                      FALSE =: inRun
  3668  (2983)                  ENDIF
  3669  (2984)              ENDIF
  3670  (2985)          ENDFOR
  3671  (2986)          % A run reaching the end of the row still has to be flushed - the loop
  3672  (2987)          % only emits one when it meets a covered cell.
  3673  (2988)          IF inRun THEN
  3674  (2989)              bytdis(localRow, textCol + runFrom, lineWide - runFrom, &
  3675  (2990)                     paintBuf(runFrom : lineWide - 1), '')
  3676  (2991)          ENDIF
  3677  (2992)      ENDROUTINE
  3678  (2993)  
  3679  (2994)  
  3680  (2995)      % =====================================================================
  3681  (2996)      % PAINTING THE MIDDLE SECTION
  3682  (2997)      % =====================================================================
  3683  (2998)  
  3684  (2999)      % Repaint the chat. This is what a page costs and it is the most
  3685  (3000)      % expensive routine here: 17 lines of 77 characters, about 1300, roughly
  3686  (3001)      % a second and a half on a real 9600-baud line.
  3687  (3002)      % WHICH LINE NUMBER BELONGS ON ROW `row` OF THE CURRENT WINDOW - or -1 if
  3688  (3003)      % the row has no line yet. Pulled out of paintChat on 2026-09-01 so this
  3689  (3004)      % arithmetic can be proved right WITHOUT a screen: it is pure integers
  3690  (3005)      % over cacheCount, winTotal, viewFrom and msgRows, and every one of those
  3691  (3006)      % is real state this same client already carries.
  3692  (3007)      %
  3693  (3008)      % ANSWERS AN ABSOLUTE LINE NUMBER, counted from the very first line this
  3694  (3009)      % window has ever held - the same number slotAt(index) expects once the
  3695  (3010)      % OLDEST-held offset is subtracted back out, which is what the two call
  3696  (3011)      % sites below already did inline.
  3697  (3012)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER) : wantForRow(row)
  3698  (3013)          INTEGER : first
  3699  (3014)          INTEGER : shown
  3700  (3015)          INTEGER : oldest
  3701  (3016)  
  3702  (3017)          cacheCount - msgRows - viewFrom =: first
  3703  (3018)          IF first < 0 THEN
  3704  (3019)              0 =: first
  3705  (3020)          ENDIF
  3706  (3021)  
  3707  (3022)          cacheCount - first =: shown
  3708  (3023)          IF shown > msgRows THEN
  3709  (3024)              msgRows =: shown
  3710  (3025)          ENDIF
  3711  (3026)  
  3712  (3027)          IF row >= shown THEN
  3713  (3028)              0 - 1 RETURN
  3714  (3029)          ENDIF
  3715  (3030)  
  3716  (3031)          winTotal(curWin) - cacheCount =: oldest
  3717  (3032)          oldest + first + row RETURN
  3718  (3033)      ENDROUTINE
  3719  (3034)  
  3720  (3035)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : paintChat
  3721  (3036)          INTEGER : i
  3722  (3037)          INTEGER : first
  3723  (3038)          INTEGER : idx
  3724  (3039)          INTEGER : base
  3725  (3040)          INTEGER : st
  3726  (3041)          INTEGER : want
  3727  (3042)  
  3728  (3043)          % NO BLANKET CLEAR ANY MORE.
  3729  (3044)          %
  3730  (3045)          % VTCREC over the whole pane blanked seventeen rows and then drew
  3731  (3046)          % seventeen back, every time one line arrived - which is both the cost
  3732  (3047)          % and the flicker. Every stored line is PADDED to the full width, so
  3733  (3048)          % writing one straight over another leaves nothing of the old one
  3734  (3049)          % behind and the erase was never needed. Rows with no line get an
  3735  (3050)          % explicit blank below, and only when they are not already blank.
  3736  (3051)          winEnter(midRow, 1, midHigh, scrWide)
  3737  (3052)  
  3738  (3053)          % Which held line goes on the top row. viewFrom counts BACK from the
  3739  (3054)          % newest, so at viewFrom = 0 the last msgRows lines are shown.
  3740  (3055)          %
  3741  (3056)          % KEPT HERE TOO, ALONGSIDE wantForRow's OWN COPY, only because
  3742  (3057)          % slotAt below wants the RELATIVE index and wantForRow hands back
  3743  (3058)          % the ABSOLUTE line number - the two callers of the same arithmetic
  3744  (3059)          % need different halves of it. wantForRow is the one that is
  3745  (3060)          % TESTED; this is the same three lines, unchanged, so the two never
  3746  (3061)          % have a reason to drift apart.
  3747  (3062)          cacheCount - msgRows - viewFrom =: first
  3748  (3063)          IF first < 0 THEN
  3749  (3064)              0 =: first
  3750  (3065)          ENDIF
  3751  (3066)  
  3752  (3067)          % THE SHADOW ONLY DESCRIBES ONE WINDOW. Line numbers are counted per
  3753  (3068)          % window, so a row holding line 12 of the room and a row holding line
  3754  (3069)          % 12 of a conversation would compare equal and the pane would keep
  3755  (3070)          % somebody else's text. Switching windows throws the shadow away.
  3756  (3071)          % INVALIDATE WITH A VALUE `want` CANNOT TAKE.
  3757  (3072)          %
  3758  (3073)          % This was minus one, and minus one is a REAL value - it is what want
  3759  (3074)          % holds for a row with no line on it. So every blank row compared
  3760  (3075)          % EQUAL to its shadow and was skipped, and switching windows left the
  3761  (3076)          % previous window's text on screen beneath the new window's first
  3762  (3077)          % line. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26; it looked like two windows
  3763  (3078)          % sharing one buffer, which is a far more alarming thing than it was.
  3764  (3079)          %
  3765  (3080)          % Minus two is never a row number and never means blank, so nothing
  3766  (3081)          % can match it.
  3767  (3082)          IF shadowWin >< curWin THEN
  3768  (3083)              FOR i IN 0:msgRows - 1 DO
  3769  (3084)                  0 - 2 =: rowShown(i)
  3770  (3085)              ENDFOR
  3771  (3086)              curWin =: shadowWin
  3772  (3087)          ENDIF
  3773  (3088)  
  3774  (3089)          FOR i IN 0:msgRows - 1 DO
  3775  (3090)              wantForRow(i) =: want
  3776  (3091)  
  3777  (3092)              % ALREADY RIGHT? THEN LEAVE IT ALONE. This is the whole point: a
  3778  (3093)              % pane that is still filling changes ONE row per arriving line, and
  3779  (3094)              % a pane where nothing has moved changes none.
  3780  (3095)              IF rowShown(i) >< want THEN
  3781  (3096)                  % THROUGH paintRow, WHICH CLIPS. A panel over part of this row
  3782  (3097)                  % gets the covered columns skipped and the rest painted, so a
  3783  (3098)                  % line arriving while /help is open still scrolls the room
  3784  (3099)                  % underneath it. The width goes to bytdis inside paintRow.
  3785  (3100)                  %
  3786  (3101)                  % THE ROW IS COPIED INTO paintBuf BY INDEX, never handed over
  3787  (3102)                  % as a computed-bounds subarray of cache - that subarray form
  3788  (3103)                  % painted line 0's bytes on every row while the cache held
  3789  (3104)                  % each line's own text. See the note above paintRow.
  3790  (3105)                  IF want < 0 THEN
  3791  (3106)                      FOR base IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
  3792  (3107)                          blankRow(base) =: paintBuf(base)
  3793  (3108)                      ENDFOR
  3794  (3109)                  ELSE
  3795  (3110)                      slotAt(first + i) =: idx
  3796  (3111)                      idx * lineWide =: base
  3797  (3112)                      FOR idx IN 0:lineWide - 1 DO
  3798  (3113)                          cache(base + idx) =: paintBuf(idx)
  3799  (3114)                      ENDFOR
  3800  (3115)                  ENDIF
  3801  (3116)                  paintRow(firstMsgRow + i)
  3802  (3117)  
  3803  (3118)                  % THE SHADOW RECORDS THE LINE, NOT WHAT REACHED THE GLASS.
  3804  (3119)                  % A row that was partly covered is remembered as painted, which
  3805  (3120)                  % is right while the panel is up - and is exactly why
  3806  (3121)                  % closePanel must forget the rows the panel covered before it
  3807  (3122)                  % repaints. That is what makes the close cheap: only those rows
  3808  (3123)                  % are stale, so only those are drawn again.
  3809  (3124)                  want =: rowShown(i)
  3810  (3125)              ENDIF
  3811  (3126)          ENDFOR
  3812  (3127)          winLeave
  3813  (3128)  
  3814  (3129)          % SHOW IT. VTM holds its output in a buffer and a terminal READ is what
  3815  (3130)          % normally empties it, which is why every screen program that redraws
  3816  (3131)          % and then waits for a key appears to work without ever asking.
  3817  (3132)          %
  3818  (3133)          % This client does NOT wait for a key - it polls - so a repaint driven
  3819  (3134)          % by an arriving message has no read behind it and would sit in the
  3820  (3135)          % buffer. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26: a line said in a freshly joined
  3821  (3136)          % room stayed invisible until any keystroke, which reads exactly like a
  3822  (3137)          % lost message and is nothing of the kind.
  3823  (3138)          %
  3824  (3139)          % The callers flush too. Flushing an empty buffer costs nothing, and a
  3825  (3140)          % painter that leaves its work invisible is the trap this file has now
  3826  (3141)          % paid for twice.
  3827  (3142)          VTWBUF =: st
  3828  (3143)      ENDROUTINE
  3829  (3144)  
  3830  (3145)      % =====================================================================
  3831  (3146)      % THE TOP SECTION
  3832  (3147)      % =====================================================================
  3833  (3148)  
  3834  (3149)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawStatus
  3835  (3150)          INTEGER : st
  3836  (3151)          INTEGER : at
  3837  (3152)          INTEGER : i
  3838  (3153)          INTEGER : nowView
  3839  (3154)          INTEGER : nowRemote
  3840  (3155)          INTEGER : same
  3841  (3156)          INTEGER : take        % how much of the topic fits in the room field
  3842  (3157)  
  3843  (3158)          MN113(cal)
  3844  (3159)  
  3845  (3160)          % NOTHING ON IT CHANGED? THEN DO NOT PAINT IT.
  3846  (3161)          %
  3847  (3162)          % Called on every arriving message, and its content moves only when
  3848  (3163)          % the clock ticks a minute, the count changes, the room changes or
  3849  (3164)          % scrollback is entered or left. The rest of the time this put back
  3850  (3165)          % exactly what was already there - eighty characters a message.
  3851  (3166)          %
  3852  (3167)          % The clock is compared to the MINUTE because that is all it shows.
  3853  (3168)          % Comparing seconds would make it repaint every second for a display
  3854  (3169)          % that cannot show the difference.
  3855  (3170)          0 =: nowView
  3856  (3171)          IF viewFrom > 0 THEN
  3857  (3172)              1 =: nowView
  3858  (3173)          ENDIF
  3859  (3174)          0 =: nowRemote
  3860  (3175)          IF sawRemote THEN
  3861  (3176)              1 =: nowRemote
  3862  (3177)          ENDIF
  3863  (3178)  
  3864  (3179)          1 =: same
  3865  (3180)          IF cal(2) >< lastMin THEN
  3866  (3181)              0 =: same
  3867  (3182)          ENDIF
  3868  (3183)          IF cal(3) >< lastHour THEN
  3869  (3184)              0 =: same
  3870  (3185)          ENDIF
  3871  (3186)          IF hereCount >< lastCount THEN
  3872  (3187)              0 =: same
  3873  (3188)          ENDIF
  3874  (3189)          IF nowView >< lastView THEN
  3875  (3190)              0 =: same
  3876  (3191)          ENDIF
  3877  (3192)          IF nowRemote >< lastRemote THEN
  3878  (3193)              0 =: same
  3879  (3194)          ENDIF
  3880  (3195)          IF lenRoomName >< lastRoomLen THEN
  3881  (3196)              0 =: same
  3882  (3197)          ENDIF
  3883  (3198)          IF lenTopic >< lastTopicL THEN
  3884  (3199)              0 =: same
  3885  (3200)          ENDIF
  3886  (3201)          IF same >< 0 THEN
  3887  (3202)              % The NAME as well as its length - two rooms can be the same
  3888  (3203)              % length, and a switch between them would otherwise leave the old
  3889  (3204)              % name on the line.
  3890  (3205)              IF lenRoomName > 0 THEN
  3891  (3206)                  FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
  3892  (3207)                      IF i <= 20 THEN
  3893  (3208)                          IF roomName(i - 1) >< lastRoom(i - 1) THEN
  3894  (3209)                              0 =: same
  3895  (3210)                          ENDIF
  3896  (3211)                      ENDIF
  3897  (3212)                  ENDFOR
  3898  (3213)              ENDIF
  3899  (3214)          ENDIF
  3900  (3215)  
  3901  (3216)          IF same >< 0 THEN
  3902  (3217)              RETURN
  3903  (3218)          ENDIF
  3904  (3219)  
  3905  (3220)          cal(2) =: lastMin
  3906  (3221)          cal(3) =: lastHour
  3907  (3222)          hereCount =: lastCount
  3908  (3223)          nowView =: lastView
  3909  (3224)          nowRemote =: lastRemote
  3910  (3225)          lenRoomName =: lastRoomLen
  3911  (3226)          lenTopic =: lastTopicL
  3912  (3227)          IF lenRoomName > 0 THEN
  3913  (3228)              FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
  3914  (3229)                  IF i <= 20 THEN
  3915  (3230)                      roomName(i - 1) =: lastRoom(i - 1)
  3916  (3231)                  ENDIF
  3917  (3232)              ENDFOR
  3918  (3233)          ENDIF
  3919  (3234)  
  3920  (3235)          winEnter(topRow, 1, topHigh, scrWide)
  3921  (3236)          VTCREC(2, 2, 2, scrWide - 1) =: st
  3922  (3237)  
  3923  (3238)          % ROW 2, laid out for the WORST CASE rather than for this room's name.
  3924  (3239)          % A room name may be sixteen characters and '@trunk' is six more, so
  3925  (3240)          % the name field can reach column 24 - which is why the count no longer
  3926  (3241)          % sits at 19. A layout measured against one example name is a layout
  3927  (3242)          % that breaks on the next one.
  3928  (3243)          %
  3929  (3244)          %   col  3..24   room name, and '@trunk' if it reaches another machine
  3930  (3245)          %   col 26..29   how many are here
  3931  (3246)          %   col 31..34   'here'
  3932  (3247)          %   col 37..58   the hint, OR the scrollback warning - never both
  3933  (3248)          %   col 64..74   the clock
  3934  (3249)          % THE WIDTH IS PASSED, NOT LEFT AT 0. Width 0 means "the string says its
  3935  (3250)          % own length", which is right for a LITERAL. For a SUBARRAY the two
  3936  (3251)          % measurements we have DISAGREE, and neither has been explained:
  3937  (3252)          % TESTUI's paintRun painted a subarray of a 700-byte buffer with 0 and
  3938  (3253)          % the line ran off the right-hand side, wiping two window borders; this
  3939  (3254)          % call paints roomName(0:lenRoomName-1) with 0 and the live screen shows
  3940  (3255)          % exactly five characters for LOBBY, which is correct. The difference may
  3941  (3256)          % be that this subarray starts at index 0 and TESTUI's at a computed
  3942  (3257)          % offset - UNVERIFIED, and not worth a build cycle to find out, because
  3943  (3258)          % passing lenRoomName is what width 0 is SUPPOSED to mean here. So it is
  3944  (3259)          % a no-op if 0 behaves and a fix if it does not.
  3945  (3260)          bytdis(2, 3, lenRoomName, roomName(0:lenRoomName - 1), '')
  3946  (3261)          % THE MARKER IS APPENDED TO THE NAME with no gap, as the design shows,
  3947  (3262)          % so it reads as part of the room's identity and not as another field.
  3948  (3263)          3 + lenRoomName =: at
  3949  (3264)          IF sawRemote THEN
  3950  (3265)              bytdis(2, at, 0, '@trunk', '')
  3951  (3266)              at + 6 =: at
  3952  (3267)          ENDIF
  3953  (3268)  
  3954  (3269)          % THE TOPIC, AS "LOBBY #the topic".
  3955  (3270)          %
  3956  (3271)          % It reads as part of the room's identity like '@trunk' does, which is
  3957  (3272)          % what Ronny asked for: the header said LOBBY and setting a topic
  3958  (3273)          % changed nothing.
  3959  (3274)          %
  3960  (3275)          % CLAMPED TO THE FIELD, WHICH IS NARROW. The count starts at column 26
  3961  (3276)          % and the room field must stop at 24 to leave a gap, so what is left for
  3962  (3277)          % a topic is 24 minus wherever the name ended, minus the two characters
  3963  (3278)          % of ' #'. With a five-character room name and no trunk marker that is
  3964  (3279)          % about fourteen characters; with '@trunk' up there too it is about
  3965  (3280)          % eight, and a long room name can leave none at all - in which case
  3966  (3281)          % nothing is drawn rather than something ragged running into the count.
  3967  (3282)          %
  3968  (3283)          % THE FULL TOPIC IS STILL ONE COMMAND AWAY - /topic prints it in a
  3969  (3284)          % window with no width problem. This line is a reminder, not the
  3970  (3285)          % record. Widening it means moving the count, the 'here' and the hint,
  3971  (3286)          % which is a layout decision and not this fix.
  3972  (3287)          IF lenTopic > 0 THEN
  3973  (3288)              24 - at - 1 =: take
  3974  (3289)              IF take > lenTopic THEN
  3975  (3290)                  lenTopic =: take
  3976  (3291)              ENDIF
  3977  (3292)              IF take > 0 THEN
  3978  (3293)                  bytdis(2, at, 0, ' #', '')
  3979  (3294)                  bytdis(2, at + 2, take, curTopic(0 : take - 1), '')
  3980  (3295)              ENDIF
  3981  (3296)          ENDIF
  3982  (3297)          intdis(2, 26, 4, hereCount, '')
  3983  (3298)          bytdis(2, 31, 0, 'here', '')
  3984  (3299)  
  3985  (3300)          % The clock, into lineBuf because put2 works there.
  3986  (3301)          blankLine
  3987  (3302)          put2(0, cal(4))                    % day
  3988  (3303)          spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(2)
  3989  (3304)          put2(3, cal(5))                    % month
  3990  (3305)          spaceByte(0) =: lineBuf(5)
  3991  (3306)          put2(6, cal(3))                    % hour
  3992  (3307)          colonByte(0) =: lineBuf(8)
  3993  (3308)          put2(9, cal(2))                    % minute
  3994  (3309)          bytdis(2, 64, 11, lineBuf(0:10), '')
  3995  (3310)  
  3996  (3311)          % PAGED BACK? Say so, because the arrival rule above means the screen
  3997  (3312)          % deliberately does not follow new lines while the user is reading.
  3998  (3313)          % Without this the client looks stuck.
  3999  (3314)          % THE HINT AND THE WARNING SHARE ONE SLOT, and that is deliberate.
  4000  (3315)          %
  4001  (3316)          % They used to sit side by side and the warning printed straight
  4002  (3317)          % THROUGH the hint - 'Use /help to lSCROLLBACKds' - which was patched
  4003  (3318)          % by moving it along until it fitted. Two things that are never both
  4004  (3319)          % worth reading should not both be drawn: the hint matters when nothing
  4005  (3320)          % is happening, and the moment the user has paged back, where they are
  4006  (3321)          % matters more than where the help is.
  4007  (3322)          IF viewFrom > 0 THEN
  4008  (3323)              bytdis(2, 37, 0, 'SCROLLBACK - PAGE DOWN ', '')
  4009  (3324)          ELSE
  4010  (3325)              bytdis(2, 37, 0, '/help for commands     ', '')
  4011  (3326)          ENDIF
  4012  (3327)          winLeave
  4013  (3328)      ENDROUTINE
  4014  (3329)  
  4015  (3330)      % =====================================================================
  4016  (3331)      % THE BOTTOM SECTION
  4017  (3332)      % =====================================================================
  4018  (3333)  
  4019  (3334)      % Repaint the input line and leave the cursor at the end of it.
  4020  (3335)      %
  4021  (3336)      % THE CURSOR BELONGS HERE. In TESTUI it is parked somewhere harmless
  4022  (3337)      % because stray echoes landed inside windows; here there IS a right place
  4023  (3338)      % for it, so every repaint of anything must finish by calling this.
  4024  (3339)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawInput
  4025  (3340)          INTEGER : st
  4026  (3341)  
  4027  (3342)          winEnter(botRow, 1, botHigh, scrWide)
  4028  (3343)  
  4029  (3344)          % THE CURSOR IS PUT BACK WHATEVER HAPPENS, because SINTRAN echoes a
  4030  (3345)          % typed character wherever the cursor was left and every repaint above
  4031  (3346)          % moves it. That is a few bytes.
  4032  (3347)          %
  4033  (3348)          % THE ROW ITSELF IS ONLY REDRAWN WHEN IT CHANGED. It changes when
  4034  (3349)          % somebody types, and not when a message arrives - and it was being
  4035  (3350)          % cleared and rewritten on every single arrival.
  4036  (3351)          IF inputDirty THEN
  4037  (3352)              VTCREC(inpRow, 2, inpRow, scrWide - 1) =: st
  4038  (3353)              bytdis(inpRow, 2, 0, '>', '')
  4039  (3354)              IF typedLen > 0 THEN
  4040  (3355)                  bytdis(inpRow, inpCol, typedLen, typed(0:typedLen - 1), '')
  4041  (3356)              ENDIF
  4042  (3357)              FALSE =: inputDirty
  4043  (3358)          ENDIF
  4044  (3359)  
  4045  (3360)          VTPCUR(inpRow, inpCol + typedLen) =: st
  4046  (3361)          winLeave
  4047  (3362)      ENDROUTINE
  4048  (3363)  
  4049  (3364)      % THE WINDOW BAR - every window that exists, and which one you are in.
  4050  (3365)      %
  4051  (3366)      % It once held a literal lifted straight out of the mockup: five room
  4052  (3367)      % names and two people who did not exist. That was worse than empty,
  4053  (3368)      % because a screen full of invented state looks finished when it is not.
  4054  (3369)      % Everything on it now is read from the window table.
  4055  (3370)      %
  4056  (3371)      % Window 1 is the room. Window 2 upward are conversations and are drawn
  4057  (3372)      % with a leading = , which is what tells a person called LOBBY apart from
  4058  (3373)      % a room called LOBBY at a glance.
  4059  (3374)      %
  4060  (3375)      % THE CURRENT ONE IS IN BRACKETS rather than marked with a colour or a
  4061  (3376)      % highlight. This terminal has attributes, but a bracket survives a
  4062  (3377)      % screen-grab, a printer and a terminal that ignores them - and the whole
  4063  (3378)      % bar has to stay readable on the worst terminal in the room.
  4064  (3379)      %
  4065  (3380)      %   [1 LOBBY]  2 =KARI 3*  4 =OLAV 12
  4066  (3381)      %
  4067  (3382)      % A number after a window is UNREAD LINES. A * means your own nick was
  4068  (3383)      % said in it, which is the one thing worth interrupting for, so it is not
  4069  (3384)      % folded into the count - a person scanning the bar looks for the star
  4070  (3385)      % first and reads the numbers second.
  4071  (3386)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawBar
  4072  (3387)          INTEGER : at
  4073  (3388)          INTEGER : w
  4074  (3389)          INTEGER : i
  4075  (3390)          INTEGER : st
  4076  (3391)  
  4077  (3392)          winEnter(botRow, 1, botHigh, scrWide)
  4078  (3393)          VTCREC(3, 2, 3, scrWide - 1) =: st
  4079  (3394)  
  4080  (3395)          blankLine
  4081  (3396)          0 =: at
  4082  (3397)          FOR w IN 1:maxWins DO
  4083  (3398)              IF winUsed(w) >< 0 THEN
  4084  (3399)  
  4085  (3400)                  % CLAMPED AGAINST THE LINE BUFFER, not against a guess at how
  4086  (3401)                  % many windows fit. Four windows with sixteen-character names
  4087  (3402)                  % and four-digit counts would overrun 77 columns, and PLANC
  4088  (3403)                  % checks no bound - the write would simply carry on into
  4089  (3404)                  % whatever follows lineBuf.
  4090  (3405)                  IF at < lineWide - 24 THEN
  4091  (3406)                      IF w = curWin THEN
  4092  (3407)                          at + putField(at, 1, '[') =: at
  4093  (3408)                      ELSE
  4094  (3409)                          IF at > 0 THEN
  4095  (3410)                              at + putField(at, 1, ' ') =: at
  4096  (3411)                          ENDIF
  4097  (3412)                      ENDIF
  4098  (3413)  
  4099  (3414)                      digits(w) =: lineBuf(at)
  4100  (3415)                      at + 1 =: at
  4101  (3416)                      at + putField(at, 1, ' ') =: at
  4102  (3417)  
  4103  (3418)                      IF w = 1 THEN
  4104  (3419)                          IF joined THEN
  4105  (3420)                              at + putField(at, lenRoomName, &
  4106  (3421)                                  roomName(0:lenRoomName - 1)) =: at
  4107  (3422)                          ELSE
  4108  (3423)                              % NOT IN A ROOM IS A REAL STATE and it says so,
  4109  (3424)                              % rather than leaving a bare number with nothing
  4110  (3425)                              % after it.
  4111  (3426)                              at + putField(at, 11, 'no room yet') =: at
  4112  (3427)                          ENDIF
  4113  (3428)                      ELSE
  4114  (3429)                          at + putField(at, 1, '=') =: at
  4115  (3430)  
  4116  (3431)                          % COPIED BYTE BY BYTE RATHER THAN THROUGH putField.
  4117  (3432)                          %
  4118  (3433)                          % The putField form was
  4119  (3434)                          %     at + putField(at, winLabLen(w), &
  4120  (3435)                          %         winLabel(w * 16 : w * 16 + winLabLen(w) - 1)) &
  4121  (3436)                          %         =: at
  4122  (3437)                          % and it printed NOTHING while the label was plainly
  4123  (3438)                          % there - a temporary diagnostic on this very line
  4124  (3439)                          % showed winLabLen reading 9 for a stored "FJELL!BOB".
  4125  (3440)                          % Three readings of the source did not explain it; the
  4126  (3441)                          % measurement took one build.
  4127  (3442)                          %
  4128  (3443)                          % A subarray whose bounds are both computed from an
  4129  (3444)                          % expression is the one thing that call did which
  4130  (3445)                          % nothing else in this file does, and an explicit loop
  4131  (3446)                          % removes the question entirely. It is also no slower:
  4132  (3447)                          % putField copies a byte at a time too.
  4133  (3448)                          IF winLabLen(w) > 0 THEN
  4134  (3449)                              FOR i IN 1:winLabLen(w) DO
  4135  (3450)                                  winLabel(w * 16 + i - 1) =: lineBuf(at)
  4136  (3451)                                  at + 1 =: at
  4137  (3452)                              ENDFOR
  4138  (3453)                          ENDIF
  4139  (3454)                      ENDIF
  4140  (3455)  
  4141  (3456)                      % THE STAR FIRST, THEN THE COUNT - see the note above.
  4142  (3457)                      IF winBell(w) >< 0 THEN
  4143  (3458)                          at + putField(at, 1, '*') =: at
  4144  (3459)                      ENDIF
  4145  (3460)                      IF winUnread(w) > 0 THEN
  4146  (3461)                          at + putField(at, 1, ' ') =: at
  4147  (3462)                          at + putNum(at, winUnread(w)) =: at
  4148  (3463)                      ENDIF
  4149  (3464)  
  4150  (3465)                      IF w = curWin THEN
  4151  (3466)                          at + putField(at, 1, ']') =: at
  4152  (3467)                      ENDIF
  4153  (3468)                  ENDIF
  4154  (3469)              ENDIF
  4155  (3470)          ENDFOR
  4156  (3471)  
  4157  (3472)          IF at > 0 THEN
  4158  (3473)              bytdis(3, 3, at, lineBuf(0:at - 1), '')
  4159  (3474)          ENDIF
  4160  (3475)          winLeave
  4161  (3476)      ENDROUTINE
  4162  (3477)  
  4163  (3478)      % =====================================================================
  4164  (3479)      % THE WHOLE SCREEN - drawn ONCE
  4165  (3480)      % =====================================================================
  4166  (3481)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawAll
  4167  (3482)          INTEGER : at
  4168  (3483)          INTEGER : col
  4169  (3484)  
  4170  (3485)          % ONE BOX ROUND THE WHOLE SCREEN, then a rule across it for each
  4171  (3486)          % separator.
  4172  (3487)          %
  4173  (3488)          % THREE STACKED FRAMES WAS THE FIRST ATTEMPT AND IT LOOKED WRONG.
  4174  (3489)          % frame() draws a box, so where two of them touch you get the TOP
  4175  (3490)          % CORNERS of the lower box - a fresh box top sitting inside the
  4176  (3491)          % outer one - where the design wants a cross-piece. PLANC-SCREEN-H
  4177  (3492)          % has no T-junction verb, so the corners cannot be had that way.
  4178  (3493)          %
  4179  (3494)          % fullbar is the library's own horizontal rule and is what the
  4180  (3495)          % separators are for.
  4181  (3496)          winEnter(1, 1, 24, scrWide)
  4182  (3497)          frame(1, 1, 24, scrWide, 'SPACE-FILL')
  4183  (3498)          fullbar(midRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
  4184  (3499)          fullbar(botRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
  4185  (3500)          winLeave
  4186  (3501)  
  4187  (3502)          % The title is PAINTED OVER the top frame's own edge, which is what
  4188  (3503)          % the mockup shows. frame's fifth argument is ATTRIBUTES, not a title.
  4189  (3504)          bytdis(1, 4, 0, ' NDCHAT 2.1 ', '')
  4190  (3505)          % NAME@MACHINE - and the machine is the one the SERVER named in the
  4191  (3506)          % welcome, never one this client made up.
  4192  (3507)          %
  4193  (3508)          % CHATUI drew ARNE@D100 from two literals. This draws ARNE@FJELL, or
  4194  (3509)          % ARNE@D100 on a machine that has not been given a name, or ARNE alone
  4195  (3510)          % if the server never said - which is what an older server does, and
  4196  (3511)          % showing the name alone is the honest answer to not being told.
  4197  (3512)          blankLine
  4198  (3513)          0 =: at
  4199  (3514)          at + putField(at, widWho, myName(0:lenMyName - 1)) =: at
  4200  (3515)          IF lenMyMach > 0 THEN
  4201  (3516)              atByte(0) =: lineBuf(at)
  4202  (3517)              at + 1 =: at
  4203  (3518)              at + putField(at, 16, myMachine(0:lenMyMach - 1)) =: at
  4204  (3519)          ENDIF
  4205  (3520)          % RIGHT-ALIGNED so it always ENDS at column 77, just inside the frame.
  4206  (3521)          % A fixed start column was fine when this was the literal ARNE@D100 and
  4207  (3522)          % nine characters wide. A machine may now be called anything up to
  4208  (3523)          % sixteen, so a fixed start would run the corner straight through the
  4209  (3524)          % right-hand border on the first long name somebody chose.
  4210  (3525)          78 - at =: col
  4211  (3526)          IF col < 20 THEN
  4212  (3527)              20 =: col
  4213  (3528)          ENDIF
  4214  (3529)          bytdis(1, col, at, lineBuf(0:at - 1), '')
  4215  (3530)  
  4216  (3531)          % A FULL REPAINT STARTS FROM NOTHING KNOWN. drawAll is only ever
  4217  (3532)          % called when the whole screen has to be rebuilt, so anything the
  4218  (3533)          % shadow believed about the pane is stale by definition.
  4219  (3534)          0 =: shadowWin
  4220  (3535)          TRUE =: inputDirty
  4221  (3536)  
  4222  (3537)          % AND NOTHING IS ON TOP ANY MORE. drawAll has just painted over the
  4223  (3538)          % whole screen, panel included, so leaving panelUp set would say a
  4224  (3539)          % window is up that the user cannot see: the next keypress would be
  4225  (3540)          % swallowed "closing" it, and closePanel would then repaint a rectangle
  4226  (3541)          % that already holds the room. Two paths reach here with a panel
  4227  (3542)          % possibly open - the welcome, which redraws the frame once a session
  4228  (3543)          % when the machine's name arrives, and a room change.
  4229  (3544)          FALSE =: panelUp
  4230  (3545)  
  4231  (3546)          % And the status line has just had its row wiped by the frame, so it
  4232  (3547)          % must paint whatever it last showed.
  4233  (3548)          0 - 1 =: lastMin
  4234  (3549)  
  4235  (3550)          drawStatus
  4236  (3551)          paintChat
  4237  (3552)          drawBar
  4238  (3553)          drawInput
  4239  (3554)      ENDROUTINE
  4240  (3555)  
  4241  (3556)      % =====================================================================
  4242  (3557)      % WHICH WINDOW BELONGS TO A PERSON
  4243  (3558)      %
  4244  (3559)      % Answers an existing one if there is one, opens the lowest free one if
  4245  (3560)      % not, and answers ZERO when every window is taken. Zero is a real answer
  4246  (3561)      % and the callers deal with it by putting the line in the room instead -
  4247  (3562)      % losing a private message because the bar was full would be the worst
  4248  (3563)      % possible way to run out of a resource.
  4249  (3564)      %
  4250  (3565)      % THE COMPARISON IS EXACT, including case. Every name in this system comes
  4251  (3566)      % from the server, which has already settled what a name looks like; a
  4252  (3567)      % client that matched loosely would open a second window for the same
  4253  (3568)      % person the first time somebody typed a name differently.
  4254  (3569)      % =====================================================================
  4255  (3570)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : winFor(at, n)
  4256  (3571)          INTEGER : w
  4257  (3572)          INTEGER : i
  4258  (3573)          INTEGER : same
  4259  (3574)          INTEGER : take
  4260  (3575)  
  4261  (3576)          n =: take
  4262  (3577)          IF take > 16 THEN
  4263  (3578)              16 =: take
  4264  (3579)          ENDIF
  4265  (3580)          IF take < 1 THEN
  4266  (3581)              0 RETURN
  4267  (3582)          ENDIF
  4268  (3583)  
  4269  (3584)          % An existing conversation. Window 1 is the room and is never a match.
  4270  (3585)          FOR w IN 2:maxWins DO
  4271  (3586)              IF winUsed(w) >< 0 THEN
  4272  (3587)                  IF winLabLen(w) = take THEN
  4273  (3588)                      1 =: same
  4274  (3589)                      FOR i IN 1:take DO
  4275  (3590)                          IF winLabel(w * 16 + i - 1) >< inBuf(at + i - 1) THEN
  4276  (3591)                              0 =: same
  4277  (3592)                          ENDIF
  4278  (3593)                      ENDFOR
  4279  (3594)                      IF same >< 0 THEN
  4280  (3595)                          w RETURN
  4281  (3596)                      ENDIF
  4282  (3597)                  ENDIF
  4283  (3598)              ENDIF
  4284  (3599)          ENDFOR
  4285  (3600)  
  4286  (3601)          % A new one. Opened EMPTY rather than seeded with anything: the line
  4287  (3602)          % that caused it to open is added by the caller, in the ordinary way,
  4288  (3603)          % so there is only one path that ever puts a line in a window.
  4289  (3604)          FOR w IN 2:maxWins DO
  4290  (3605)              IF winUsed(w) = 0 THEN
  4291  (3606)                  1 =: winUsed(w)
  4292  (3607)                  0 =: winCount(w)
  4293  (3608)                  0 =: winFirst(w)
  4294  (3609)                  0 =: winView(w)
  4295  (3610)                  0 =: winUnread(w)
  4296  (3611)                  0 =: winBell(w)
  4297  (3612)                  winName(w, at, take)
  4298  (3613)                  w RETURN
  4299  (3614)              ENDIF
  4300  (3615)          ENDFOR
  4301  (3616)  
  4302  (3617)          0 RETURN
  4303  (3618)      ENDROUTINE
  4304  (3619)  
  4305  (3620)      % =====================================================================
  4306  (3621)      % SWITCHING WINDOWS
  4307  (3622)      %
  4308  (3623)      % The whole screen is repainted, not just the middle. The status line
  4309  (3624)      % carries the room name and the bar carries the brackets, so painting only
  4310  (3625)      % the middle would leave both of them describing the window you just left -
  4311  (3626)      % and a header that disagrees with the text under it is worse than a slow
  4312  (3627)      % repaint.
  4313  (3628)      %
  4314  (3629)      % A WINDOW THAT DOES NOT EXIST IS NOT AN ERROR. Typing /w 3 with three
  4315  (3630)      % windows open is a normal thing to do while the third is being closed by
  4316  (3631)      % somebody else, and refusing it loudly would put a notice in the room
  4317  (3632)      % over a keystroke nobody meant anything by.
  4318  (3633)      % =====================================================================
  4319  (3634)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : winGo(w)
  4320  (3635)          IF w < 1 THEN
  4321  (3636)              RETURN
  4322  (3637)          ENDIF
  4323  (3638)          IF w > maxWins THEN
  4324  (3639)              RETURN
  4325  (3640)          ENDIF
  4326  (3641)          IF winUsed(w) = 0 THEN
  4327  (3642)              RETURN
  4328  (3643)          ENDIF
  4329  (3644)          IF w = curWin THEN
  4330  (3645)              RETURN
  4331  (3646)          ENDIF
  4332  (3647)  
  4333  (3648)          % SAVE BEFORE LOAD, always. The two are a pair - see winSave.
  4334  (3649)          winSave
  4335  (3650)          winUse(w)
  4336  (3651)  
  4337  (3652)          % LOOKED AT, so it is no longer unread and no longer ringing. This is
  4338  (3653)          % the one path where that is true - see winUse.
  4339  (3654)          0 =: winUnread(w)
  4340  (3655)          0 =: winBell(w)
  4341  (3656)  
  4342  (3657)          % ONLY WHAT CHANGED. Switching windows changes the pane, the status
  4343  (3658)          % line and the bar - and nothing else. The frame, the title and the
  4344  (3659)          % typed line are all exactly as they were, so drawAll would rub out
  4345  (3660)          % twenty-four rows to alter three regions.
  4346  (3661)          %
  4347  (3662)          % This is TESTUI's rule and it is the one this file got wrong: put
  4348  (3663)          % back what the change actually touched, never the whole screen.
  4349  (3664)          IF screenOn THEN
  4350  (3665)              paintChat
  4351  (3666)              drawStatus
  4352  (3667)              drawBar
  4353  (3668)              drawInput
  4354  (3669)              flush
  4355  (3670)          ENDIF
  4356  (3671)      ENDROUTINE
  4357  (3672)  
  4358  (3673)      % The next window that exists, wrapping round. This is what a single key
  4359  (3674)      % gets you: with two windows open it is a toggle, which is the case that
  4360  (3675)      % matters most - one room and one person.
  4361  (3676)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : winNext
  4362  (3677)          INTEGER : i
  4363  (3678)          INTEGER : w
  4364  (3679)  
  4365  (3680)          FOR i IN 1:maxWins DO
  4366  (3681)              curWin + i =: w
  4367  (3682)              DO
  4368  (3683)                  WHILE w > maxWins
  4369  (3684)                  w - maxWins =: w
  4370  (3685)              ENDDO
  4371  (3686)              IF winUsed(w) >< 0 THEN
  4372  (3687)                  winGo(w)
  4373  (3688)                  RETURN
  4374  (3689)              ENDIF
  4375  (3690)          ENDFOR
  4376  (3691)      ENDROUTINE
  4377  (3692)  
  4378  (3693)      % =====================================================================
  4379  (3694)      % THE FAKE FEED - one made-up line
  4380  (3695)      % =====================================================================
  4381  (3696)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : pageUp
  4382  (3697)          INTEGER : most
  4383  (3698)          cacheCount - msgRows =: most
  4384  (3699)          IF most < 0 THEN
  4385  (3700)              0 =: most
  4386  (3701)          ENDIF
  4387  (3702)          viewFrom + msgRows =: viewFrom
  4388  (3703)          IF viewFrom > most THEN
  4389  (3704)              most =: viewFrom
  4390  (3705)          ENDIF
  4391  (3706)          paintChat
  4392  (3707)          drawStatus
  4393  (3708)          drawInput
  4394  (3709)          flush
  4395  (3710)      ENDROUTINE
  4396  (3711)  
  4397  (3712)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : pageDown
  4398  (3713)          viewFrom - msgRows =: viewFrom
  4399  (3714)          IF viewFrom < 0 THEN
  4400  (3715)              0 =: viewFrom
  4401  (3716)          ENDIF
  4402  (3717)          paintChat
  4403  (3718)          drawStatus
  4404  (3719)          drawInput
  4405  (3720)          flush
  4406  (3721)      ENDROUTINE
  4407  (3722)  
  4408  (3723)      % =====================================================================
  4409  (3724)      % THE KEYBOARD
  4410  (3725)      % =====================================================================
  4411  (3726)  
  4412  (3727)      % Does what has been typed start with this command word?
  4413  (3728)      %
  4414  (3729)      % CASE-FOLDED, because a user types /exit and /EXIT without thinking about
  4415  (3730)      % it, and SINTRAN itself does not care either.
  4416  (3731)      %
  4417  (3732)      % Compares against typedLen, so "/exits" does NOT match "/exit" - the length
  4418  (3733)      % has to be right as well as the letters. That matters the moment there is
  4419  (3734)      % a /e and a /exit to tell apart.
  4420  (3735)      ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN (BYTES) : typedIs(word)
  4421  (3736)          INTEGER : n
  4422  (3737)          INTEGER : i
  4423  (3738)          INTEGER : a
  4424  (3739)          INTEGER : b
  4425  (3740)  
  4426  (3741)          MAXINDEX(word, 1) + 1 =: n
  4427  (3742)          IF n >< typedLen THEN
  4428  (3743)              FALSE RETURN
  4429  (3744)          ENDIF
  4430  (3745)          IF n = 0 THEN
  4431  (3746)              FALSE RETURN
  4432  (3747)          ENDIF
  4433  (3748)  
  4434  (3749)          FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
  4435  (3750)              typed(i) =: a
  4436  (3751)              word(i) =: b
  4437  (3752)              % Fold a lower-case letter up. 97 is 'a' and 122 is 'z'; 32 is the
  4438  (3753)              % gap to the capital.
  4439  (3754)              IF a >= 97 AND a <= 122 THEN
  4440  (3755)                  a - 32 =: a
  4441  (3756)              ENDIF
  4442  (3757)              IF b >= 97 AND b <= 122 THEN
  4443  (3758)                  b - 32 =: b
  4444  (3759)              ENDIF
  4445  (3760)              IF a >< b THEN
  4446  (3761)                  FALSE RETURN
  4447  (3762)              ENDIF
  4448  (3763)          ENDFOR
  4449  (3764)          TRUE RETURN
  4450  (3765)      ENDROUTINE
  4451  (3766)  
  4452  (3767)      % Take one key WITHOUT waiting, and let VTM say what it was.
  4453  (3768)      %
  4454  (3769)      % TWO CALLS, AND EACH DOES THE HALF THE OTHER CANNOT.
  4455  (3770)      %
  4456  (3771)      %   MON66  answers HOW MANY BYTES ARE WAITING. That is what keeps the
  4457  (3772)      %          timer alive - without it the read blocks and nothing else in
  4458  (3773)      %          the program can happen.
  4459  (3774)      %   VTINBT reads the key and DECODES it. A function key is an escape
  4460  (3775)      %          sequence, every terminal sends a different one, and VTM is the
  4461  (3776)      %          layer that knows which. This program never sees a byte of it.
  4462  (3777)      %
  4463  (3778)      % A function key is several bytes on the wire and VTINBT consumes all of
  4464  (3779)      % them in one call, so MON66 answering "at least one" is enough - it does
  4465  (3780)      % not have to know how many the key will turn out to be.
  4466  (3781)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER : pollKey
  4467  (3782)          INTEGER : waiting
  4468  (3783)          % NOT "ch" - the client already has one and the compiler answers
  4469  (3784)          % IDENTIFIER ALREADY SPECIFIED/DECLARED. It is only a warning, and that
  4470  (3785)          % is exactly why it is worth fixing rather than leaving: a shadowed
  4471  (3786)          % name compiles and then behaves in whichever way nobody expected.
  4472  (3787)          INTEGER : keyByte
  4473  (3788)          INTEGER : st
  4474  (3789)          INTEGER : dev
  4475  (3790)          INTEGER : mode
  4476  (3791)          INTEGER : arg4
  4477  (3792)  
  4478  (3793)          MON66(1) =: waiting
  4479  (3794)          IF waiting <= 0 THEN
  4480  (3795)              0 RETURN
  4481  (3796)          ENDIF
  4482  (3797)  
  4483  (3798)          % arg 1 is our own terminal. The 2 and the 3 are STAR-TREK's and are
  4484  (3799)          % NOT understood - no ND document describes this call at all - so they
  4485  (3800)          % are passed exactly as the vendor wrote them rather than guessed at.
  4486  (3801)          % PASSED AS VARIABLES, NOT LITERALS - and that is not fussiness.
  4487  (3802)          % MEASURED 2026-08-25: with literals in the call PAGE UP did nothing
  4488  (3803)          % at all, while the identical call with variables in KEYPROB.PLNC
  4489  (3804)          % returned 201 every time. The call is made the way it was proved.
  4490  (3805)          1 =: dev
  4491  (3806)          2 =: mode
  4492  (3807)          3 =: arg4
  4493  (3808)          0 =: keyByte
  4494  (3809)          VTINBT(dev, mode, keyByte, arg4) =: st
  4495  (3810)          keyByte RETURN
  4496  (3811)      ENDROUTINE
  4497  (3812)  
  4498  (3813)      % =====================================================================
  4499  (3814)      % MAIN
  4500  (3815)      % =====================================================================
  4501  (3816)  
  4502  (3817)      % =====================================================================
  4503  (3818)      % THE SCREEN RENDERER'S SIDE OF AN ARRIVING MESSAGE
  4504  (3819)      %
  4505  (3820)      % One routine per JOB, not one per kind: everything the room says reduces
  4506  (3821)      % to a SPEAKER and a LINE, and the cache already knows how to wrap that
  4507  (3822)      % across rows. A notice with no speaker uses '*', which is what the line
  4508  (3823)      % renderer has always printed for the same thing.
  4509  (3824)      %
  4510  (3825)      % Answers TRUE when it has dealt with the message, so showArrived can
  4511  (3826)      % return and leave its OUTPUT code untouched. That is what lets the two
  4512  (3827)      % renderers live side by side instead of one being rewritten into the
  4513  (3828)      % other: the LINE renderer stays exactly as it was, and stays the fallback
  4514  (3829)      % for a printing terminal.
  4515  (3830)      % =====================================================================
  4516  (3831)  
  4517  (3832)      % A line being composed out of pieces. 250 rather than 256 because every
  4518  (3833)      % copy into it is clamped and the clamp needs somewhere to stop.
  4519  (3834)      BYTES   : uiBuf(0:255)
  4520  (3835)      INTEGER : uiLen := 0
  4521  (3836)  
  4522  (3837)      % THE SPEAKER GOES IN A BUFFER OF ITS OWN, and that is not tidiness.
  4523  (3838)      %
  4524  (3839)      % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26. addSaid was called as
  4525  (3840)      %
  4526  (3841)      %     addSaid(inBuf(2 : 2 + nameLength - 1), inBuf(textAt : textAt + textLength - 1))
  4527  (3842)      %
  4528  (3843)      % - TWO SUBARRAYS OF THE SAME ARRAY - and the rows came out smeared across
  4529  (3844)      % the screen, each one carrying part of its neighbour:
  4530  (3845)      %
  4531  (3846)      %     18:21  SYSTE@VIDDA
  4532  (3847)      %            SYSTE@VIDDASYSTEM@VIDDA!does this land without a keypress
  4533  (3848)      %                       SYSTEM@VIDDAsecond
  4534  (3849)      %
  4535  (3850)      % A NOTICE built the same line correctly the whole time, and that is what
  4536  (3851)      % identified it: uiNotice passes addSaid(starByte, uiBuf(...)) - two
  4537  (3852)      % DIFFERENT arrays - and rendered
  4538  (3853)      %
  4539  (3854)      %     18:21  *            here: TESTER SYSTEM@SKOGEN SYSTEM@VIDDA
  4540  (3855)      %
  4541  (3856)      % perfectly on the same screen, in the same paint, through the same
  4542  (3857)      % addSaid and the same pushRow. One variable differed.
  4543  (3858)      %
  4544  (3859)      % So the speaker is copied out first and the two arguments are never
  4545  (3860)      % subarrays of one array again.
  4546  (3861)      BYTES   : whoBuf(0:31)
  4547  (3862)      INTEGER : whoLen := 0
  4548  (3863)  
  4549  (3864)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : uiClear
  4550  (3865)          0 =: uiLen
  4551  (3866)      ENDROUTINE
  4552  (3867)  
  4553  (3868)      % Append a literal.
  4554  (3869)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : uiWord(w)
  4555  (3870)          INTEGER : i
  4556  (3871)          INTEGER : n
  4557  (3872)          MAXINDEX(w, 1) + 1 =: n
  4558  (3873)          FOR i IN 1:n DO
  4559  (3874)              IF uiLen < 250 THEN
  4560  (3875)                  w(i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
  4561  (3876)                  uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
  4562  (3877)              ENDIF
  4563  (3878)          ENDFOR
  4564  (3879)      ENDROUTINE
  4565  (3880)  
  4566  (3881)      % Append a field out of the arriving message.
  4567  (3882)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : uiFrom(at, n)
  4568  (3883)          INTEGER : i
  4569  (3884)          IF n < 1 THEN
  4570  (3885)              RETURN
  4571  (3886)          ENDIF
  4572  (3887)          FOR i IN 1:n DO
  4573  (3888)              IF uiLen < 250 THEN
  4574  (3889)                  inBuf(at + i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
  4575  (3890)                  uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
  4576  (3891)              ENDIF
  4577  (3892)          ENDFOR
  4578  (3893)      ENDROUTINE
  4579  (3894)  
  4580  (3895)      % A notice - no speaker, so the star column that the line renderer uses.
  4581  (3896)      % Append from a module buffer that carries its length separately - the
  4582  (3897)      % nickname and the machine both do. uiFrom reads inBuf; this does not.
  4583  (3898)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER) : uiWho2(src, n)
  4584  (3899)          INTEGER : i
  4585  (3900)          IF n < 1 THEN
  4586  (3901)              RETURN
  4587  (3902)          ENDIF
  4588  (3903)          FOR i IN 1:n DO
  4589  (3904)              IF uiLen < 250 THEN
  4590  (3905)                  src(i - 1) =: uiBuf(uiLen)
  4591  (3906)                  uiLen + 1 =: uiLen
  4592  (3907)              ENDIF
  4593  (3908)          ENDFOR
  4594  (3909)      ENDROUTINE
  4595  (3910)  
  4596  (3911)      % =====================================================================
  4597  (3912)      % THE PANEL - an answer that is FOR YOU, not for the room
  4598  (3913)      %
  4599  (3914)      % /help, /who, /list, /map and an asked-for /topic are all replies to the
  4600  (3915)      % person who typed them. They are not things anybody said, so putting them
  4601  (3916)      % in the room mixes one person's questions into everybody's conversation
  4602  (3917)      % and pushes real lines off the top.
  4603  (3918)      %
  4604  (3919)      % They go in a window instead: built as a TABLE OF LINES, measured, and
  4605  (3920)      % centred over the room. Any key closes it.
  4606  (3921)      %
  4607  (3922)      % THE TABLE IS WHAT MAKES THE SIZING SAFE. The window measures the text,
  4608  (3923)      % so adding a command or lengthening a line moves the frame instead of
  4609  (3924)      % overflowing it. A window sized by a number typed next to the text goes
  4610  (3925)      % wrong the first time somebody edits the text - the same trap as a
  4611  (3926)      % hand-counted 'ALnn' width, which this file has already paid for.
  4612  (3927)      % =====================================================================
  4613  (3928)      CONSTANT panelMax=22
  4614  (3929)      CONSTANT panelWide=70
  4615  (3930)      BYTES : panelBuf(0:1539)
  4616  (3931)      INTEGER ARRAY : panelLen(0:21)
  4617  (3932)      INTEGER : panelRows := 0
  4618  (3933)  
  4619  (3934)      % The panel's flag and rectangle are declared UP WITH THE SCREEN GEOMETRY,
  4620  (3935)      % because paintChat is written before this point and PLANC is single pass -
  4621  (3936)      % a name must be declared before the line that uses it.
  4622  (3937)  
  4623  (3938)      % DID THIS CLIENT ASK? Both of these answers arrive as a kind that is also
  4624  (3939)      % used for something else - kWho for the count taken automatically on the
  4625  (3940)      % way in, kTopic for a change the whole room is told about - so the kind
  4626  (3941)      % alone cannot say whether the reply is FOR YOU. Typing the command is
  4627  (3942)      % what makes it yours.
  4628  (3943)      % HAS THE FIRST WELCOME BEEN SEEN? Every /join gets a welcome of its own,
  4629  (3944)      % so without this the "you are TESTER on FJELL" line is repeated on every
  4630  (3945)      % room change - and the machine has not changed, so it says nothing new.
  4631  (3946)      BOOLEAN : firstWelcome := TRUE
  4632  (3947)      % TEMPORARY - 2026-09-01, see announceWelcome.
  4633  (3948)      INTEGER : welcomeCalls := 0
  4634  (3949)  
  4635  (3950)      BOOLEAN : askedWho := FALSE
  4636  (3951)      BOOLEAN : askedTopic := FALSE
  4637  (3952)  
  4638  (3953)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : panelClear
  4639  (3954)          0 =: panelRows
  4640  (3955)      ENDROUTINE
  4641  (3956)  
  4642  (3957)      % One line from a literal.
  4643  (3958)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES) : panelAdd(t)
  4644  (3959)          INTEGER : i
  4645  (3960)          INTEGER : n
  4646  (3961)          INTEGER : base
  4647  (3962)  
  4648  (3963)          IF panelRows >= panelMax THEN
  4649  (3964)              RETURN
  4650  (3965)          ENDIF
  4651  (3966)          MAXINDEX(t, 1) + 1 =: n
  4652  (3967)          IF n > panelWide THEN
  4653  (3968)              panelWide =: n
  4654  (3969)          ENDIF
  4655  (3970)          panelRows * panelWide =: base
  4656  (3971)          IF n > 0 THEN
  4657  (3972)              FOR i IN 1:n DO
  4658  (3973)                  t(i - 1) =: panelBuf(base + i - 1)
  4659  (3974)              ENDFOR
  4660  (3975)          ENDIF
  4661  (3976)          n =: panelLen(panelRows)
  4662  (3977)          panelRows + 1 =: panelRows
  4663  (3978)      ENDROUTINE
  4664  (3979)  
  4665  (3980)      % A run of text out of the arriving message, WRAPPED onto as many lines as
  4666  (3981)      % it needs.
  4667  (3982)      %
  4668  (3983)      % IT BREAKS ON A SPACE WHERE IT CAN. A /who answer is a list of names and
  4669  (3984)      % cutting one in half mid-name is exactly the thing a reader has to undo
  4670  (3985)      % in their head. Where there is no space to break on - one enormous word -
  4671  (3986)      % it cuts, because the alternative is a line that runs off the frame.
  4672  (3987)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : panelAddFrom(at, n)
  4673  (3988)          INTEGER : i
  4674  (3989)          INTEGER : from
  4675  (3990)          INTEGER : take
  4676  (3991)          INTEGER : look
  4677  (3992)          INTEGER : cut
  4678  (3993)          INTEGER : base
  4679  (3994)  
  4680  (3995)          IF n < 1 THEN
  4681  (3996)              RETURN
  4682  (3997)          ENDIF
  4683  (3998)  
  4684  (3999)          0 =: from
  4685  (4000)          DO
  4686  (4001)              WHILE from < n
  4687  (4002)              IF panelRows >= panelMax THEN
  4688  (4003)                  RETURN
  4689  (4004)              ENDIF
  4690  (4005)  
  4691  (4006)              n - from =: take
  4692  (4007)              IF take > 60 THEN
  4693  (4008)                  60 =: take
  4694  (4009)  
  4695  (4010)                  % Walk back to the last space in what we were going to take.
  4696  (4011)                  0 =: cut
  4697  (4012)                  FOR i IN 1:take - 1 DO
  4698  (4013)                      IF inBuf(at + from + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
  4699  (4014)                          i =: cut
  4700  (4015)                      ENDIF
  4701  (4016)                  ENDFOR
  4702  (4017)                  IF cut > 0 THEN
  4703  (4018)                      cut =: take
  4704  (4019)                  ENDIF
  4705  (4020)              ENDIF
  4706  (4021)  
  4707  (4022)              panelRows * panelWide =: base
  4708  (4023)              0 =: look
  4709  (4024)              FOR i IN 1:take DO
  4710  (4025)                  IF look < panelWide THEN
  4711  (4026)                      inBuf(at + from + i - 1) =: panelBuf(base + look)
  4712  (4027)                      look + 1 =: look
  4713  (4028)                  ENDIF
  4714  (4029)              ENDFOR
  4715  (4030)              look =: panelLen(panelRows)
  4716  (4031)              panelRows + 1 =: panelRows
  4717  (4032)  
  4718  (4033)              from + take =: from
  4719  (4034)  
  4720  (4035)              % Skip the space we broke on, so the next line does not start with
  4721  (4036)              % it and look indented by one.
  4722  (4037)              IF from < n THEN
  4723  (4038)                  IF inBuf(at + from) = spaceByte(0) THEN
  4724  (4039)                      from + 1 =: from
  4725  (4040)                  ENDIF
  4726  (4041)              ENDIF
  4727  (4042)          ENDDO
  4728  (4043)      ENDROUTINE
  4729  (4044)  
  4730  (4045)      % The command list. NOT the room name - the status line already carries
  4731  (4046)      % that, and it was the one part of the old help that was not constant.
  4732  (4047)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : buildHelp
  4733  (4048)          panelClear
  4734  (4049)          panelAdd('CHAT commands')
  4735  (4050)          panelAdd(' ')
  4736  (4051)          panelAdd('  /join <room>          join another room')
  4737  (4052)          panelAdd('  /nick <name>          change your nickname')
  4738  (4053)          panelAdd('  /who                  who is in this room')
  4739  (4054)          panelAdd('  /list                 rooms, and how many in each')
  4740  (4055)          panelAdd('  /map                  the machines and their trunks')
  4741  (4056)          panelAdd('  /topic                show the room topic')
  4742  (4057)          panelAdd('  /topic <text>         set it')
  4743  (4058)          panelAdd('  /tell <name> <text>   a message to one person')
  4744  (4059)          panelAdd('  /w <n>                go to window n  (ctrl-W cycles)')
  4745  (4060)          panelAdd('  /close                close a conversation window')
  4746  (4061)          panelAdd('  /leave                leave the room')
  4747  (4062)          panelAdd('  /exit                 Exit chat program')
  4748  (4063)          panelAdd(' ')
  4749  (4064)          panelAdd('  anything else is said to the room')
  4750  (4065)      ENDROUTINE
  4751  (4066)  
  4752  (4067)      % =====================================================================
  4753  (4068)      % DRAW IT - measured from the table, centred over the room
  4754  (4069)      % =====================================================================
  4755  (4070)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drawPanel
  4756  (4071)          INTEGER : i
  4757  (4072)          INTEGER : w
  4758  (4073)          INTEGER : h
  4759  (4074)          INTEGER : row
  4760  (4075)          INTEGER : col
  4761  (4076)          INTEGER : base
  4762  (4077)  
  4763  (4078)          panelAdd(' ')
  4764  (4079)          panelAdd('  -- any key closes this --')
  4765  (4080)  
  4766  (4081)          % THE WIDEST LINE DECIDES THE WIDTH. Nothing is counted by hand, and
  4767  (4082)          % the closing hint above is measured with everything else - it is a
  4768  (4083)          % line like any other, so it cannot be the one that overflows.
  4769  (4084)          0 =: w
  4770  (4085)          IF panelRows > 0 THEN
  4771  (4086)              FOR i IN 0:panelRows - 1 DO
  4772  (4087)                  IF panelLen(i) > w THEN
  4773  (4088)                      panelLen(i) =: w
  4774  (4089)                  ENDIF
  4775  (4090)              ENDFOR
  4776  (4091)          ENDIF
  4777  (4092)  
  4778  (4093)          % Two columns of frame and one space of padding either side.
  4779  (4094)          w + 4 =: w
  4780  (4095)          panelRows + 2 =: h
  4781  (4096)  
  4782  (4097)          % CLAMPED TO THE SCREEN, AND DELIBERATELY INSIDE THE FRAME.
  4783  (4098)          %
  4784  (4099)          % Text taller than the terminal would otherwise draw a frame off the
  4785  (4100)          % bottom and take the layout with it. Clamping loses the tail, which is
  4786  (4101)          % visible and fixable; drawing past the edge is neither.
  4787  (4102)          %
  4788  (4103)          % THE FRAME IS NOW OUT OF BOUNDS - rows 2..23 and columns 2..79, not
  4789  (4104)          % 1..24 and 1..80. That is what makes closePanel cheap and provable:
  4790  (4105)          % the only things a panel can cover are the status row, the two
  4791  (4106)          % separators, the pane, the input line and the bar, and every one of
  4792  (4107)          % those is a single call to put back. Allowing it over the border would
  4793  (4108)          % mean repainting frame SEGMENTS, because frame() carries SPACE-FILL
  4794  (4109)          % and would blank the whole screen on the way past.
  4795  (4110)          IF w > scrWide - 2 THEN
  4796  (4111)              scrWide - 2 =: w
  4797  (4112)          ENDIF
  4798  (4113)          IF h > 22 THEN
  4799  (4114)              22 =: h
  4800  (4115)          ENDIF
  4801  (4116)  
  4802  (4117)          % CENTRED INSIDE THE FRAME. Integer division floors, so an odd
  4803  (4118)          % remainder puts the window one column left of centre rather than off
  4804  (4119)          % the edge.
  4805  (4120)          (scrWide - w) / 2 + 1 =: col
  4806  (4121)          (24 - h) / 2 + 1 =: row
  4807  (4122)          IF col < 2 THEN
  4808  (4123)              2 =: col
  4809  (4124)          ENDIF
  4810  (4125)          IF row < 2 THEN
  4811  (4126)              2 =: row
  4812  (4127)          ENDIF
  4813  (4128)  
  4814  (4129)          % KEEP IT. This is the whole fix - see the note beside the declarations.
  4815  (4130)          row =: panRow
  4816  (4131)          col =: panCol
  4817  (4132)          h   =: panHigh
  4818  (4133)          w   =: panWid
  4819  (4134)  
  4820  (4135)          % A VIEWPORT CLIPS, so everything below is in the panel's own
  4821  (4136)          % coordinates and nothing it draws can reach the room behind it.
  4822  (4137)          winEnter(row, col, h, w)
  4823  (4138)          frame(1, 1, h, w, 'SPACE-FILL')
  4824  (4139)          IF panelRows > 0 THEN
  4825  (4140)              FOR i IN 0:panelRows - 1 DO
  4826  (4141)                  i * panelWide =: base
  4827  (4142)                  IF panelLen(i) > 0 THEN
  4828  (4143)                      IF 2 + i < h THEN
  4829  (4144)                          bytdis(2 + i, 3, panelLen(i), &
  4830  (4145)                                 panelBuf(base : base + panelLen(i) - 1), '')
  4831  (4146)                      ENDIF
  4832  (4147)                  ENDIF
  4833  (4148)              ENDFOR
  4834  (4149)          ENDIF
  4835  (4150)          winLeave
  4836  (4151)          TRUE =: panelUp
  4837  (4152)  
  4838  (4153)          % THE SHADOW IS NOW A LIE. This window has just been painted straight
  4839  (4154)          % over the pane, so the rows it covered no longer hold what rowShown
  4840  (4155)          % says they do - and paintChat would skip them as "already correct",
  4841  (4156)          % leaving the panel's remains on screen after it closed.
  4842  (4157)          %
  4843  (4158)          % Zeroing shadowWin makes the next paint redraw every row. That is the
  4844  (4159)          % one case where a full pane repaint is right: something else really
  4845  (4160)          % did overwrite it.
  4846  (4161)          0 =: shadowWin
  4847  (4162)      ENDROUTINE
  4848  (4163)  
  4849  (4164)      % =====================================================================
  4850  (4165)      % CLOSE THE PANEL, AND PUT BACK ONLY WHAT IT COVERED
  4851  (4166)      %
  4852  (4167)      % This replaces a call to drawAll. drawAll rebuilds all 24 rows - frame,
  4853  (4168)      % title, both separators, the whole pane, the status line, the bar and the
  4854  (4169)      % input line - to undo a window that covered a fraction of that. It always
  4855  (4170)      % looked correct, which is why it survived; it is also the flicker, and it
  4856  (4171)      % is the reason Ronny reported "the whole fucking screen is repainted".
  4857  (4172)      %
  4858  (4173)      % The rectangle is known now (panRow/panCol/panHigh/panWid), so the work is
  4859  (4174)      % exactly: erase it, put back the pieces that were under it, and stop. The
  4860  (4175)      % panel is clamped inside the frame by drawPanel, so the frame itself can
  4861  (4176)      % never be one of those pieces - which is what keeps this short.
  4862  (4177)      %
  4863  (4178)      % ORDER MATTERS, bottom up, the painter's algorithm: the separators sit
  4864  (4179)      % under the pane rows in the sense that they are drawn on the same screen
  4865  (4180)      % and must not be drawn over the top of it.
  4866  (4181)      % =====================================================================
  4867  (4182)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : closePanel
  4868  (4183)          INTEGER : i
  4869  (4184)          INTEGER : prow
  4870  (4185)          INTEGER : st
  4871  (4186)          BOOLEAN : hitStatus
  4872  (4187)          BOOLEAN : hitInput
  4873  (4188)          BOOLEAN : hitBar
  4874  (4189)          BOOLEAN : hitMid
  4875  (4190)          BOOLEAN : hitBot
  4876  (4191)  
  4877  (4192)          IF NOT panelUp THEN
  4878  (4193)              RETURN
  4879  (4194)          ENDIF
  4880  (4195)  
  4881  (4196)          % ASKED WHILE THE PANEL IS STILL UP. rowCovered answers FALSE once the
  4882  (4197)          % flag is cleared, so every question about what was underneath has to
  4883  (4198)          % be asked before that happens.
  4884  (4199)          rowCovered(statusPRow) =: hitStatus
  4885  (4200)          rowCovered(inputPRow) =: hitInput
  4886  (4201)          rowCovered(barPRow) =: hitBar
  4887  (4202)          rowCovered(midRow) =: hitMid
  4888  (4203)          rowCovered(botRow) =: hitBot
  4889  (4204)  
  4890  (4205)          FALSE =: panelUp
  4891  (4206)  
  4892  (4207)          % Erase exactly the rectangle it occupied. VTCREC is viewport-relative,
  4893  (4208)          % so from inside the window the rectangle is simply the whole of it and
  4894  (4209)          % there are no absolute corners left to get the wrong way round.
  4895  (4210)          winEnter(panRow, panCol, panHigh, panWid)
  4896  (4211)          VTCREC(1, 1, panHigh, panWid) =: st
  4897  (4212)          winLeave
  4898  (4213)  
  4899  (4214)          % The separators are one row each and cheap. fullbar is safe to repeat
  4900  (4215)          % - unlike frame, which carries SPACE-FILL and would blank the screen.
  4901  (4216)          winEnter(1, 1, 24, scrWide)
  4902  (4217)          IF hitMid THEN
  4903  (4218)              fullbar(midRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
  4904  (4219)          ENDIF
  4905  (4220)          IF hitBot THEN
  4906  (4221)              fullbar(botRow, 2, 1, scrWide - 2)
  4907  (4222)          ENDIF
  4908  (4223)          winLeave
  4909  (4224)  
  4910  (4225)          % FORGET ONLY THE PANE ROWS THE PANEL COVERED.
  4911  (4226)          %
  4912  (4227)          % paintChat records a row as painted even when part of it was clipped
  4913  (4228)          % away, which is right while the panel is up. Those rows - and only
  4914  (4229)          % those - are now stale, so clearing their shadow is what makes the
  4915  (4230)          % next paintChat repaint exactly the covered strip and nothing else.
  4916  (4231)          % Minus two is used because minus one is a REAL value meaning "blank".
  4917  (4232)          FOR i IN 0:msgRows - 1 DO
  4918  (4233)              midRow + firstMsgRow + i - 1 =: prow
  4919  (4234)              IF prow >= panRow AND prow <= panRow + panHigh - 1 THEN
  4920  (4235)                  0 - 2 =: rowShown(i)
  4921  (4236)              ENDIF
  4922  (4237)          ENDFOR
  4923  (4238)          paintChat
  4924  (4239)  
  4925  (4240)          % The one-row things, each only if it was under the panel. Both of the
  4926  (4241)          % first two short-circuit on "nothing changed", so each needs its own
  4927  (4242)          % invalidation or it would decide the row is already right - it is not,
  4928  (4243)          % the panel was over it.
  4929  (4244)          IF hitStatus THEN
  4930  (4245)              0 - 1 =: lastRoomLen
  4931  (4246)              drawStatus
  4932  (4247)          ENDIF
  4933  (4248)          IF hitInput THEN
  4934  (4249)              TRUE =: inputDirty
  4935  (4250)              drawInput
  4936  (4251)          ENDIF
  4937  (4252)          IF hitBar THEN
  4938  (4253)              drawBar
  4939  (4254)          ENDIF
  4940  (4255)  
  4941  (4256)          flush
  4942  (4257)      ENDROUTINE
  4943  (4258)  
  4944  (4259)  
  4945  (4260)      % An answer that arrived as a message: a heading, then the text wrapped.
  4946  (4261)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (BYTES, INTEGER, INTEGER) : panelAnswer(head, at, n)
  4947  (4262)          panelClear
  4948  (4263)          panelAdd(head)
  4949  (4264)          panelAdd(' ')
  4950  (4265)          IF n < 1 THEN
  4951  (4266)              panelAdd('  (nothing)')
  4952  (4267)          ELSE
  4953  (4268)              panelAddFrom(at, n)
  4954  (4269)          ENDIF
  4955  (4270)          drawPanel
  4956  (4271)      ENDROUTINE
  4957  (4272)  
  4958  (4273)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : uiNotice
  4959  (4274)          IF uiLen < 1 THEN
  4960  (4275)              RETURN
  4961  (4276)          ENDIF
  4962  (4277)          addSaid(starByte, uiBuf(0:uiLen - 1))
  4963  (4278)      ENDROUTINE
  4964  (4279)  
  4965  (4280)      % ANNOUNCE THE FIRST WELCOME, ONCE - moved here 2026-09-01 so the check
  4966  (4281)      % and the flag it reads live in the SAME module.
  4967  (4282)      %
  4968  (4283)      % MEASURED: with firstWelcome EXPORTed and read back through an IMPORT in
  4969  (4284)      % CHATARR.PLNC, this branch fired TWICE for one real join on a client that
  4970  (4285)      % never touched the room a second time - firstWelcome AND cacheCount both
  4971  (4286)      % read back as their INITIAL values on the second firing, which is not
  4972  (4287)      % what a stray extra message would look like. A ROUTINE call across that
  4973  (4288)      % same boundary is what every other line in this room already goes
  4974  (4289)      % through - uiWord, uiWho2, uiNotice, addSaid - and none of those have
  4975  (4290)      % ever misbehaved. The difference is DATA crossing the boundary versus
  4976  (4291)      % CODE crossing it, so the fix is to stop asking CHATARR to read and write
  4977  (4292)      % this module's own flag from the other side of it.
  4978  (4293)      %
  4979  (4294)      % ANSWERS TRUE THE ONE TIME IT ANNOUNCES, so the caller can still tell a
  4980  (4295)      % genuine first join from a later room change without needing its own
  4981  (4296)      % copy of the flag.
  4982  (4297)      ROUTINE VOID, BOOLEAN : announceWelcome
  4983  (4298)          % TEMPORARY - 2026-09-01. Counts every CALL, guard or no guard, so a
  4984  (4299)          % second visible line can be told apart from a second real CALL.
  4985  (4300)          welcomeCalls + 1 =: welcomeCalls
  4986  (4301)          uiClear
  4987  (4302)          IF welcomeCalls = 1 THEN
  4988  (4303)              uiWord('[C1] ')
  4989  (4304)          ENDIF
  4990  (4305)          IF welcomeCalls = 2 THEN
  4991  (4306)              uiWord('[C2] ')
  4992  (4307)          ENDIF
  4993  (4308)          IF welcomeCalls >= 3 THEN
  4994  (4309)              uiWord('[C3+] ')
  4995  (4310)          ENDIF
  4996  (4311)          IF firstWelcome THEN
  4997  (4312)              uiWord('fw=T ')
  4998  (4313)          ELSE
  4999  (4314)              uiWord('fw=F ')
  5000  (4315)          ENDIF
  5001  (4316)          IF cacheCount = 0 THEN
  5002  (4317)              uiWord('cc=0 ')
  5003  (4318)          ELSE
  5004  (4319)              uiWord('cc>0 ')
  5005  (4320)          ENDIF
  5006  (4321)          uiWord('you are ')
  5007  (4322)          uiWho2(myName, lenMyName)
  5008  (4323)          IF lenMyMach > 0 THEN
  5009  (4324)              uiWord(' on ')
  5010  (4325)              uiWho2(myMachine, lenMyMach)
  5011  (4326)          ENDIF
  5012  (4327)          uiNotice
  5013  (4328)  
  5014  (4329)          IF NOT firstWelcome THEN
  5015  (4330)              FALSE RETURN
  5016  (4331)          ENDIF
  5017  (4332)          IF cacheCount >< 0 THEN
  5018  (4333)              FALSE RETURN
  5019  (4334)          ENDIF
  5020  (4335)          FALSE =: firstWelcome
  5021  (4336)          TRUE RETURN
  5022  (4337)      ENDROUTINE
  5023  (4338)  
  5024  (4339)      % PUT A LINE IN A WINDOW THAT MAY NOT BE THE ONE ON SCREEN.
  5025  (4340)      %
  5026  (4341)      % The line is built by the ordinary ui* helpers, so it wraps and is padded
  5027  (4342)      % exactly like every other line - stepping into the window and back out
  5028  (4343)      % again is the whole trick, and it is why winUse exists separately from
  5029  (4344)      % winGo.
  5030  (4345)      %
  5031  (4346)      % THE COUNTERS ARE ONLY TOUCHED WHEN IT IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S WINDOW. A
  5032  (4347)      % message arriving in the window you are reading has been seen by
  5033  (4348)      % definition, and showing "1 unread" against the line you are looking at
  5034  (4349)      % would train a reader to ignore the number.
  5035  (4350)      %
  5036  (4351)      % Note it does NOT repaint. The caller is the arrival path, which repaints
  5037  (4352)      % once for everything that arrived - see the main loop.
  5038  (4353)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
  5039  (4354)              putInWin(w, nmAt, nmLen, txAt, txLen)
  5040  (4355)          INTEGER : prev
  5041  (4356)  
  5042  (4357)          curWin =: prev
  5043  (4358)          IF w >< prev THEN
  5044  (4359)              winSave
  5045  (4360)              winUse(w)
  5046  (4361)          ENDIF
  5047  (4362)  
  5048  (4363)          % THE STARS ARE NOT DECORATION. A reply typed as an ordinary line goes
  5049  (4364)          % to the whole room, so a private message that looks like room traffic
  5050  (4365)          % is a privacy failure caused entirely by the display. They stay even
  5051  (4366)          % in the person's own window, where the context is already private,
  5052  (4367)          % because the line is what gets read back later out of context.
  5053  (4368)          uiClear
  5054  (4369)          uiWord('*(')
  5055  (4370)          uiFrom(nmAt, nmLen)
  5056  (4371)          uiWord(') ')
  5057  (4372)          uiFrom(txAt, txLen)
  5058  (4373)          uiNotice
  5059  (4374)  
  5060  (4375)          IF w >< prev THEN
  5061  (4376)              winSave
  5062  (4377)              winUse(prev)
  5063  (4378)              winUnread(w) + 1 =: winUnread(w)
  5064  (4379)  
  5065  (4380)              % A DIRECT MESSAGE ALWAYS RINGS. Elsewhere the star means "your
  5066  (4381)              % nick was said"; a message addressed to you personally is the
  5067  (4382)              % strongest form of that, so it does not need a name match.
  5068  (4383)              1 =: winBell(w)
  5069  (4384)          ENDIF
  5070  (4385)      ENDROUTINE
  5071  (4386)  
  5072  (4387)      % YOUR OWN SIDE OF A CONVERSATION.
  5073  (4388)      %
  5074  (4389)      % Separate from putInWin because the two read their text from different
  5075  (4390)      % places: an arriving message is in inBuf, and your own is in lastTell,
  5076  (4391)      % which the send path kept precisely because the server does not send it
  5077  (4392)      % back. Trying to serve both from one routine would mean a flag deciding
  5078  (4393)      % which array to read, and a flag like that is read wrongly exactly once.
  5079  (4394)      %
  5080  (4395)      % NOTHING IS MARKED UNREAD. This line is your own; counting it would put
  5081  (4396)      % a number on the bar for something you just typed.
  5082  (4397)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER) : putSentInWin(w)
  5083  (4398)          INTEGER : prev
  5084  (4399)  
  5085  (4400)          curWin =: prev
  5086  (4401)          IF w >< prev THEN
  5087  (4402)              winSave
  5088  (4403)              winUse(w)
  5089  (4404)          ENDIF
  5090  (4405)  
  5091  (4406)          uiClear
  5092  (4407)          uiWord('*(')
  5093  (4408)          IF lenMyName > 0 THEN
  5094  (4409)              uiWord(myName(0:lenMyName - 1))
  5095  (4410)          ENDIF
  5096  (4411)          uiWord(') ')
  5097  (4412)          IF lastTellLen > 0 THEN
  5098  (4413)              uiWord(lastTell(0:lastTellLen - 1))
  5099  (4414)          ENDIF
  5100  (4415)          uiNotice
  5101  (4416)  
  5102  (4417)          IF w >< prev THEN
  5103  (4418)              winSave
  5104  (4419)              winUse(prev)
  5105  (4420)          ENDIF
  5106  (4421)      ENDROUTINE
  5107  (4422)  
  5108  (4423)      % Copy the speaker out of the arriving message into its own buffer.
  5109  (4424)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER) : uiWho(at, n)
  5110  (4425)          INTEGER : i
  5111  (4426)          0 =: whoLen
  5112  (4427)          IF n < 1 THEN
  5113  (4428)              RETURN
  5114  (4429)          ENDIF
  5115  (4430)          FOR i IN 1:n DO
  5116  (4431)              IF whoLen < 30 THEN
  5117  (4432)                  inBuf(at + i - 1) =: whoBuf(whoLen)
  5118  (4433)                  whoLen + 1 =: whoLen
  5119  (4434)              ENDIF
  5120  (4435)          ENDFOR
  5121  (4436)      ENDROUTINE
  5122  (4437)  
  5123  (4438)      % A line somebody said: speaker in one buffer, text in another.
  5124  (4439)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID (INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER, INTEGER) : &
  5125  (4440)              uiSaid(nameAt, nameLen, txtAt, txtLen)
  5126  (4441)          uiWho(nameAt, nameLen)
  5127  (4442)          uiClear
  5128  (4443)          uiFrom(txtAt, txtLen)
  5129  (4444)          IF whoLen < 1 THEN
  5130  (4445)              uiNotice
  5131  (4446)              RETURN
  5132  (4447)          ENDIF
  5133  (4448)          IF uiLen < 1 THEN
  5134  (4449)              addSaid(whoBuf(0:whoLen - 1), spaceByte)
  5135  (4450)              RETURN
  5136  (4451)          ENDIF
  5137  (4452)          addSaid(whoBuf(0:whoLen - 1), uiBuf(0:uiLen - 1))
  5138  (4453)      ENDROUTINE
  5139  (4454)  
  5140  (4455)  
  5141  (4456)      % HOW MANY NAMES ARE IN A /who ANSWER.
  5142  (4457)      %
  5143  (4458)      % The server sends them space separated - "TESTER SYSTEM@SKOGEN
  5144  (4459)      % SYSTEM@VIDDA" - so counting the names is counting the runs of non-space.
  5145  (4460)      % Nothing is parsed beyond that: the client does not care what a name looks
  5146  (4461)      % like, only how many there are.
  5147  (4462)      %
  5148  (4463)      % IT COUNTS PEOPLE ON OTHER MACHINES TOO, which is what "here" should mean
  5149  (4464)      % in a room that spans machines - the same answer /who itself gives.
  5150  (4465)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (INTEGER, INTEGER) : countNames(at, n)
  5151  (4466)          INTEGER : i
  5152  (4467)          INTEGER : seen
  5153  (4468)          INTEGER : inWord
  5154  (4469)  
  5155  (4470)          0 =: seen
  5156  (4471)          0 =: inWord
  5157  (4472)          IF n < 1 THEN
  5158  (4473)              0 RETURN
  5159  (4474)          ENDIF
  5160  (4475)          FOR i IN 0:n - 1 DO
  5161  (4476)              IF inBuf(at + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
  5162  (4477)                  0 =: inWord
  5163  (4478)              ELSE
  5164  (4479)                  IF inWord = 0 THEN
  5165  (4480)                      seen + 1 =: seen
  5166  (4481)                  ENDIF
  5167  (4482)                  1 =: inWord
  5168  (4483)              ENDIF
  5169  (4484)          ENDFOR
  5170  (4485)          seen RETURN
  5171  (4486)      ENDROUTINE
  5172  (4487)  
  5173  (4488)  
  5174  (4489)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5175  (4490)      % Take everything the port is holding and print it, WITHOUT WAITING.
  5176  (4491)      %
  5177  (4492)      % flags ZERO means no XFWTF, and that is XMSG's own no-wait: the call
  5178  (4493)      % returns at once whether or not a message was there. Nothing about the
  5179  (4494)      % terminal is involved, which is why this works on a machine where the
  5180  (4495)      % no-wait TERMINAL switch (TNOWAIT 307B) cannot be reached from PLANC.
  5181  (4496)      %
  5182  (4497)      % A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE DOES RETURN ZERO ON THIS MACHINE, and the opposite
  5183  (4498)      % claim used to stand here. It read: "A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE DOES NOT RETURN
  5184  (4499)      % ZERO ... a message arrived exactly when the status is POSITIVE", citing
  5185  (4500)      % the constants file's "Message types: Returned as successfull status from
  5186  (4501)      % XFRCV", XMTNO=1 through XMTPS=6.
  5187  (4502)      %
  5188  (4503)      % MEASURED 2026-08-18: the server, doing the same call, prints st= 0 with
  5189  (4504)      % the type in the msgType out-parameter. The constants file is describing
  5190  (4505)      % the RAW MON 200B interface underneath; through the XMP library the status
  5191  (4506)      % is XMOK and the type comes back separately. Appendix G's sample agrees -
  5192  (4507)      % it tests "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK" and only then reads MSGTYPE.
  5193  (4508)      %
  5194  (4509)      % Excluding zero cost the whole join: XMPFRCV took the welcome off the
  5195  (4510)      % queue, returned XMOK, and the test below dropped straight out of the loop
  5196  (4511)      % without printing, reading or releasing it. See the note on the test.
  5197  (4512)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5198  (4513)      % PLANC HAS NO "WHILE cond DO ... ENDWHILE". WHILE is a CONTINUE-TEST
  5199  (4514)      % written INSIDE a DO ... ENDDO body: reaching it with a false condition
  5200  (4515)      % leaves the loop. ND-60.117.5 shows it that way ("continue the loop only
  5201  (4516)      % for negative array elements ... WHILE a(i)<0"), and writing the header
  5202  (4517)      % form gets WHILE NOT INSIDE LOOP followed by ILLEGAL SYNTAX "ENDWHILE".
  5203  (4518)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5204  (4519)      % Print an XMSG status with its name and meaning, then say where it came
  5205  (4520)      % from. Called on every path that gives up, so a failure never leaves just
  5206  (4521)      % a number - or worse, nothing at all.
  5207  (4522)      %
  5208  (4523)      % THE LIBRARY'S STATUS MODEL IS NOT THE KERNEL'S, and that difference cost
  5209  (4524)      % a whole evening. Through the XMP routines:
  5210  (4525)      %
  5211  (4526)      %   0                  XMOK - done. The kernel's own OK is 1; XMP-B02:DEFS
  5212  (4527)      %                      remaps it and says so: "NOTE change from XMSG OK=1".
  5213  (4528)      %   16896              XMXENTM - NOT TERMINATED. This is what an EMPTY port
  5214  (4529)      %                      answers, and it is NOT an error.
  5215  (4530)      %   16896 + n          an error, where n is the absolute value of the raw
  5216  (4531)      %                      kernel code. So the raw XEIBP = -6 appears here as
  5217  (4532)      %                      XMXEIBP = 16902.
  5218  (4533)      %   1 .. 6             a MESSAGE TYPE. Documented as a status from XFRCV,
  5219  (4534)      %                      but MEASURED 2026-08-18 the library answers XMOK and
  5220  (4535)      %                      puts the type in the msgType out-parameter instead.
  5221  (4536)      %                      Both forms are accepted on a receive for that reason.
  5222  (4537)      %
  5223  (4538)      % Which is why testing a receive with "> 0" is wrong: 16896 and every error
  5224  (4539)      % are positive too. Measured live: type=0 rel=16902, an empty port being
  5225  (4540)      % read as a message and then released, for ever.
  5226  (4541)      %
  5227  (4542)      % AND WHY TESTING IT WITH "1..6 ONLY" IS EQUALLY WRONG, which took longer to
  5228  (4543)      % find because it fails SILENTLY: XMPFRCV dequeues the message and returns
  5229  (4544)      % XMOK, the test rejects it, and the loop exits having printed nothing. A
  5230  (4545)      % receive must accept XMOK **or** 1..6 - see drainPort.
  5231  (4546)      %
  5232  (4547)      % The numbers are from XMP-B02:DEFS as extracted from the ND-10609 media;
  5233  (4548)      % the texts are the matching XE... comments in XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL.
  5234  (4549)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5235  (4550)  
  5236  (4551)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5237  (4552)      % Switch this terminal's INPUT side in and out of no-wait.
  5238  (4553)      %
  5239  (4554)      % THIS IS THE THING THE POLL LOOP ALWAYS ASSUMED AND NOBODY EVER SWITCHED
  5240  (4555)      % ON. The loop below was written for a no-wait terminal - it catches
  5241  (4556)      % ROUTINEERROR from MON1 and reads ErrCode 3 as "nothing was typed" - and a
  5242  (4557)      % comment there claims "the terminal is in no-wait". It was not. Nothing in
  5243  (4558)      % this program ever asked for it, so MON1 BLOCKED, and the loop never got
  5244  (4559)      % back round to drainPort until the user pressed return.
  5245  (4560)      %
  5246  (4561)      % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-20: two people in a room, and neither saw the
  5247  (4562)      % other's line until they typed something themselves. The speaker always saw
  5248  (4563)      % their own words at once, because pressing return is what let the loop go
  5249  (4564)      % round. That is indistinguishable from a message that was never sent, and
  5250  (4565)      % it cost an afternoon of hunting a delivery fault in a server that was
  5251  (4566)      % delivering to everybody correctly.
  5252  (4567)      %
  5253  (4568)      % IT DID NOT NEED MAC AFTER ALL. The notes at the foot of this file weigh a
  5254  (4569)      % hand-written MAC interface routine for TNOWAIT (307B) against an XFWAK
  5255  (4570)      % experiment, because "TNOWAIT cannot be had here". Both are unnecessary:
  5256  (4571)      % MONITOR_CALL takes a call BY NAME and handles output parameters - CHATSV
  5257  (4572)      % has been calling MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) for exactly that reason -
  5258  (4573)      % and MON-CALL-1B-A00 is already in this client's LOAD list. NoWaitSwitch
  5259  (4574)      % (NOWT 36B) takes three parameters and ALL THREE ARE INPUTS, so there is
  5260  (4575)      % not even an output to get wrong.
  5261  (4576)      %
  5262  (4577)      % ALWAYS SWITCH IT BACK. A terminal left in no-wait is one the user's shell
  5263  (4578)      % has to live with after this program ends, so every exit path goes through
  5264  (4579)      % terminalWait - see the leave path at the end of the main loop.
  5265  (4580)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5266  (4581)      % EVERY ONE OF THESE IS GUARDED, AND THE FIRST VERSION WAS NOT.
  5267  (4582)      %
  5268  (4583)      % MEASURED ON D100, 2026-08-21. The unguarded call killed the client the
  5269  (4584)      % instant it joined:
  5270  (4585)      %
  5271  (4586)      %     @NDCHAT
  5272  (4587)      %     CHAT: you are OLAV  (/nick to change)
  5273  (4588)      %     CHAT: joining (LOBBY) - type and press return
  5274  (4589)      %     NO ROUTINEERROR HANDLER, ERRETURN=177777B
  5275  (4590)      %     @
  5276  (4591)      %
  5277  (4592)      % A MONITOR_CALL that SINTRAN refuses raises a ROUTINEERROR, and an
  5278  (4593)      % unhandled one unwinds the whole program - after the join has been sent,
  5279  (4594)      % so it also leaves a seat behind in the room. The server was fine; the
  5280  (4595)      % client shot itself.
  5281  (4596)      %
  5282  (4597)      % THE FALLBACK IS THE OLD BEHAVIOUR, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS SAFE. Without the
  5283  (4598)      % switch, MON1 blocks and the loop comes round when the user presses return
  5284  (4599)      % - the very thing this change is trying to improve, but a working client.
  5285  (4600)      % So a refusal is worth ONE LINE ON SCREEN and nothing more.
  5286  (4601)      %
  5287  (4602)      % WHICH NAME IS RIGHT IS NOT SETTLED. MONITOR_CALL by NAME works here -
  5288  (4603)      % CHATSV has been calling MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) for weeks - so the
  5289  (4604)      % mechanism is proved and it is the SPELLING that is in doubt. The Monitor
  5290  (4605)      % Calls manual gives two candidates and their short names:
  5291  (4606)      %     NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  LDN, io flag, no-wait flag - ALL INPUTS
  5292  (4607)      %     TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) LDN, io flag, no-wait flag, status OUT
  5293  (4608)      % If the line below prints, try the other name, and then try inputFlag the
  5294  (4609)      % other way round - 0 for input is what this file has always claimed and
  5295  (4610)      % nothing has ever exercised it.
  5296  (4611)      % TWO NAMES TRIED, AND THE REASON PRINTED. One build, one answer.
  5297  (4612)      %
  5298  (4613)      % 'NoWaitSwitch' is refused on D100 - measured 2026-08-21, the client
  5299  (4614)      % printed its fallback line. But a refusal alone does not say WHETHER the
  5300  (4615)      % name is unknown or the PARAMETERS are wrong, and guessing between those
  5301  (4616)      % costs a build cycle each time. ErrCode is supplied by the runtime inside
  5302  (4617)      % the handler and it is the difference between the two, so it is printed.
  5303  (4618)      %
  5304  (4619)      % Then the other spelling is tried. The Monitor Calls manual offers both:
  5305  (4620)      %     NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  LDN, io flag, no-wait flag       3 IN
  5306  (4621)      %     TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) LDN, io flag, no-wait flag, st   3 IN 1 OUT
  5307  (4622)      % MONITOR_CALL handles an output parameter - CHATSV passes one to TimeOut -
  5308  (4623)      % so the second form costs nothing to attempt.
  5309  (4624)      %
  5310  (4625)      % IF BOTH ARE REFUSED, the remaining suspect is ownTerminal. It is 1, the
  5311  (4626)      % SINTRAN "own terminal" file number, and what NoWaitSwitch wants is the
  5312  (4627)      % LDN OF A CHARACTER DEVICE, which is not obviously the same thing.
  5313  (4628)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5314  (4629)      % ASK SINTRAN WHICH TERMINAL THIS IS.
  5315  (4630)      %
  5316  (4631)      % ExecutionInfo (RSIO 143B) hands back the execution mode and, for an
  5317  (4632)      % interactive program, "your terminal number" as BOTH the command input and
  5318  (4633)      % the command output device. That is the LDN the no-wait calls want, and the
  5319  (4634)      % manual names this call by name as the way to get it.
  5320  (4635)      %
  5321  (4636)      % ALL FOUR PARAMETERS ARE OUTPUTS. Nothing goes in.
  5322  (4637)      %
  5323  (4638)      % The mode is worth having anyway: 0 interactive, 1 batch, 2 mode job,
  5324  (4639)      % 3 RT. A chat client run from a MODE file gets a FILE number here, not a
  5325  (4640)      % terminal, and switching no-wait on that would be meaningless - so it is
  5326  (4641)      % only used when the mode says interactive.
  5327  (4642)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5328  (4643)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : findMyLdn
  5329  (4644)          BOOLEAN : askFailed
  5330  (4645)          INTEGER : execMode, inDev, outDev, userIdx
  5331  (4646)  
  5332  (4647)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5333  (4648)              TRUE =: askFailed
  5334  (4649)          ENDON
  5335  (4650)          FALSE =: askFailed
  5336  (4651)  
  5337  (4652)          0 =: execMode
  5338  (4653)          0 =: inDev
  5339  (4654)          0 =: outDev
  5340  (4655)          0 =: userIdx
  5341  (4656)  
  5342  (4657)          MONITOR_CALL('ExecutionInfo', execMode, inDev, outDev, userIdx)
  5343  (4658)  
  5344  (4659)          % Interactive only, and only if it gave back something usable. Anything
  5345  (4660)          % else leaves myLdn at 1, which is the behaviour this program has always
  5346  (4661)          % had - a failure here must not make the client worse than it was.
  5347  (4662)          IF NOT askFailed THEN
  5348  (4663)              IF execMode = 0 THEN
  5349  (4664)                  IF inDev > 1 THEN
  5350  (4665)                      inDev =: myLdn
  5351  (4666)                  ENDIF
  5352  (4667)              ENDIF
  5353  (4668)          ENDIF
  5354  (4669)      ENDROUTINE
  5355  (4670)  
  5356  (4671)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : terminalNoWait
  5357  (4672)          BOOLEAN : switchFailed
  5358  (4673)          INTEGER : nowaitStatus
  5359  (4674)  
  5360  (4675)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5361  (4676)              TRUE =: switchFailed
  5362  (4677)              ErrCode =: firstErr
  5363  (4678)          ENDON
  5364  (4679)          FALSE =: switchFailed
  5365  (4680)          0 =: firstErr
  5366  (4681)  
  5367  (4682)          % THE NAME THAT WORKS GOES FIRST, and this used to be the other way
  5368  (4683)          % round. NoWaitSwitch is REFUSED on this machine - ErrCode -1, measured -
  5369  (4684)          % so trying it first meant printing
  5370  (4685)          %
  5371  (4686)          %     CHAT: NoWaitSwitch err     -1
  5372  (4687)          %
  5373  (4688)          % on EVERY start-up, before quietly succeeding under the other name. A
  5374  (4689)          % diagnostic for a call we already know will fail is not a diagnostic,
  5375  (4690)          % it is noise, and it made a working start-up look broken. terminalWait
  5376  (4691)          % has always tried TerminalNoWait first; the two must agree or the
  5377  (4692)          % restore does nothing.
  5378  (4693)          0 =: nowaitStatus
  5379  (4694)          % myLdn, NOT ownTerminal. See the long note beside the declaration:
  5380  (4695)          % ownTerminal is a FILE number and the manual says file numbers are
  5381  (4696)          % illegal here. That single substitution is the whole fix.
  5382  (4697)          MONITOR_CALL('TerminalNoWait', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOn, &
  5383  (4698)              nowaitStatus)
  5384  (4699)  
  5385  (4700)          IF switchFailed THEN
  5386  (4701)              % Fall back SILENTLY. A machine where the other name is the live
  5387  (4702)              % one is a possibility, not a fault worth reporting.
  5388  (4703)              ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5389  (4704)                  TRUE =: switchFailed
  5390  (4705)                  ErrCode =: firstErr
  5391  (4706)              ENDON
  5392  (4707)              FALSE =: switchFailed
  5393  (4708)              0 =: firstErr
  5394  (4709)  
  5395  (4710)              MONITOR_CALL('NoWaitSwitch', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOn)
  5396  (4711)          ENDIF
  5397  (4712)  
  5398  (4713)          % ONLY IF BOTH WERE REFUSED is anything said, and then it says what the
  5399  (4714)          % user will SEE rather than which call failed - the number helps nobody
  5400  (4715)          % holding a keyboard.
  5401  (4716)          % A REFUSAL ARRIVES TWO DIFFERENT WAYS, AND BOTH MEAN THE SAME THING TO
  5402  (4717)          % WHOEVER IS HOLDING THE KEYBOARD.
  5403  (4718)          %
  5404  (4719)          %   NoWaitSwitch   (NOWT   36B)  RAISES          - ErrCode -1
  5405  (4720)          %   TerminalNoWait (TNOWAI 307B) RAISES NOTHING  - status -1
  5406  (4721)          %
  5407  (4722)          % MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-22 by printing the status, which until then
  5408  (4723)          % was captured into nowaitStatus and NEVER READ. That is why this was a
  5409  (4724)          % puzzle for so long: the call looked like it worked, because it did not
  5410  (4725)          % raise, and MON1 went on blocking anyway. AN ABSENT ERROR IS NOT A
  5411  (4726)          % SUCCESS - the earlier note here claimed TerminalNoWait "SUCCEEDS", and
  5412  (4727)          % that was inferred from the absence of a raise rather than measured.
  5413  (4728)          %
  5414  (4729)          % THAT CONCLUSION WAS WRONG, AND IT IS KEPT ABOVE BECAUSE IT IS THE
  5415  (4730)          % INSTRUCTIVE PART. "No-wait terminal input is simply NOT AVAILABLE to
  5416  (4731)          % this program on this machine" was written down as a fact to design
  5417  (4732)          % around. It was a MEASUREMENT of one wrong argument, promoted to a
  5418  (4733)          % property of the machine.
  5419  (4734)          %
  5420  (4735)          % Status -1 is defined in ND-860228-2 as "error in parameters", and the
  5421  (4736)          % offending parameter was the first one: a FILE number where an LDN
  5422  (4737)          % belongs. The manual says so beside every call that takes an LDN, and
  5423  (4738)          % names ExecutionInfo as the way to get the right value. See findMyLdn.
  5424  (4739)          %
  5425  (4740)          % TWO WHOLE DAYS went into the SPELLING of the call and the value of the
  5426  (4741)          % io flag - the two arguments that were already correct - because the
  5427  (4742)          % status number was read as a verdict on the call instead of as what it
  5428  (4743)          % literally says: your parameters are wrong.
  5429  (4744)          %
  5430  (4745)          % The number IS printed again if it still fails, with the LDN beside it,
  5431  (4746)          % because those two together are the whole diagnosis. One line, only on
  5432  (4747)          % failure, and it should never appear.
  5433  (4748)          % SAY IT WHATEVER THE STATUS SAYS, because the status is now LYING to
  5434  (4749)          % the user by being 0. This line used to fire on a refusal, and a
  5435  (4750)          % refusal is exactly what we no longer get - the call is accepted and
  5436  (4751)          % the behaviour is unchanged. Tying the warning to the status would
  5437  (4752)          % leave a user with a client that silently sits on their messages and
  5438  (4753)          % nothing on screen to explain it, which is worse than the old noise.
  5439  (4754)          %
  5440  (4755)          % Put it back under the IF the day a silent listener sees a line arrive
  5441  (4756)          % on its own. That is the test; nothing else counts.
  5442  (4757)          %
  5443  (4758)          % THAT DAY IS HERE FOR THE SCREEN RENDERER, and only for it. Its
  5444  (4759)          % keyboard asks MON66 how many bytes are waiting instead of trusting
  5445  (4760)          % no-wait, so the loop goes round without a keystroke and a line DOES
  5446  (4761)          % arrive on its own. The warning would be false, and it was printing
  5447  (4762)          % over the window bar as well - it is written for a renderer that owns
  5448  (4763)          % the whole page and scrolls it.
  5449  (4764)          %
  5450  (4765)          % The LINE renderer still means every word of it.
  5451  (4766)          IF NOT screenOn THEN
  5452  (4767)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', '$new lines show when you press return$')
  5453  (4768)          ENDIF
  5454  (4769)  
  5455  (4770)          % THE MEASUREMENT THAT ANSWERED IT, AND IT IS NOT GOOD NEWS.
  5456  (4771)          %
  5457  (4772)          % A build printing the LDN and the status unconditionally was run on
  5458  (4773)          % D100 on 2026-08-22. It said:
  5459  (4774)          %
  5460  (4775)          %     CHAT: no-wait ldn     38 status      0
  5461  (4776)          %
  5462  (4777)          % 38 is a REAL terminal LDN - the console banner for that line ends
  5463  (4778)          % "TERMINAL 7/ TET10 L:46 (38)" and the 38 in brackets is it. So
  5464  (4779)          % ExecutionInfo worked, the LDN is right, the io flag is right (the
  5465  (4780)          % manual: 0 for input), and the call is ACCEPTED - status 0, where the
  5466  (4781)          % same call with file number 1 had answered -1.
  5467  (4782)          %
  5468  (4783)          % AND MON1 STILL BLOCKS. Two clients, one left untouched: the welcome,
  5469  (4784)          % a joining notice and a line spoken from the other terminal all sat
  5470  (4785)          % queued and arrived together on one keystroke, exactly as before.
  5471  (4786)          %
  5472  (4787)          % So the parameters were genuinely wrong AND fixing them was not enough.
  5473  (4788)          % What remains unexplained is why an accepted no-wait switch changes
  5474  (4789)          % nothing about InByte on this machine. UNVERIFIED, and the honest state
  5475  (4790)          % of it: a plain SINTRAN call, accepted, with no observable effect.
  5476  (4791)          % Suspect the emulated terminal driver before suspecting the program -
  5477  (4792)          % but suspect nothing until it is measured, because guessing at this
  5478  (4793)          % call has already cost two days.
  5479  (4794)          %
  5480  (4795)          % The print is gone again: it fires on every start-up and says nothing
  5481  (4796)          % a user can act on. The numbers are here instead, with a date.
  5482  (4797)      ENDROUTINE
  5483  (4798)  
  5484  (4799)      % Putting it BACK is silent whatever happens. If the switch never went on,
  5485  (4800)      % switching it off is a no-op; if it did, this is the only thing standing
  5486  (4801)      % between the user and a shell whose terminal does not wait for input.
  5487  (4802)      % PUT IT BACK WITH THE NAME THAT ACTUALLY WORKS.
  5488  (4803)      %
  5489  (4804)      % This used to call NoWaitSwitch, which is REFUSED on this machine with
  5490  (4805)      % ErrCode -1 - so the restore silently did nothing while the switch-on had
  5491  (4806)      % quietly succeeded under the other name. A terminal left in no-wait is one
  5492  (4807)      % the user's shell has to live with, so the two must use the SAME name.
  5493  (4808)      % TerminalNoWait is tried first here for that reason, with NoWaitSwitch kept
  5494  (4809)      % only as a fallback for a machine where the other one is the live one.
  5495  (4810)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : terminalWait
  5496  (4811)          BOOLEAN : switchFailed
  5497  (4812)          INTEGER : nowaitStatus
  5498  (4813)  
  5499  (4814)          0 =: nowaitStatus
  5500  (4815)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5501  (4816)              TRUE =: switchFailed
  5502  (4817)          ENDON
  5503  (4818)          FALSE =: switchFailed
  5504  (4819)  
  5505  (4820)          MONITOR_CALL('TerminalNoWait', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOff, &
  5506  (4821)              nowaitStatus)
  5507  (4822)  
  5508  (4823)          IF switchFailed THEN
  5509  (4824)              ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5510  (4825)                  TRUE =: switchFailed
  5511  (4826)              ENDON
  5512  (4827)              FALSE =: switchFailed
  5513  (4828)              MONITOR_CALL('NoWaitSwitch', myLdn, inputFlag, noWaitOff)
  5514  (4829)          ENDIF
  5515  (4830)      ENDROUTINE
  5516  (4831)  
  5517  (4832)      % Give the machine back a moment. Called only when a pass found NOTHING -
  5518  (4833)      % no message waiting and no key pressed - so it never delays anything that
  5519  (4834)      % actually happened. Guarded for the same reason as the two above: a sleep
  5520  (4835)      % that cannot be had is not a reason to end the program.
  5521  (4836)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5522  (4837)      % Give the machine a moment when a pass found nothing.
  5523  (4838)      %
  5524  (4839)      % A GUARD THAT SETS A FLAG NOBODY READS IS NOT A GUARD, IT IS A SILENCER.
  5525  (4840)      % This routine set sleepFailed and never looked at it again, so a refused
  5526  (4841)      % TimeOut returned instantly and the main loop went round again with NO
  5527  (4842)      % SLEEP AT ALL - a SPIN against XMSG, invisible from the screen, the log and
  5528  (4843)      % the kernel tables alike. The comment beside sleepUnits already warns that
  5529  (4844)      % a bare loop here is the shape that burned the machine under CHATSV.
  5530  (4845)      %
  5531  (4846)      % That is exactly how GetUserName hid: guarded, refused, silent, and only
  5532  (4847)      % found because a diagnostic line was added on purpose. Every swallowed
  5533  (4848)      % failure in this program is a place the machine can be hurt without
  5534  (4849)      % anything saying so.
  5535  (4850)      %
  5536  (4851)      % SAID ONCE, NOT EVERY PASS. At a fifth of a second a per-pass message is a
  5537  (4852)      % flood that hides itself, so the first refusal speaks and the rest are
  5538  (4853)      % counted by their absence. If this line appears, the client has NO pacing
  5539  (4854)      % and should not be left running.
  5540  (4855)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5541  (4856)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : idleSleep
  5542  (4857)          BOOLEAN : sleepFailed
  5543  (4858)          ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  5544  (4859)              TRUE =: sleepFailed
  5545  (4860)          ENDON
  5546  (4861)          FALSE =: sleepFailed
  5547  (4862)  
  5548  (4863)          % Count this empty pass and pick the length from the run of them. The
  5549  (4864)          % counter is cleared by the main loop the moment anything happens, so
  5550  (4865)          % a busy client never reaches the long sleep at all.
  5551  (4866)          IF idleRuns < idleRunsMax THEN
  5552  (4867)              idleRuns + 1 =: idleRuns
  5553  (4868)          ENDIF
  5554  (4869)          IF idleRuns >= idleRunsMax THEN
  5555  (4870)              sleepLong =: sleepUnits
  5556  (4871)          ELSE
  5557  (4872)              sleepShort =: sleepUnits
  5558  (4873)          ENDIF
  5559  (4874)  
  5560  (4875)          MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', sleepUnits, sleepKind, sleepWhy)
  5561  (4876)  
  5562  (4877)          IF sleepFailed AND NOT sleepBroken THEN
  5563  (4878)              TRUE =: sleepBroken
  5564  (4879)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL43', '$WARNING: cannot sleep - this client SPINS$')
  5565  (4880)          ENDIF
  5566  (4881)      ENDROUTINE
  5567  (4882)  
  5568  (4883)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : drainPort
  5569  (4884)          XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
  5570  (4885)          INTEGER : taken
  5571  (4886)          % readStatus - 2026-09-01. returnStatus is one shared variable
  5572  (4887)          % reused by xmpfrcv, xmpfmst, xmpfrea and xmpfrel in that order, so
  5573  (4888)          % by the time anything downstream could look at it, xmpfrel has
  5574  (4889)          % already overwritten whatever xmpfrea itself answered. Nothing
  5575  (4890)          % checked the READ succeeding before trusting what it read into.
  5576  (4891)          INTEGER : readStatus
  5577  (4892)  $IF DBGUI = 1 $THEN
  5578       .          % Instrument locals - see dbgShow beside slotAt.
  5579       .          INTEGER : dbgAt, dbgT0
  5580       .  $ENDIF
  5581  (4896)  
  5582  (4897)          % THIS LOOP IS BOUNDED, and that is not caution for its own sake - the
  5583  (4898)          % unbounded version hung the client on the first live run. It kept being
  5584  (4899)          % handed a message, printed it, and asked again for ever; the terminal
  5585  (4900)          % filled with the same line until ESC broke it. Whether the message was
  5586  (4901)          % never really released or the peer kept re-offering it is not settled,
  5587  (4902)          % but either way a loop that only ends when the PEER says so is a loop
  5588  (4903)          % the peer can hang. One pass takes at most this many and then returns
  5589  (4904)          % to the keyboard; anything still waiting is taken on the next pass.
  5590  (4905)          0 =: taken
  5591  (4906)  
  5592  (4907)          DO
  5593  (4908)              xmpfrcv(0, myPort, msgType, remotePortHash, msgIdent, nBytes) &
  5594  (4909)                  =: returnStatus
  5595  (4910)  
  5596  (4911)              % XMOK IS A SUCCESSFUL RECEIVE. THIS TEST MUST ACCEPT ZERO.
  5597  (4912)              %
  5598  (4913)              % It did not, and that single missing case is what silently ate the
  5599  (4914)              % welcome. MEASURED on D100 2026-08-18, end to end:
  5600  (4915)              %
  5601  (4916)              %   the server prints  SV: rcv st=  0  t=  2  len= 25
  5602  (4917)              %                                  ^ XMOK, with the type in msgType
  5603  (4918)              %
  5604  (4919)              %   the client's port  LIST-PORTS: port 13  Qhead 161641  Qlen 1
  5605  (4920)              %   one keystroke later                     Qhead 0       Qlen 0
  5606  (4921)              %   and the client printed NOTHING - not even the unconditional
  5607  (4922)              %   "type= / rel=" line below, which every taken message reaches.
  5608  (4923)              %
  5609  (4924)              % That is the whole story: XMPFRCV succeeded, returned XMOK, and
  5610  (4925)              % DEQUEUED the message - which is why Qlen fell to zero - and then
  5611  (4926)              % this continue-test read 0 >= XMTNO as false and left the loop
  5612  (4927)              % before a single byte was printed, before XMPFMST, before XMPFREA,
  5613  (4928)              % and before XMPFREL. The message was received and abandoned. It was
  5614  (4929)              % never returned to the sender either, which is why the server saw
  5615  (4930)              % no bounce.
  5616  (4931)              %
  5617  (4932)              % THE OLD COMMENT HERE ARGUED THE OPPOSITE and was wrong. It claimed
  5618  (4933)              % "a successful receive does not return zero", on the reasoning that
  5619  (4934)              % 0 means OK, 16896 means an empty port, and a bare "> 0" test is
  5620  (4935)              % true for both - all of which is correct ABOUT THE OTHER CALLS and
  5621  (4936)              % does not follow for XMPFRCV. The vendor sample in Appendix G tests
  5622  (4937)              % "IF RETURNSTATUS >< XMOK THEN bad status" and only then looks at
  5623  (4938)              % MSGTYPE, so XMOK is the success case and the TYPE lives in the
  5624  (4939)              % out-parameter, not in the status.
  5625  (4940)              %
  5626  (4941)              % The earlier "type=0 rel=16902" reading that seemed to prove the
  5627  (4942)              % old rule was an EMPTY port being read and then released - a real
  5628  (4943)              % fault, but a different one, and fixing it by excluding zero threw
  5629  (4944)              % out every genuine message with it.
  5630  (4945)              %
  5631  (4946)              % CHATSV was widened for exactly this reason and this client never
  5632  (4947)              % was. Both sides now spell the same rule, and the range is kept
  5633  (4948)              % beside XMOK rather than replaced by it, because which form turns
  5634  (4949)              % up is measured rather than assumed.
  5635  (4950)              %
  5636  (4951)              % 16896 (XMXENTM, "not terminated") is what an EMPTY port answers
  5637  (4952)              % and is still correctly excluded - it is neither XMOK nor 1..6.
  5638  (4953)              WHILE returnStatus = XMOK OR &
  5639  (4954)                    (returnStatus >= XMTNO AND returnStatus <= XMTPS)
  5640  (4955)  
  5641  (4956)              taken + 1 =: taken
  5642  (4957)              WHILE taken <= maxDrain
  5643  (4958)  
  5644  (4959)              xmpfmst(0, msgIdent, msgType, senderMagic, nBytes) =: returnStatus
  5645  (4960)  
  5646  (4961)              % CLAMP BEFORE READING. nBytes is the SENDER'S message size, handed
  5647  (4962)              % back by XMSG - not a size this program chose. inBuf holds
  5648  (4963)              % inBufSize bytes and PLANC checks no array bound, so passing nBytes
  5649  (4964)              % straight in lets anyone who sends a longer message write straight
  5650  (4965)              % past the end of it. That is a WRITE overflow and it happens before
  5651  (4966)              % a single field is parsed, so none of the checks in showArrived
  5652  (4967)              % could save it.
  5653  (4968)              nBytes =: readAmount
  5654  (4969)              IF readAmount > inBufSize THEN
  5655  (4970)                  inBufSize =: readAmount
  5656  (4971)              ENDIF
  5657  (4972)  
  5658  (4973)              xmpfrea(0, 0, &
  5659  (4974)                  ADDR(inBuf(0)) FORCE XMUSERADDRESS, &
  5660  (4975)                  0, readAmount, readLength) =: returnStatus
  5661  (4976)              returnStatus =: readStatus
  5662  (4977)              % A RETURN MESSAGE IS OUR OWN LETTER COMING BACK UNDELIVERED.
  5663  (4978)              % XMTRE is the type XMSG uses for "abnormal condition", and the
  5664  (4979)              % commonest reason a join bounces is that the peer does not have us
  5665  (4980)              % as a friend - the same fault XRUNN and XRNRO report from the
  5666  (4981)              % other direction. Saying so here means the user is told once,
  5667  (4982)              % whichever way the machine chooses to complain.
  5668  (4983)              IF msgType = XMTRE THEN
  5669  (4984)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL40', 'that message came BACK - undeliverable.$')
  5670  (4985)                  showFriendAdvice
  5671  (4986)              ENDIF
  5672  (4987)  
  5673  (4988)              xmpfrel(0, msgIdent) =: returnStatus
  5674  (4989)  
  5675  (4990)              % A "type= rel=" line used to print here on every message taken.
  5676  (4991)              % It was temporary, it said so, and it has done its job: the loop
  5677  (4992)              % that printed the same message for ever is understood and fixed
  5678  (4993)              % (see the ROUTINEERROR handler in the main loop), and the empty
  5679  (4994)              % port being read and released - the "type=0 rel=16902" reading -
  5680  (4995)              % is excluded by the continue-test above.
  5681  (4996)              %
  5682  (4997)              % What it proved is kept in the comments that surround it rather
  5683  (4998)              % than on the user's screen. Put it back if this loop ever misbehaves
  5684  (4999)              % again: msgType and returnStatus are the two numbers that say why.
  5685  (5000)  
  5686  (5001)              % ---- THE HISTORY HEADER COMES OFF HERE, AND ONLY HERE ------
  5687  (5002)              %
  5688  (5003)              % READ THE TIME FIRST: trStrip slides the header away and the
  5689  (5004)              % bytes are gone afterwards, exactly as with the relay fields.
  5690  (5005)              %
  5691  (5006)              % ONCE, at the arrival, NOT in the renderers. trStrip leaves the
  5692  (5007)              % KIND byte in place, so a second call on the same buffer would
  5693  (5008)              % happily strip another seven bytes - and there are two
  5694  (5009)              % renderers, either of which may run. One call here cannot get
  5695  (5010)              % that wrong.
  5696  (5011)              %
  5697  (5012)              % trStrip answers the length unchanged for a kind with no header,
  5698  (5013)              % so this is safe to do for every message without a test.
  5699  (5014)              FALSE =: msgIsHist
  5700  (5015)              TRUE =: timeKnown
  5701  (5016)              IF cmKind(inBuf) = kHist THEN
  5702  (5017)                  TRUE =: msgIsHist
  5703  (5018)                  hsHour(inBuf) =: histHour
  5704  (5019)                  hsMin(inBuf) =: histMin
  5705  (5020)                  IF hsYear(inBuf) = 0 THEN
  5706  (5021)                      FALSE =: timeKnown
  5707  (5022)                  ENDIF
  5708  (5023)              ENDIF
  5709  (5024)  
  5710  (5025)              trStrip(inBuf, readLength, cmKind(inBuf)) =: readLength
  5711  (5026)  
  5712  (5027)              % -1 MEANS THE MESSAGE WAS TOO SHORT FOR THE HEADER IT CLAIMED.
  5713  (5028)              % Dropped rather than shown: reading on would take the middle of
  5714  (5029)              % a truncated timestamp as a name length and build a plausible
  5715  (5030)              % line out of rubbish, which is worse than losing one.
  5716  (5031)              IF readLength < 0 THEN
  5717  (5032)                  0 =: readLength
  5718  (5033)              ENDIF
  5719  (5034)  
  5720  (5035)              % THE POINT OF readStatus. A read that did not actually
  5721  (5036)              % succeed has not put anything new in inBuf - what is there is
  5722  (5037)              % whatever the LAST successful read left, and parsing it again
  5723  (5038)              % is how one real message would come back as every arrival
  5724  (5039)              % after it. Nothing to show is the honest answer to a failed
  5725  (5040)              % read, not the last thing that worked.
  5726  (5041)              IF readStatus = XMOK AND readLength > 0 THEN
  5727  (5042)  $IF DBGUI = 1 $THEN
  5728       .                  % The instrument - capture the arrival's first text byte
  5729       .                  % BEFORE showArrived touches anything, paint after.
  5730       .                  % See dbgShow beside slotAt.
  5731       .                  cmTxAt(inBuf) =: dbgAt
  5732       .                  0 =: dbgT0
  5733       .                  IF dbgAt >= 0 AND dbgAt < 256 THEN
  5734       .                      inBuf(dbgAt) =: dbgT0
  5735       .                  ENDIF
  5736       .  $ENDIF
  5737  (5052)                  showArrived
  5738  (5053)  $IF DBGUI = 1 $THEN
  5739       .                  dbgShow(cmKind(inBuf), readLength, dbgT0)
  5740       .  $ENDIF
  5741  (5056)              ENDIF
  5742  (5057)          ENDDO
  5743  (5058)  
  5744  (5059)          % How many this pass printed, for the main loop's idle test. Nothing
  5745  (5060)          % else reads it, and it is a COUNT rather than a boolean so a future
  5746  (5061)          % question like "are we falling behind" has a number to look at.
  5747  (5062)          taken =: lastDrainCount
  5748  (5063)  
  5749  (5064)          % ---- DID THE PORT GO AWAY WHILE WE WERE NOT LOOKING? ---------------
  5750  (5065)          % The loop above ends on ANY status that is not a message, and until now
  5751  (5066)          % it ended silently on all of them. That is right for XMXENTM, which
  5752  (5067)          % just means the port is empty and is the normal way round; it is wrong
  5753  (5068)          % for XMXENRU, "XMSG is not running", because the client then sits at
  5754  (5069)          % its prompt looking perfectly connected to a room that no longer
  5755  (5070)          % exists, and everything typed goes nowhere.
  5756  (5071)          %
  5757  (5072)          % MEASURED 2026-08-18: XMSG went down under a running server. The
  5758  (5073)          % SERVER span at full speed on the same status (it waits with XFWTF, so
  5759  (5074)          % a receive that cannot block returns instantly for ever); this client
  5760  (5075)          % would not have spun, because its drain never waits - it would just
  5761  (5076)          % have gone quiet and told nobody. Two different failures from one
  5762  (5077)          % cause, which is why both programs needed their own answer.
  5763  (5078)          %
  5764  (5079)          % Stopping is the honest one here. There is no reconnect to attempt: a
  5765  (5080)          % port belongs to an XMSG that is gone, and when it comes back this
  5766  (5081)          % program has to open a new one and rejoin from the beginning - which
  5767  (5082)          % is exactly what starting it again does.
  5768  (5083)          IF returnStatus = XMXENRU THEN
  5769  (5084)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'XMSG has stopped - leaving$')
  5770  (5085)              FALSE =: joined
  5771  (5086)              0 =: serverMagic
  5772  (5087)              FALSE =: running
  5773  (5088)          ENDIF
  5774  (5089)      ENDROUTINE
  5775  (5090)  
  5776  (5091)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5777  (5092)      % Does the typed line begin with "/word"? Case is folded, so /WHO and /who
  5778  (5093)      % are the same command.
  5779  (5094)      %
  5780  (5095)      % typed(0) is the slash, so the word starts at 1. A match also requires the
  5781  (5096)      % character AFTER the word to be a space or the end of the line - without
  5782  (5097)      % that test "/nickname" would match "/nick" and the rest be read as an
  5783  (5098)      % argument.
  5784  (5099)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5785  (5100)      % THE LENGTH IS NOT A PARAMETER - THE KEYWORD IS ASKED FOR IT.
  5786  (5101)      %
  5787  (5102)      % Every caller passes a literal, and each one used to hand the count in
  5788  (5103)      % beside it. Rename a command, or add one, and the number beside it is
  5789  (5104)      % silently wrong: the build is clean and the match then runs over the wrong
  5790  (5105)      % number of characters, so /nick could start matching /nickname or stop
  5791  (5106)      % matching at all. The same trap as an 'ALn' field width.
  5792  (5107)      %
  5793  (5108)      % MAXINDEX(word, 1) is the DECLARED UPPER BOUND and a string literal
  5794  (5109)      % subscripts from ZERO, so the length is that plus one.
  5795  (5110)      ROUTINE VOID, INTEGER (BYTES) : cmdIs(word)
  5796  (5111)          INTEGER : i, c, w, wordLen
  5797  (5112)          MAXINDEX(word, 1) + 1 =: wordLen
  5798  (5113)  
  5799  (5114)          IF typedLen < wordLen + 1 THEN
  5800  (5115)              0 RETURN
  5801  (5116)          ENDIF
  5802  (5117)  
  5803  (5118)          FOR i IN 1:wordLen DO
  5804  (5119)              typed(i) =: c
  5805  (5120)              % Fold lower case to upper: 'a' is 97, 'z' is 122.
  5806  (5121)              IF c >= 97 AND c <= 122 THEN
  5807  (5122)                  c - 32 =: c
  5808  (5123)              ENDIF
  5809  (5124)              word(i - 1) =: w
  5810  (5125)              IF c >< w THEN
  5811  (5126)                  0 RETURN
  5812  (5127)              ENDIF
  5813  (5128)          ENDFOR
  5814  (5129)  
  5815  (5130)          % The word must END here - the next character is a space or nothing.
  5816  (5131)          IF typedLen = wordLen + 1 THEN
  5817  (5132)              1 RETURN
  5818  (5133)          ENDIF
  5819  (5134)  
  5820  (5135)          IF typed(wordLen + 1) = 32 THEN
  5821  (5136)              1 RETURN
  5822  (5137)          ENDIF
  5823  (5138)  
  5824  (5139)          0 RETURN
  5825  (5140)      ENDROUTINE
  5826  (5141)  
  5827  (5142)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5828  (5143)      % A line beginning with "/" is a command, not something to say.
  5829  (5144)      %
  5830  (5145)      % WHAT IS AND IS NOT IMPLEMENTED, and the difference matters. /nick, /leave
  5831  (5146)      % and /quit map onto message kinds that ALREADY EXIST on the wire - kRename
  5832  (5147)      % and kLeave - and /join re-sends the join CHAT already sends at startup, to
  5833  (5148)      % a different room name. None of those invent anything.
  5834  (5149)      %
  5835  (5150)      % /who does NOT map onto anything. There is no query kind in the format, and
  5836  (5151)      % the format is pinned byte-for-byte by ChatMessageGoldenBytesTests on the
  5837  (5152)      % C# side, so adding one is a change to BOTH ends in the same commit - not
  5838  (5153)      % something this file may do on its own. It says so rather than pretending.
  5839  (5154)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  5840  (5155)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : handleCommand
  5841  (5156)          INTEGER : at, argLen, i, length
  5842  (5157)          % Where the name ends and the message begins in a /tell.
  5843  (5158)          INTEGER : cut
  5844  (5159)  
  5845  (5160)          IF cmdIs('HELP') = 1 THEN
  5846  (5161)              % ON A SCREEN THE HELP IS A WINDOW, and it is built from the same
  5847  (5162)              % table the line renderer prints below - so the two can never say
  5848  (5163)              % different things, and the window is measured from the text
  5849  (5164)              % rather than sized by a number somebody has to remember to update.
  5850  (5165)              IF screenOn THEN
  5851  (5166)                  buildHelp
  5852  (5167)                  drawPanel
  5853  (5168)                  flush
  5854  (5169)                  RETURN
  5855  (5170)              ENDIF
  5856  (5171)  
  5857  (5172)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL15', 'CHAT commands:$')
  5858  (5173)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL11', 'you are in ')
  5859  (5174)              showRoom
  5860  (5175)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  5861  (5176)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  /join <room>   join another room, short name$')
  5862  (5177)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL38', '  /nick <name>   change your nickname$')
  5863  (5178)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', '  /who           who is in this room$')
  5864  (5179)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL45', '  /list          rooms, and how many in each$')
  5865  (5180)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL47', '  /map           the machines and their trunks$')
  5866  (5181)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', '  /topic         show the room topic$')
  5867  (5182)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', '  /topic <text>  set it$')
  5868  (5183)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '  /tell <name> <text>  a message to one person$')
  5869  (5184)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', '  /leave         leave the room$')
  5870  (5185)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '  /exit          Exit chat program$')
  5871  (5186)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL36', '  anything else is said to the room$')
  5872  (5187)              RETURN
  5873  (5188)          ENDIF
  5874  (5189)  
  5875  (5190)          % /who - ask the room for its membership list.
  5876  (5191)          %
  5877  (5192)          % This used to print three lines saying the format had no kind for it.
  5878  (5193)          % It has one now: kWho = 11, added to ChatMessageKind.cs, CHATSV.PLNC
  5879  (5194)          % and here together, which is what those lines said the change needed.
  5880  (5195)          % The answer arrives later as an ordinary message and is printed by
  5881  (5196)          % showArrived - nothing waits here.
  5882  (5197)          % /who - who is in my room, or, WITH NO ROOM, who is anywhere.
  5883  (5198)          %
  5884  (5199)          % SENT EITHER WAY, and the same kind either way. This used to refuse
  5885  (5200)          % with "not in a room yet - wait or /join", which was the client
  5886  (5201)          % answering a question it is not the one that knows. The SERVER knows
  5887  (5202)          % whether we hold a seat, so it picks: kWho back if we do, kAllWho -
  5888  (5203)          % every room and everyone in it - if we do not.
  5889  (5204)          %
  5890  (5205)          % Ronny asked for exactly that on 2026-08-22, and it is the right shape
  5891  (5206)          % for the same reason /list is allowed unjoined: somebody with no room
  5892  (5207)          % is precisely the person who needs to be told where everybody is.
  5893  (5208)          %
  5894  (5209)          % THIS NEEDS serverMagic TO SURVIVE LEAVING, and it did not until the
  5895  (5210)          % same day. /leave used to zero it along with the joined flag, so this
  5896  (5211)          % question had no address to go to and printed nothing at all - the
  5897  (5212)          % server was answering correctly and the letter never left. The address
  5898  (5213)          % of the server and whether we hold a seat are two different facts; see
  5899  (5214)          % the note in the LEAVE branch.
  5900  (5215)          % ASKED BY HAND, so the answer is for this person and belongs in a
  5901  (5216)          % window rather than in everybody's room.
  5902  (5217)          IF cmdIs('WHO') = 1 THEN
  5903  (5218)              TRUE =: askedWho
  5904  (5219)              buildOwn(kWho, 0) =: length
  5905  (5220)              sendToServer(length)
  5906  (5221)              RETURN
  5907  (5222)          ENDIF
  5908  (5223)  
  5909  (5224)          % /list - which rooms exist and how many are in each.
  5910  (5225)          %
  5911  (5226)          % ALLOWED WHILE UNJOINED, unlike /who. "What rooms are there" is exactly
  5912  (5227)          % the question somebody asks when they have just been refused a name and
  5913  (5228)          % are deciding where to go, and refusing to answer it then would be
  5914  (5229)          % refusing at the one moment it is useful. The server needs no seat to
  5915  (5230)          % answer - it walks the seats it already has.
  5916  (5231)          IF cmdIs('LIST') = 1 THEN
  5917  (5232)              buildOwn(kRooms, 0) =: length
  5918  (5233)              sendToServer(length)
  5919  (5234)              RETURN
  5920  (5235)          ENDIF
  5921  (5236)  
  5922  (5237)          % /map - which machines there are and which trunks are up.
  5923  (5238)          %
  5924  (5239)          % ALLOWED WHILE UNJOINED, same as /list. "Is the other machine even
  5925  (5240)          % there?" is exactly the question somebody asks when the room looks
  5926  (5241)          % empty, and that is often when they have no seat either.
  5927  (5242)          %
  5928  (5243)          % READ-ONLY. There is deliberately no way to change a trunk from here -
  5929  (5244)          % START-TRUNK and STOP-TRUNK live in CHAT-MON, on the admin port, and
  5930  (5245)          % that separation is the whole reason /map is a room kind.
  5931  (5246)          IF cmdIs('MAP') = 1 THEN
  5932  (5247)              buildOwn(kMap, 0) =: length
  5933  (5248)              sendToServer(length)
  5934  (5249)              RETURN
  5935  (5250)          ENDIF
  5936  (5251)  
  5937  (5252)          % /topic          show this room's topic
  5938  (5253)          % /topic <text>   set it, and tell everybody in the room
  5939  (5254)          %
  5940  (5255)          % Needs a room, because a topic belongs to one. The server refuses a
  5941  (5256)          % stranger in silence, so asking while unjoined would simply do nothing
  5942  (5257)          % at all - say why instead.
  5943  (5258)          IF cmdIs('TOPIC') = 1 THEN
  5944  (5259)              IF NOT joined THEN
  5945  (5260)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'not in a room yet - wait or /join$')
  5946  (5261)                  RETURN
  5947  (5262)              ENDIF
  5948  (5263)  
  5949  (5264)              % 7 = past "/TOPIC ". If the line is exactly "/TOPIC" there is no
  5950  (5265)              % space and typedLen is 6, so argLen comes out negative - hence
  5951  (5266)              % the "< 1" test rather than "= 0".
  5952  (5267)              7 =: at
  5953  (5268)              typedLen - at =: argLen
  5954  (5269)              IF argLen < 1 THEN
  5955  (5270)                  % ASKING, not setting. An empty text is the question - and
  5956  (5271)                  % remembering that we asked is the only way to tell OUR answer
  5957  (5272)                  % from a topic somebody else changed, because both arrive as
  5958  (5273)                  % the same kind.
  5959  (5274)                  TRUE =: askedTopic
  5960  (5275)                  buildOwn(kTopic, 0) =: length
  5961  (5276)                  sendToServer(length)
  5962  (5277)                  RETURN
  5963  (5278)              ENDIF
  5964  (5279)  
  5965  (5280)              % Bounded to what the server will store, and said out loud rather
  5966  (5281)              % than quietly clipped - somebody who typed a long topic should not
  5967  (5282)              % have to work out for themselves where it was cut.
  5968  (5283)              IF argLen > maxTopicLen THEN
  5969  (5284)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL32', 'that topic is too long (max 64)$')
  5970  (5285)                  RETURN
  5971  (5286)              ENDIF
  5972  (5287)  
  5973  (5288)              buildOwnFrom(kTopic, at, argLen) =: length
  5974  (5289)              sendToServer(length)
  5975  (5290)              RETURN
  5976  (5291)          ENDIF
  5977  (5292)  
  5978  (5293)          IF cmdIs('JOIN') = 1 THEN
  5979  (5294)              6 =: at
  5980  (5295)              typedLen - at =: argLen
  5981  (5296)              IF argLen < 1 THEN
  5982  (5297)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL24', '/join needs a room name$')
  5983  (5298)                  RETURN
  5984  (5299)              ENDIF
  5985  (5300)              IF argLen > maxNameLen THEN
  5986  (5301)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL27', 'that room name is too long$')
  5987  (5302)                  RETURN
  5988  (5303)              ENDIF
  5989  (5304)  
  5990  (5305)              % LEAVE THE OLD ROOM FIRST. Without this the old room still counts
  5991  (5306)              % us as a member and still holds our seat: it never hears that we
  5992  (5307)              % went, and a room only gives a seat back on a clean leave or on a
  5993  (5308)              % reap. Two or three /joins and the room is full of people who are
  5994  (5309)              % not there. The reported "/join doesn't work" hid this one - the
  5995  (5310)              % join never reached a room, so the leak never had a chance to show.
  5996  (5311)              IF joined THEN
  5997  (5312)                  buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
  5998  (5313)                  sendToServer(length)
  5999  (5314)              ENDIF
  6000  (5315)  
  6001  (5316)              % Re-point the room and send the join again. setRoom adds the
  6002  (5317)              % "CHAT-" prefix; the raw copy that stood here is the whole of the
  6003  (5318)              % /join defect.
  6004  (5319)              setRoom(at, argLen)
  6005  (5320)              FALSE =: joined
  6006  (5321)              0 =: serverMagic
  6007  (5322)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', 'joining ')
  6008  (5323)              showRoom
  6009  (5324)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', '...$')
  6010  (5325)              sendJoin
  6011  (5326)              RETURN
  6012  (5327)          ENDIF
  6013  (5328)  
  6014  (5329)          IF cmdIs('NICK') = 1 THEN
  6015  (5330)              6 =: at
  6016  (5331)              typedLen - at =: argLen
  6017  (5332)              IF argLen < 1 THEN
  6018  (5333)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL19', '/nick needs a name$')
  6019  (5334)                  RETURN
  6020  (5335)              ENDIF
  6021  (5336)              IF argLen > maxNameLen THEN
  6022  (5337)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL22', 'that name is too long$')
  6023  (5338)                  RETURN
  6024  (5339)              ENDIF
  6025  (5340)              % NOT JOINED? THEN THIS IS NOT A RENAME - IT IS A RETRY.
  6026  (5341)              %
  6027  (5342)              % This used to answer "not in the room yet" and stop, which
  6028  (5343)              % left a refused user with NO WAY OUT: the server turns a
  6029  (5344)              % duplicate nickname away, and the client then refused the one
  6030  (5345)              % command that could fix it. The only escape was to quit and
  6031  (5346)              % edit CHAT:CNFG by hand, which no end user will do.
  6032  (5347)              % MEASURED 2026-08-20 with two terminals on one SINTRAN user:
  6033  (5348)              % both read the same CHAT:CNFG, both wanted the same name, and
  6034  (5349)              % the second was stuck.
  6035  (5350)              %
  6036  (5351)              % Both halves were individually right - the server should
  6037  (5352)              % refuse a duplicate, and a rename normally IS a message to a
  6038  (5353)              % room. The combination was the trap.
  6039  (5354)              %
  6040  (5355)              % With no room to tell, changing the name is purely local:
  6041  (5356)              % take the new name and knock again.
  6042  (5357)              % FROM HERE ON, ANY REFUSAL IS ABOUT A NAME THE USER PICKED.
  6043  (5358)              %
  6044  (5359)              % Set BEFORE the name is even validated, because the intent is what
  6045  (5360)              % matters: once somebody has typed /nick, "that name is taken" means
  6046  (5361)              % somebody else has it - NOT that they are in the room twice. See the
  6047  (5362)              % kReject handler, which switches its whole message on this.
  6048  (5363)              TRUE =: userChoseNick
  6049  (5364)  
  6050  (5365)              % DRAIN FIRST, THEN DECIDE. A WELCOME MAY BE SITTING UNREAD.
  6051  (5366)              %
  6052  (5367)              % MEASURED 2026-08-22 with the server running: typing /nick straight
  6053  (5368)              % after starting the client printed
  6054  (5369)              %
  6055  (5370)              %     joining (LOBBY) as SYSTEM
  6056  (5371)              %     trying TESTER
  6057  (5372)              %     you are TESTER  (/nick to change)
  6058  (5373)              %     cannot join as TESTER - already joined
  6059  (5374)              %
  6060  (5375)              % "trying" is printed by the retry branch BELOW, so that branch ran -
  6061  (5376)              % which means `joined` was still FALSE. It was false only because the
  6062  (5377)              % first join's kWelcome had not been PROCESSED yet; `joined` is set
  6063  (5378)              % where kWelcome is handled. So the client sent a SECOND join and the
  6064  (5379)              % server refused it, correctly, as already joined.
  6065  (5380)              %
  6066  (5381)              % Nothing was wrong with either branch. The state they switch on was
  6067  (5382)              % simply out of date, because the welcome was still in the port. That
  6068  (5383)              % is easy to hit here: the client only renders new lines when RETURN
  6069  (5384)              % is pressed, so a welcome can be waiting while the user is typing.
  6070  (5385)              %
  6071  (5386)              % One drain pass costs nothing and settles it. It is deliberately
  6072  (5387)              % BEFORE the NOT joined test rather than inside it - the same staleness
  6073  (5388)              % would make a rename look like a retry OR a retry look like a rename.
  6074  (5389)              drainPort
  6075  (5390)  
  6076  (5391)              IF NOT joined THEN
  6077  (5392)                  setMyName(at, argLen)
  6078  (5393)  
  6079  (5394)                  % "TRYING", NOT "YOU ARE" - WE ARE NOT THAT NAME YET. This is a
  6080  (5395)                  % retry: the name goes into a fresh join and the SERVER decides.
  6081  (5396)                  % Announcing it here and then printing the server's refusal two
  6082  (5397)                  % lines later is what put
  6083  (5398)                  %     you are ANNA
  6084  (5399)                  %     OLAV: that nickname is taken
  6085  (5400)                  % on the screen - the client claiming a name, the server denying
  6086  (5401)                  % a different one, and nothing to connect the two.
  6087  (5402)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL7', 'trying ')
  6088  (5403)                  showMyName
  6089  (5404)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  6090  (5405)                  sendJoin
  6091  (5406)                  RETURN
  6092  (5407)              ENDIF
  6093  (5408)  
  6094  (5409)              % THE NEW NAME GOES IN THE NAME FIELD - see buildRename, which is
  6095  (5410)              % where the whole story of this being wrong the other way round is
  6096  (5411)              % written down. It reads the name straight out of typed, so there is
  6097  (5412)              % no shuffling to do here either.
  6098  (5413)              buildRename(at, argLen) =: length
  6099  (5414)              sendToServer(length)
  6100  (5415)  
  6101  (5416)              % Our own name changes only when the room says so, with kRenOk.
  6102  (5417)              RETURN
  6103  (5418)          ENDIF
  6104  (5419)  
  6105  (5420)          % /TELL NAME what to say - a message to ONE person.
  6106  (5421)          %
  6107  (5422)          % A SEAT IS REQUIRED, and not because the other person has to be in a
  6108  (5423)          % room - they do not, and that is half the point of the feature. It is
  6109  (5424)          % because the SENDER needs a name for the message to be from, which is
  6110  (5425)          % the same reason the server refuses this below its own seat test.
  6111  (5426)          % /w N - go to a window. Also /window.
  6112  (5427)          %
  6113  (5428)          % A COMMAND AS WELL AS A KEY, because the key cycles and cycling past
  6114  (5429)          % the one you want is annoying with four open. The command is also the
  6115  (5430)          % only form that works on a printing terminal, which has no bar to
  6116  (5431)          % look at but can still hold a conversation.
  6117  (5432)          IF cmdIs('W') = 1 OR cmdIs('WINDOW') = 1 THEN
  6118  (5433)              IF NOT screenOn THEN
  6119  (5434)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'windows need a screen terminal$')
  6120  (5435)                  RETURN
  6121  (5436)              ENDIF
  6122  (5437)  
  6123  (5438)              % The digit is the last character typed, so a bare /w is a cycle
  6124  (5439)              % rather than an error - which is what a person who typed it
  6125  (5440)              % without thinking almost certainly meant.
  6126  (5441)              IF typedLen < 3 THEN
  6127  (5442)                  winNext
  6128  (5443)                  RETURN
  6129  (5444)              ENDIF
  6130  (5445)  
  6131  (5446)              typed(typedLen - 1) - 48 =: at
  6132  (5447)              IF at < 1 THEN
  6133  (5448)                  winNext
  6134  (5449)                  RETURN
  6135  (5450)              ENDIF
  6136  (5451)              IF at > maxWins THEN
  6137  (5452)                  winNext
  6138  (5453)                  RETURN
  6139  (5454)              ENDIF
  6140  (5455)              winGo(at)
  6141  (5456)              RETURN
  6142  (5457)          ENDIF
  6143  (5458)  
  6144  (5459)          % /close - put a conversation window away.
  6145  (5460)          %
  6146  (5461)          % THE ROOM CANNOT BE CLOSED. It is where a plain line goes when no
  6147  (5462)          % conversation is open, and a client with no window at all would have
  6148  (5463)          % nowhere to put an arriving room line.
  6149  (5464)          IF cmdIs('CLOSE') = 1 THEN
  6150  (5465)              IF curWin = 1 THEN
  6151  (5466)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'the room window stays open$')
  6152  (5467)                  RETURN
  6153  (5468)              ENDIF
  6154  (5469)  
  6155  (5470)              0 =: winUsed(curWin)
  6156  (5471)              0 =: winCount(curWin)
  6157  (5472)              0 =: winLabLen(curWin)
  6158  (5473)              winGo(1)
  6159  (5474)  
  6160  (5475)              % THE BAR HAS LOST AN ENTRY even if winGo decided it had nothing
  6161  (5476)              % to do, so it is redrawn here - and only it. Nothing else on the
  6162  (5477)              % screen changed when a window went away.
  6163  (5478)              IF screenOn THEN
  6164  (5479)                  drawBar
  6165  (5480)                  drawInput
  6166  (5481)                  flush
  6167  (5482)              ENDIF
  6168  (5483)              RETURN
  6169  (5484)          ENDIF
  6170  (5485)  
  6171  (5486)          IF cmdIs('TELL') = 1 THEN
  6172  (5487)              IF NOT joined THEN
  6173  (5488)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL34', 'not in a room yet - wait or /join$')
  6174  (5489)                  RETURN
  6175  (5490)              ENDIF
  6176  (5491)  
  6177  (5492)              % 6 = past "/TELL ". Exactly "/TELL" is typedLen 5, so argLen comes
  6178  (5493)              % out negative - hence "< 1" rather than "= 0".
  6179  (5494)              6 =: at
  6180  (5495)              typedLen - at =: argLen
  6181  (5496)              IF argLen < 1 THEN
  6182  (5497)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL', '/tell NAME what to say - or D102!NAME$')
  6183  (5498)                  RETURN
  6184  (5499)              ENDIF
  6185  (5500)  
  6186  (5501)              % THE FIRST SPACE SEPARATES THE NAME FROM THE MESSAGE.
  6187  (5502)              0 - 1 =: cut
  6188  (5503)              FOR i IN 0:argLen - 1 DO
  6189  (5504)                  IF cut < 0 THEN
  6190  (5505)                      IF typed(at + i) = spaceByte(0) THEN
  6191  (5506)                          i =: cut
  6192  (5507)                      ENDIF
  6193  (5508)                  ENDIF
  6194  (5509)              ENDFOR
  6195  (5510)  
  6196  (5511)              IF cut < 1 THEN
  6197  (5512)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'who to? /tell NAME what to say$')
  6198  (5513)                  RETURN
  6199  (5514)              ENDIF
  6200  (5515)              IF argLen - cut - 1 < 1 THEN
  6201  (5516)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'nothing to say to them$')
  6202  (5517)                  RETURN
  6203  (5518)              ENDIF
  6204  (5519)  
  6205  (5520)              % A SLASH IN THE NAME WOULD SPLIT IN THE WRONG PLACE at the far
  6206  (5521)              % end, and the message would go to a name nobody has. Said out
  6207  (5522)              % loud rather than sent and lost.
  6208  (5523)              FOR i IN 0:cut - 1 DO
  6209  (5524)                  IF typed(at + i) = slashByte(0) THEN
  6210  (5525)                      OUTPUT(1, 'AL', 'a name cannot have a slash in it$')
  6211  (5526)                      RETURN
  6212  (5527)                  ENDIF
  6213  (5528)              ENDFOR
  6214  (5529)  
  6215  (5530)              % KEPT BEFORE IT IS SENT, so the confirmation has something to
  6216  (5531)              % show. See lastTell.
  6217  (5532)              argLen - cut - 1 =: lastTellLen
  6218  (5533)              IF lastTellLen > 76 THEN
  6219  (5534)                  76 =: lastTellLen
  6220  (5535)              ENDIF
  6221  (5536)              FOR i IN 1:lastTellLen DO
  6222  (5537)                  typed(at + cut + i) =: lastTell(i - 1)
  6223  (5538)              ENDFOR
  6224  (5539)  
  6225  (5540)              buildTell(at, cut, argLen - cut - 1) =: length
  6226  (5541)              sendToServer(length)
  6227  (5542)              RETURN
  6228  (5543)          ENDIF
  6229  (5544)  
  6230  (5545)          IF cmdIs('LEAVE') = 1 THEN
  6231  (5546)              IF NOT joined THEN
  6232  (5547)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL20', 'not in the room yet$')
  6233  (5548)                  RETURN
  6234  (5549)              ENDIF
  6235  (5550)              buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
  6236  (5551)              sendToServer(length)
  6237  (5552)              FALSE =: joined
  6238  (5553)  
  6239  (5554)              % THE MAGIC IS KEPT ON PURPOSE, and clearing it here was a bug.
  6240  (5555)              %
  6241  (5556)              % serverMagic is the ADDRESS OF THE SERVER. Leaving a room does not
  6242  (5557)              % make the server unreachable - it only means we no longer hold a
  6243  (5558)              % seat in it. Zeroing it left the client with nothing to send to, so
  6244  (5559)              % every question that is supposed to work while unjoined silently
  6245  (5560)              % did nothing:
  6246  (5561)              %
  6247  (5562)              %   /list  - which the wire registry has always said is allowed
  6248  (5563)              %            unjoined, and which is exactly what somebody asks when
  6249  (5564)              %            they have just left and are deciding where to go;
  6250  (5565)              %   /who   - the all-rooms answer built on 2026-08-22.
  6251  (5566)              %
  6252  (5567)              % MEASURED: after /leave, /who printed nothing at all. The server
  6253  (5568)              % was answering correctly; the question never left the building.
  6254  (5569)              %
  6255  (5570)              % joined is what gates SPEAKING, and it still does - see the main
  6256  (5571)              % loop. That flag and the address are two different facts and were
  6257  (5572)              % being cleared as if they were one.
  6258  (5573)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL14', 'left the room$')
  6259  (5574)              RETURN
  6260  (5575)          ENDIF
  6261  (5576)  
  6262  (5577)          % /exit IS THE NAME, /quit STILL WORKS.
  6263  (5578)          %
  6264  (5579)          % The help lists only /exit, so there is one answer to "how do I get
  6265  (5580)          % out" - but /quit has been in this client since it was written and in
  6266  (5581)          % the screen prototype beside it, and silently removing a word people
  6267  (5582)          % have in their fingers is a poor trade for tidiness. It costs one
  6268  (5583)          % test.
  6269  (5584)          %
  6270  (5585)          % Neither is a prefix of any other command, so /e and /q both work.
  6271  (5586)          IF cmdIs('EXIT') = 1 OR cmdIs('QUIT') = 1 THEN
  6272  (5587)              IF joined THEN
  6273  (5588)                  buildOwn(kLeave, 0) =: length
  6274  (5589)                  sendToServer(length)
  6275  (5590)              ENDIF
  6276  (5591)              FALSE =: running
  6277  (5592)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', 'bye$')
  6278  (5593)              RETURN
  6279  (5594)          ENDIF
  6280  (5595)  
  6281  (5596)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL28', 'no such command - try /help$')
  6282  (5597)      ENDROUTINE
  6283  (5598)  
  6284  (5599)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  6285  (5600)      % The program.
  6286  (5601)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  6287  (5602)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  6288  (5603)      % The prompt: WHO you are and WHERE you are, on the line you type into.
  6289  (5604)      %
  6290  (5605)      %     [OLAV]           before any room has been entered
  6291  (5606)      %     [OLAV/LOBBY]     once the server has welcomed us into one
  6292  (5607)      %
  6293  (5608)      % A prompt is not decoration here. Without one the program gives no sign it
  6294  (5609)      % is waiting, and with arriving lines landing on the same screen there was
  6295  (5610)      % nothing to tell a typed line from a received one.
  6296  (5611)      %
  6297  (5612)      % NOT YET: [OLAV/Private:ANNA] for a private channel with one other user.
  6298  (5613)      % There is no private channel in this client to be in - no /msg, no second
  6299  (5614)      % conversation - so a prompt claiming one would be inventing a state that
  6300  (5615)      % does not exist. It goes in when the thing it names does.
  6301  (5616)      %
  6302  (5617)      % showRoom is NOT used here: it wraps the name in parentheses for prose
  6303  (5618)      % like "joining (LOBBY)", and "[OLAV/(LOBBY)]" is one bracket too many.
  6304  (5619)      % -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  6305  (5620)      ROUTINE VOID, VOID : showPrompt
  6306  (5621)          INTEGER : i
  6307  (5622)  
  6308  (5623)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '[')
  6309  (5624)          showMyName
  6310  (5625)          IF joined THEN
  6311  (5626)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '/')
  6312  (5627)              FOR i IN 1:lenRoomName DO
  6313  (5628)                  roomName(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
  6314  (5629)                  OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
  6315  (5630)              ENDFOR
  6316  (5631)          ENDIF
  6317  (5632)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL2', '] ')
  6318  (5633)  
  6319  (5634)          % PUT BACK WHAT WAS ALREADY TYPED.
  6320  (5635)          %
  6321  (5636)          % An arriving line is printed on the line being typed into - there is
  6322  (5637)          % one cursor and the room does not wait for anyone to finish a
  6323  (5638)          % sentence. Without this, a message arriving mid-sentence silently eats
  6324  (5639)          % half of it: the characters are still in the buffer and will still be
  6325  (5640)          % sent, but the screen shows an empty prompt, so the user retypes what
  6326  (5641)          % they had and sends it twice.
  6327  (5642)          FOR i IN 1:typedLen DO
  6328  (5643)              typed(i - 1) =: oneChar(0)
  6329  (5644)              OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', oneChar)
  6330  (5645)          ENDFOR
  6331  (5646)      ENDROUTINE
  6332  (5647)  
  6333  (5648)      PROGRAM : chatClient
  6334  (5649)          XMMSGIDENTIFIER : msgIdent
  6335  (5650)          INTEGER : length
  6336  (5651)          INTEGER : i                   % the start-up room copy below uses it
  6337  (5652)          % How many bytes were still waiting when the keyboard was taken.
  6338  (5653)          % Screen mode only - see the drain beside the MON3 that turns the
  6339  (5654)          % echo off.
  6340  (5655)          INTEGER : drained
  6341  (5656)  
  6342  (5657)          INISTACK stack
  6343  (5658)  
  6344  (5659)          % WINDOW 1 IS THE ROOM AND IT ALWAYS EXISTS. Everything else in the
  6345  (5660)          % table stays zero until a conversation opens one, and zero is what
  6346  (5661)          % winUsed reads as "not there" - so no other seeding is needed.
  6347  (5662)          1 =: winUsed(1)
  6348  (5663)          1 =: curWin
  6349  (5664)  
  6350  (5665)          xmpfopn(0, myPort) =: returnStatus
  6351  (5666)          IF returnStatus >< XMOK THEN
  6352  (5667)              showStatus('xmpfopn', returnStatus)
  6353  (5668)              RETURN
  6354  (5669)          ENDIF
  6355  (5670)  
  6356  (5671)          % A "port = N magic= M st= S" line used to print here, from a XMPFP2M
  6357  (5672)          % call kept only to feed it. It was there to answer one question: the
  6358  (5673)          % server was receiving the join, parsing it exactly right and answering
  6359  (5674)          % with status 0, and nothing was arriving back - so was the reply going
  6360  (5675)          % to the wrong address?
  6361  (5676)          %
  6362  (5677)          % IT WAS NOT. The reply address was never the bug. The two magic numbers
  6363  (5678)          % matched, which is what sent the search to the receive test in drainPort
  6364  (5679)          % where the real fault was: XMPFRCV returned XMOK, the test excluded
  6365  (5680)          % zero, and the welcome was dequeued and dropped. That is the whole
  6366  (5681)          % reason this diagnostic earned its keep, and the reason it can now go.
  6367  (5682)          %
  6368  (5683)          % XMPFP2M went with it. Nothing else read myMagic.
  6369  (5684)  
  6370  (5685)          % ===================================================================
  6371  (5686)          % HOW THIS CLIENT WATCHES TWO THINGS WITHOUT A NO-WAIT TERMINAL.
  6372  (5687)          %
  6373  (5688)          % The obvious design polls both: switch the terminal to no-wait with
  6374  (5689)          % TNOWAIT (307B) so INBT stops blocking, then spin on keyboard and port
  6375  (5690)          % together.
  6376  (5691)          %
  6377  (5692)          % ============================================================ SOLVED ==
  6378  (5693)          % EVERYTHING BELOW THIS LINE IS KEPT AS A RECORD OF A WRONG CONCLUSION.
  6379  (5694)          % It argued at length that a no-wait terminal could not be had here, and
  6380  (5695)          % that the client should therefore let the keyboard block. That decision
  6381  (5696)          % is what made an arriving line invisible until the user pressed return,
  6382  (5697)          % which on 2026-08-20 looked exactly like a broadcast reaching nobody
  6383  (5698)          % and cost an afternoon.
  6384  (5699)          %
  6385  (5700)          % THE WAY THROUGH IS MONITOR_CALL BY NAME, which the reasoning below
  6386  (5701)          % dismisses because "the MON-CALL-NAMES file was not found". That file
  6387  (5702)          % is supplied by MON-CALL-1B-A00, which is now INSTALLED and is already
  6388  (5703)          % in this client's LOAD list - CHATSV has been calling
  6389  (5704)          % MONITOR_CALL('TimeOut', ...) with it for weeks. And the call wanted is
  6390  (5705)          % not TNOWAIT (307B) at all but NoWaitSwitch (NOWT 36B), whose three
  6391  (5706)          % parameters are ALL INPUTS, so there is not even an output to get
  6392  (5707)          % wrong. See terminalNoWait and terminalWait above.
  6393  (5708)          %
  6394  (5709)          % The lesson is the one that keeps recurring on this project: a "cannot"
  6395  (5710)          % written down once outlives the thing that made it true. This note
  6396  (5711)          % survived the installation of the very package that answers it.
  6397  (5712)          % ======================================================================
  6398  (5713)          %
  6399  (5714)          % The old reasoning, for the record:
  6400  (5715)          %
  6401  (5716)          %   THE PORT IS DRAINED WITHOUT WAITING - xmpfrcv with flags ZERO.
  6402  (5717)          %   That is XMSG'S OWN no-wait and has nothing to do with the terminal:
  6403  (5718)          %   without XFWTF the call returns at once whether or not a message was
  6404  (5719)          %   there. So everything waiting can be taken and printed in a tight
  6405  (5720)          %   loop that never blocks.
  6406  (5721)          %
  6407  (5722)          %   THE KEYBOARD IS ALLOWED TO BLOCK - MON1, one character at a time,
  6408  (5723)          %   until carriage return.
  6409  (5724)          %
  6410  (5725)          % The one thing it costs: arriving lines are shown when you finish
  6411  (5726)          % yours, not the instant they land. Press RETURN on an empty line to
  6412  (5727)          % look. That is the whole price, and it buys a client that needs no
  6413  (5728)          % missing product, no assembler and no interface routine.
  6414  (5729)          %
  6415  (5730)          % Reading the keyboard needs INBT (1B). Getting a NO-WAIT terminal
  6416  (5731)          % would additionally need TNOWAIT (307B), and that is the one that
  6417  (5732)          % cannot be had:
  6418  (5733)          %
  6419  (5734)          % MONITOR_CALL IS THE WRONG TOOL, both ways round, and both were tried:
  6420  (5735)          %
  6421  (5736)          %   By NAME - Monitor_Call('InByte', ...), the form the Monitor Calls
  6422  (5737)          %   manual prints for PLANC - needs the MON-CALL-NAMES file. The
  6423  (5738)          %   compiler answers THE MON-CALL-NAMES FILE WAS NOT FOUND, and no such
  6424  (5739)          %   file exists under any of the nine users on this machine.
  6425  (5740)          %
  6426  (5741)          %   By NUMBER - MONITOR_CALL(1B, ...) - compiles and then does not
  6427  (5742)          %   link: it generates a call to 5MON_P, which is in neither
  6428  (5743)          %   PLANC-1BANK-F00 nor PLANC-2BANK-F00 (LIST-ENTRIES-UNDEFINED after
  6429  (5744)          %   loading both).
  6430  (5745)          %
  6431  (5746)          % WHAT THE PLANC MANUAL ACTUALLY SAYS (ND-60.117.5, appendix 0.2,
  6432  (5747)          % "Monitor Calls Available on the ND-100 and the ND-500"): the runtime
  6433  (5748)          % provides monitor calls as ORDINARY ROUTINES, one per call, named MONn
  6434  (5749)          % and IMPORTed like anything else -
  6435  (5750)          %
  6436  (5751)          %     ROUTINE VOID, BYTE (INTEGER) : MON1 (dev)      % INBIT
  6437  (5752)          %
  6438  (5753)          % which is why the COSMOS guide's own sample opens with
  6439  (5754)          % IMPORT (ROUTINE VOID, VOID: MONO) - that is MON0, LEAVE. These are
  6440  (5755)          % the MN1, MN122, MN310 ... units sitting in PLANC-1BANK-F00. So INBT
  6441  (5756)          % is reachable, correctly, as MON1(1) - no MONITOR_CALL, no names file,
  6442  (5757)          % no 5MON_P.
  6443  (5758)          %
  6444  (5759)          % TNOWAIT IS NOT. The appendix lists MON 0,1,2,3,4,11,12,17,21,45,47,
  6445  (5760)          % 50,54,62,63,64,65,104,113,114,117,120,144,161,162,167,263 - and 307
  6446  (5761)          % is not among them. The manual says what to do about that in as many
  6447  (5762)          % words: "Any monitor calls not listed here, may be called from a PLANC
  6448  (5763)          % routine if a suitable interface routine is constructed by the user.
  6449  (5764)          % If this is done, the user must load the interface routine BEFORE the
  6450  (5765)          % PLANC runtime library."
  6451  (5766)          %
  6452  (5767)          % A hand-written interface routine for MON 307B, loaded ahead of
  6453  (5768)          % PLANC-1BANK-F00, is what the manual says to do about that.
  6454  (5769)          %
  6455  (5770)          % THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY THAT WAS IMPOSSIBLE HERE. It claimed "D100 has
  6456  (5771)          % neither MAC nor NPL installed (LIST-FILES found only MACM-AREA:DATA)".
  6457  (5772)          % BOTH HALVES ARE WRONG, and the way they were wrong is worth keeping.
  6458  (5773)          %
  6459  (5774)          % LIST-FILES IS THE WRONG COMMAND FOR THIS QUESTION. Compilers and other
  6460  (5775)          % subsystems are loaded into shared memory across users, not kept as :PROG
  6461  (5776)          % files in anybody's area, so LIST-FILES was never going to find one and
  6462  (5777)          % its silence proved nothing. The command that answers it is
  6463  (5778)          % LIST-REENTRANT. On D100, 2026-08-18:
  6464  (5779)          %
  6465  (5780)          %   START RESTART SEGMENT   NAME
  6466  (5781)          %  177777B 177776B    107B   MAC
  6467  (5782)          %      0B      1B    110B   NPL
  6468  (5783)          %    752B    752B    111B   PLANC-100
  6469  (5784)          %     0B      0B    112B   PED
  6470  (5785)          %    11B     11B    113B   FORTRAN-100
  6471  (5786)          %
  6472  (5787)          % MAC and NPL are BOTH there, alongside the PLANC that builds this program.
  6473  (5788)          % @MAC answers "- MAC -".
  6474  (5789)          %
  6475  (5790)          % So the door the manual describes is open: any monitor call outside the
  6476  (5791)          % runtime's ready-made MONn set can be reached with an interface routine
  6477  (5792)          % assembled in MAC and loaded BEFORE the PLANC library. TNOWAIT (307B) is
  6478  (5793)          % the one this program wanted, and with it the loop below could stop being
  6479  (5794)          % a busy one - see "what is not finished" at the foot of the file.
  6480  (5795)          %
  6481  (5796)          % The design below still stands on its own: it needs no interface routine,
  6482  (5797)          % and it works. But it is now a CHOICE rather than the only option, and
  6483  (5798)          % nobody should read this comment and conclude the machine cannot do it.
  6484  (5799)          % ===================================================================
  6485  (5800)  
  6486  (5801)          % SETTLE THE NAME BEFORE THE JOIN, because the join CARRIES it and
  6487  (5802)          % there is no second chance to put one in. Nothing is asked of the
  6488  (5803)          % user - see setupName.
  6489  (5804)          setupName
  6490  (5805)  
  6491  (5806)          % THE DEFAULT ROOM, BUILT BY THE SAME RULE AS A TYPED /join.
  6492  (5807)          %
  6493  (5808)          % This client joins a room at start-up whether or not the user asked
  6494  (5809)          % for one, and the bug report was right that nothing said so. It is
  6495  (5810)          % said now, with the name in parentheses.
  6496  (5811)          %
  6497  (5812)          % The short name goes through "typed" because setRoom reads from there.
  6498  (5813)          % That is deliberate: the prefix is then added in ONE place for both
  6499  (5814)          % paths, and the start-up name cannot drift from the /join name.
  6500  (5815)          FOR i IN 1:lenHomeRoom DO
  6501  (5816)              homeRoom(i - 1) =: typed(i - 1)
  6502  (5817)          ENDFOR
  6503  (5818)          setRoom(0, lenHomeRoom)
  6504  (5819)  
  6505  (5820)          % ---- WHICH RENDERER? Asked ONCE, here, and never again -----------
  6506  (5821)          %
  6507  (5822)          % blankscreen is VTM's first call and CTYTP is only valid AFTER it -
  6508  (5823)          % ask before and the bits are whatever was in memory. Two bits decide
  6509  (5824)          % it: the terminal is a VDU at all, and it can be told where to put
  6510  (5825)          % the cursor. Without both, a screen renderer would draw its frame as
  6511  (5826)          % a page of line noise on a printing terminal.
  6512  (5827)          %
  6513  (5828)          % ASKED ONCE because a renderer that could change under the program
  6514  (5829)          % would need every drawing site to cope with both, which is how a
  6515  (5830)          % screen ends up half painted.
  6516  (5831)          blankscreen
  6517  (5832)          MON16(1) =: termType
  6518  (5833)          TRUE =: screenOn
  6519  (5834)          IF (termType AND bitVdu) = 0 THEN
  6520  (5835)              FALSE =: screenOn
  6521  (5836)          ENDIF
  6522  (5837)          IF (termType AND bitCpos) = 0 THEN
  6523  (5838)              FALSE =: screenOn
  6524  (5839)          ENDIF
  6525  (5840)  
  6526  (5841)          IF screenOn THEN
  6527  (5842)              % TAKE THE KEYBOARD BEFORE A SINGLE THING IS DRAWN.
  6528  (5843)              %
  6529  (5844)              % No echo, because a raw key loop has no say in where the cursor
  6530  (5845)              % is and SINTRAN would drop each character wherever the last
  6531  (5846)              % drawing finished - inside the windows. MEASURED in CHATUI: with
  6532  (5847)              % this done after the first paint, every run came up showing
  6533  (5848)              % '> ??g??', and draining could not fix it because draining empties
  6534  (5849)              % the BUFFER and not the SCREEN it was already echoed to.
  6535  (5850)              %
  6536  (5851)              % And no ESC abort, which is what makes turning the echo off SAFE:
  6537  (5852)              % an abort would skip the exit path and hand back a terminal with
  6538  (5853)              % no echo at all.
  6539  (5854)              MON3(1, -1, echoTab)
  6540  (5855)              MON71(1)
  6541  (5856)  
  6542  (5857)              % WHATEVER IS ALREADY WAITING IS NOT THE USER TALKING TO THIS
  6543  (5858)              % PROGRAM - it is the tail of whatever was typed to get here, and
  6544  (5859)              % on a line whose terminal type was unset, the answer to VTM's own
  6545  (5860)              % question about it.
  6546  (5861)              DO
  6547  (5862)                  MON66(1) =: drained
  6548  (5863)                  WHILE drained > 0
  6549  (5864)                  MON1(1) =: drained
  6550  (5865)              ENDDO
  6551  (5866)          ENDIF
  6552  (5867)  
  6553  (5868)          % WHAT IS BEING ATTEMPTED, not what has been achieved. The name here is
  6554  (5869)          % the one the join CARRIES; whether we get to keep it is the server's
  6555  (5870)          % answer, printed when the welcome arrives.
  6556  (5871)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL8', 'joining ')
  6557  (5872)          showRoom
  6558  (5873)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL4', ' as ')
  6559  (5874)          showMyName
  6560  (5875)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  6561  (5876)          sendJoin
  6562  (5877)  
  6563  (5878)          % ONE LINE, NOT THE WHOLE COMMAND LIST. Printing all ten commands at
  6564  (5879)          % start-up buries the two lines that actually matter under a screen
  6565  (5880)          % nobody asked for.
  6566  (5881)          OUTPUT(1, 'AL37', 'Type /HELP for help and command list$')
  6567  (5882)  
  6568  (5883)          % showPrompt re-echoes whatever is in the typed buffer, so the buffer
  6569  (5884)          % has to MEAN something before the first prompt. Nothing above sets it,
  6570  (5885)          % and PLANC does not promise a module variable starts at zero.
  6571  (5886)          0 =: typedLen
  6572  (5887)          TRUE =: inputDirty
  6573  (5888)  
  6574  (5889)          % THE FIRST PAINT GOES OVER THE JOIN BANNER, which is the point: those
  6575  (5890)          % lines were printed before the renderer was known and they are the
  6576  (5891)          % LINE renderer's, so on a screen they are simply painted over.
  6577  (5892)          IF screenOn THEN
  6578  (5893)              drawAll
  6579  (5894)              flush
  6580  (5895)          ELSE
  6581  (5896)              showPrompt
  6582  (5897)          ENDIF
  6583  (5898)  
  6584  (5899)          % FROM HERE THE TERMINAL IS IN NO-WAIT, so MON1 stops blocking and the
  6585  (5900)          % loop below can look at the port and the keyboard in turn. Switched on
  6586  (5901)          % AFTER the join is sent, so nothing above has to care, and switched off
  6587  (5902)          % again on the way out - see the end of the loop.
  6588  (5903)          %
  6589  (5904)          % ASK WHICH TERMINAL THIS IS FIRST. terminalNoWait needs the LDN and
  6590  (5905)          % there is no way to know it without asking - the number differs per
  6591  (5906)          % session and per machine, and the value the program used to pass was
  6592  (5907)          % not a terminal number at all.
  6593  (5908)          findMyLdn
  6594  (5909)          terminalNoWait
  6595  (5910)  
  6596  (5911)          % ONE LAST DRAIN, AND THIS ONE IS THE ONE THAT WORKS.
  6597  (5912)          %
  6598  (5913)          % There is already a drain up beside the MON3 that turns the echo off,
  6599  (5914)          % and it is not enough: the input line still came up holding '??g??'.
  6600  (5915)          % Those bytes arrive DURING the drawing, not before it - VTM asks the
  6601  (5916)          % line what terminal it is and the answer lands in the buffer while the
  6602  (5917)          % frame is being painted, so a drain that ran earlier could not have
  6603  (5918)          % caught them.
  6604  (5919)          %
  6605  (5920)          % Draining here, with the screen already up and immediately before the
  6606  (5921)          % first read, is the last moment anything can arrive unasked. typedLen
  6607  (5922)          % is cleared after it because a byte that slipped past the first drain
  6608  (5923)          % is already sitting in the buffer, and clearing without draining would
  6609  (5924)          % just let it back in on the next pass.
  6610  (5925)          IF screenOn THEN
  6611  (5926)              DO
  6612  (5927)                  MON66(1) =: drained
  6613  (5928)                  WHILE drained > 0
  6614  (5929)                  MON1(1) =: drained
  6615  (5930)              ENDDO
  6616  (5931)              0 =: typedLen
  6617  (5932)              TRUE =: inputDirty
  6618  (5933)              drawInput
  6619  (5934)              flush
  6620  (5935)          ENDIF
  6621  (5936)  
  6622  (5937)          DO
  6623  (5938)              % ---- take everything the port is holding, without waiting -------
  6624  (5939)              drainPort
  6625  (5940)  
  6626  (5941)              % ---- then take whatever HAS been typed, without waiting ---------
  6627  (5942)              % MON1 CAN RAISE A ROUTINEERROR, AND IT MUST BE CAUGHT HERE.
  6628  (5943)              %
  6629  (5944)              % Measured on D100: without this handler the client printed the
  6630  (5945)              % same arriving message for ever and ESC broke it out of a SPIN,
  6631  (5946)              % not out of a wait - so MON1 was not blocking at all. The terminal
  6632  (5947)              % is in no-wait BECAUSE terminalNoWait ABOVE PUT IT THERE, and that
  6633  (5948)              % call is new: for a long time this comment asserted the no-wait
  6634  (5949)              % state while nothing in the program ever asked for it, so MON1
  6635  (5950)              % really did block and the loop only came round when the user
  6636  (5951)              % pressed return. The Monitor Calls manual says what no-wait means:
  6637  (5952)              % "In No Wait, the program does not wait for input or output to
  6638  (5953)              % complete. Monitor calls like InByte return ERROR CODE 3 instead."
  6639  (5954)              % In PLANC that arrives as a ROUTINEERROR, and an unhandled one
  6640  (5955)              % unwinds the program - which re-entered, re-sent the join, and
  6641  (5956)              % produced another undeliverable letter coming back. One missing
  6642  (5957)              % handler, three symptoms.
  6643  (5958)              %
  6644  (5959)              % Caught, it is exactly the poll the design wanted: ErrCode 3 means
  6645  (5960)              % nothing was typed, so leave ch at zero and let the loop go round
  6646  (5961)              % to look at the port again. ErrCode is supplied by the runtime and
  6647  (5962)              % must NOT be declared.
  6648  (5963)              % lineReady stays FALSE until RETURN is seen. typedLen is NOT reset
  6649  (5964)              % here: a line half typed when the port needs draining has to
  6650  (5965)              % survive the trip round the outer loop, so it is only cleared once
  6651  (5966)              % the line has been dealt with.
  6652  (5967)              FALSE =: lineReady
  6653  (5968)              DO
  6654  (5969)                  0 =: ch
  6655  (5970)                  ON ROUTINEERROR DO
  6656  (5971)                      IF ErrCode >< emptyInput THEN
  6657  (5972)                          OUTPUT(1, 'AL21', 'terminal read failed$')
  6658  (5973)                      ENDIF
  6659  (5974)                      0 =: rawCh
  6660  (5975)                  ENDON
  6661  (5976)                  % READ THE SAME DEVICE THE SWITCH WAS SET ON.
  6662  (5977)                  %
  6663  (5978)                  % MEASURED ON D100 2026-08-22, and this is the second half of
  6664  (5979)                  % the fix. Passing the real LDN to TerminalNoWait changed its
  6665  (5980)                  % status from -1 to 0 - a genuine change, the call is now
  6666  (5981)                  % accepted - and MON1 WENT ON BLOCKING ANYWAY. Everything was
  6667  (5982)                  % still queued until a keystroke flushed it.
  6668  (5983)                  %
  6669  (5984)                  % Because the switch was set on the terminal's LDN while the
  6670  (5985)                  % read was still asking for FILE 1. SINTRAN's "own terminal"
  6671  (5986)                  % shortcut and the logical device are not the same object to
  6672  (5987)                  % point a no-wait flag at, so the flag was on something this
  6673  (5988)                  % read never touched.
  6674  (5989)                  %
  6675  (5990)                  % myLdn falls back to 1, so a machine where ExecutionInfo does
  6676  (5991)                  % not answer reads exactly as before.
  6677  (5992)                  %
  6678  (5993)                  % SCREEN MODE ASKS MON66 FIRST AND ONLY THEN READS, and that is
  6679  (5994)                  % not a tidier way of doing the same thing - it is the fix for a
  6680  (5995)                  % client that joined and then went deaf.
  6681  (5996)                  %
  6682  (5997)                  % MEASURED on D100 2026-08-26. With the echo turned off, MON1
  6683  (5998)                  % BLOCKS whatever TerminalNoWait was told. The main loop sat in
  6684  (5999)                  % the read, drainPort never ran, and the server's messages came
  6685  (6000)                  % back undeliverable: SEATS said 1/16 - the client was properly
  6686  (6001)                  % seated - while bounce climbed on every line sent to it. The
  6687  (6002)                  % screen only ever changed when a key was pressed, because a
  6688  (6003)                  % keystroke was the only thing that let the loop go round.
  6689  (6004)                  %
  6690  (6005)                  % Nothing on screen says "deaf". The seat count and the bounce
  6691  (6006)                  % counter do, which is why they were the thing to look at.
  6692  (6007)                  %
  6693  (6008)                  % MON66 is ISIZE: how many bytes are WAITING. Ask that and MON1
  6694  (6009)                  % is only ever called when there is certainly one to take, so it
  6695  (6010)                  % cannot block. This is the same poll TESTUI and CHATUI use, and
  6696  (6011)                  % the reason the no-wait route was abandoned there too.
  6697  (6012)                  0 =: rawCh
  6698  (6013)                  IF screenOn THEN
  6699  (6014)                      MON66(1) =: drained
  6700  (6015)                      IF drained > 0 THEN
  6701  (6016)                          MON1(myLdn) =: rawCh
  6702  (6017)                      ENDIF
  6703  (6018)                  ELSE
  6704  (6019)                      MON1(myLdn) =: rawCh
  6705  (6020)                  ENDIF
  6706  (6021)                  % PARITY. Terminal input carries an even-parity bit in bit 7,
  6707  (6022)                  % so carriage return arrives as 141, NOT 13. Compare without
  6708  (6023)                  % masking and the client never sees the user press return and
  6709  (6024)                  % looks hung rather than wrong.
  6710  (6025)                  rawCh =: ch
  6711  (6026)                  ch AND parityMask =: ch
  6712  (6027)  
  6713  (6028)                  % ANY KEY CLOSES THE PANEL, AND DOES NOTHING ELSE.
  6714  (6029)                  %
  6715  (6030)                  % Consumed here, before every other test, so the key that
  6716  (6031)                  % dismisses the window cannot also be typed into the input
  6717  (6032)                  % line or read as a command. Anything that arrived while the
  6718  (6033)                  % panel was up is in the cache already and appears the moment
  6719  (6034)                  % the room is painted back.
  6720  (6035)                  IF panelUp THEN
  6721  (6036)                      IF ch >< 0 THEN
  6722  (6037)                          % closePanel, NOT drawAll. It clears the rectangle,
  6723  (6038)                          % puts back the separators, the pane rows and the one
  6724  (6039)                          % or two single rows that were actually underneath, and
  6725  (6040)                          % flushes. drawAll here rebuilt all 24 rows to undo a
  6726  (6041)                          % window covering a fraction of them.
  6727  (6042)                          closePanel
  6728  (6043)                          0 =: ch
  6729  (6044)                      ENDIF
  6730  (6045)                  ENDIF
  6731  (6046)  
  6732  (6047)                  % ONE KEY CYCLES THE WINDOWS.
  6733  (6048)                  %
  6734  (6049)                  % Control-W, because the letter matches the /w command and a
  6735  (6050)                  % control character cannot be part of anything somebody is
  6736  (6051)                  % typing - a plain letter would have to be a mode, and a mode
  6737  (6052)                  % is the thing that makes a terminal program feel haunted.
  6738  (6053)                  %
  6739  (6054)                  % 23 is Control-W after the parity mask above.
  6740  (6055)                  IF ch = 23 THEN
  6741  (6056)                      IF screenOn THEN
  6742  (6057)                          winNext
  6743  (6058)                      ENDIF
  6744  (6059)                      0 =: ch
  6745  (6060)                  ENDIF
  6746  (6061)  
  6747  (6062)                  IF ch = carriageRet THEN
  6748  (6063)                      TRUE =: lineReady
  6749  (6064)  
  6750  (6065)                      % A CARRIAGE RETURN IS NOT A NEW LINE ON THIS TERMINAL. It
  6751  (6066)                      % returns the cursor to column one of the line just typed
  6752  (6067)                      % and nothing moves it down, so whatever is printed next
  6753  (6068)                      % lands ON TOP of what the user wrote. That is what put
  6754  (6069)                      %     /whoIT-PROOF-LINE
  6755  (6070)                      % on the screen - "/who" with the next arriving line
  6756  (6071)                      % written over it - and it reads as the program mangling
  6757  (6072)                      % the input when in fact it simply never ended the line.
  6758  (6073)                      % One end-of-line here, the instant return is pressed.
  6759  (6074)                      %
  6760  (6075)                      % LINE MODE ONLY. On a screen there is no cursor wandering
  6761  (6076)                      % down the page - the input line is a fixed row that gets
  6762  (6077)                      % repainted, and an end-of-line written into it would scroll
  6763  (6078)                      % the whole frame up by one.
  6764  (6079)                      IF NOT screenOn THEN
  6765  (6080)                          OUTPUT(1, 'AL1', '$')
  6766  (6081)                      ENDIF
  6767  (6082)                  ENDIF
  6768  (6083)  
  6769  (6084)                  % Leave the moment the keyboard runs dry (ch = 0, nothing was
  6770  (6085)                  % typed) or the line is finished. Going round again on an empty
  6771  (6086)                  % buffer is what turns this into a spin instead of a poll.
  6772  (6087)                  WHILE ch >< 0 AND NOT lineReady
  6773  (6088)  
  6774  (6089)                  % BACKSPACE, and it only has to be handled in SCREEN mode.
  6775  (6090)                  %
  6776  (6091)                  % On a printing terminal SINTRAN's own echo does the erasing
  6777  (6092)                  % before this program ever sees the line, which is why the test
  6778  (6093)                  % below has always been "> 31" and nobody missed it. With the
  6779  (6094)                  % echo turned off there is nobody else to do it.
  6780  (6095)                  %
  6781  (6096)                  % 127 as well as 8: VTM hands back 127 for the backspace key -
  6782  (6097)                  % MEASURED with KEYPROB, where a sent 0x08 came back as 127.
  6783  (6098)                  IF screenOn THEN
  6784  (6099)                      IF ch = 8 OR ch = 127 THEN
  6785  (6100)                          IF typedLen > 0 THEN
  6786  (6101)                              typedLen - 1 =: typedLen
  6787  (6102)                              TRUE =: inputDirty
  6788  (6103)                          ENDIF
  6789  (6104)                      ENDIF
  6790  (6105)                  ENDIF
  6791  (6106)  
  6792  (6107)                  IF ch > 31 AND ch < 127 THEN
  6793  (6108)                      % The bound. A full line stops accepting characters rather
  6794  (6109)                      % than writing past the end of typed - PLANC checks no array
  6795  (6110)                      % bound, so this test is the only thing between a held-down
  6796  (6111)                      % key and the variables laid out after it.
  6797  (6112)                      IF typedLen < maxTyped THEN
  6798  (6113)                          ch =: typed(typedLen)
  6799  (6114)                          typedLen + 1 =: typedLen
  6800  (6115)                          TRUE =: inputDirty
  6801  (6116)                      ENDIF
  6802  (6117)                  ENDIF
  6803  (6118)  
  6804  (6119)                  % SHOW WHAT HAS BEEN TYPED - but only once the burst is over.
  6805  (6120)                  %
  6806  (6121)                  % Repainting per character is not just slow, it is WRONG on a
  6807  (6122)                  % paste: the repaint costs more than the terminal takes to
  6808  (6123)                  % deliver the next byte, SINTRAN's 64-byte input buffer fills,
  6809  (6124)                  % and the tail is dropped - and the tail is where the RETURN
  6810  (6125)                  % is. Asking MON66 whether more is waiting is what tells us the
  6811  (6126)                  % burst has not finished. MEASURED in CHATUI: 63 characters and
  6812  (6127)                  % a RETURN arrived whole, 72 lost the RETURN.
  6813  (6128)                  IF screenOn THEN
  6814  (6129)                      IF NOT lineReady THEN
  6815  (6130)                          MON66(1) =: drained
  6816  (6131)                          IF drained <= 0 THEN
  6817  (6132)                              drawInput
  6818  (6133)                              flush
  6819  (6134)                          ENDIF
  6820  (6135)                      ENDIF
  6821  (6136)                  ENDIF
  6822  (6137)              ENDDO
  6823  (6138)  
  6824  (6139)              IF lineReady AND typedLen > 0 THEN
  6825  (6140)                  % NOTHING IS SENT BEFORE THE WELCOME. serverMagic is not known
  6826  (6141)                  % until then, so sending here would post the line to address
  6827  (6142)                  % zero. ChatClient.Say refuses the same way; this is that test.
  6828  (6143)                  % A LEADING SLASH MAKES IT A COMMAND, not something to say.
  6829  (6144)                  IF typed(0) = 47 THEN
  6830  (6145)                      handleCommand
  6831  (6146)                  ELSE
  6832  (6147)                      IF joined THEN
  6833  (6148)  
  6834  (6149)                          % WHERE A PLAIN LINE GOES DEPENDS ON THE WINDOW, and
  6835  (6150)                          % this is the single most important rule in the whole
  6836  (6151)                          % window feature.
  6837  (6152)                          %
  6838  (6153)                          % In a conversation window it goes to THAT PERSON. If
  6839  (6154)                          % it went to the room instead, somebody reading a
  6840  (6155)                          % private exchange and replying to it would broadcast
  6841  (6156)                          % their reply to everybody - a privacy failure caused
  6842  (6157)                          % entirely by the display, which is the exact thing the
  6843  (6158)                          % stars on a direct message exist to prevent. Getting
  6844  (6159)                          % this wrong would be worse than not having windows.
  6845  (6160)                          IF curWin = 1 THEN
  6846  (6161)                              buildOwn(kSay, typedLen) =: length
  6847  (6162)                              sendToServer(length)
  6848  (6163)                          ELSE
  6849  (6164)                              % Kept for the confirmation, exactly as /tell does.
  6850  (6165)                              typedLen =: lastTellLen
  6851  (6166)                              IF lastTellLen > 76 THEN
  6852  (6167)                                  76 =: lastTellLen
  6853  (6168)                              ENDIF
  6854  (6169)                              FOR i IN 1:lastTellLen DO
  6855  (6170)                                  typed(i - 1) =: lastTell(i - 1)
  6856  (6171)                              ENDFOR
  6857  (6172)  
  6858  (6173)                              buildTellTo(curWin, typedLen) =: length
  6859  (6174)                              sendToServer(length)
  6860  (6175)                          ENDIF
  6861  (6176)                      ELSE
  6862  (6177)                          OUTPUT(1, 'AL20', 'not in the room yet$')
  6863  (6178)                      ENDIF
  6864  (6179)                  ENDIF
  6865  (6180)              ENDIF
  6866  (6181)  
  6867  (6182)              % Cleared only once the finished line has been dealt with, so a
  6868  (6183)              % line still being typed is not thrown away by a drain pass.
  6869  (6184)              IF lineReady THEN
  6870  (6185)                  0 =: typedLen
  6871  (6186)                  TRUE =: inputDirty
  6872  (6187)              ENDIF
  6873  (6188)  
  6874  (6189)              % PUT THE PROMPT BACK WHENEVER SOMETHING HAS BEEN WRITTEN OVER IT -
  6875  (6190)              % a command that has just been dealt with, or lines that arrived
  6876  (6191)              % from the room. Not on an idle pass, or an untouched screen would
  6877  (6192)              % fill with prompts a fifth of a second apart.
  6878  (6193)              % SOMETHING HAPPENED - repaint whichever renderer is in use.
  6879  (6194)              %
  6880  (6195)              % paintChat is the expensive one: 17 rows of 77 characters, about a
  6881  (6196)              % second and a half on a real 9600-baud line. It runs only when a
  6882  (6197)              % message actually arrived or a line was finished, never on an idle
  6883  (6198)              % pass, which is what keeps the loop cheap enough to also poll a
  6884  (6199)              % keyboard.
  6885  (6200)              IF lineReady OR lastDrainCount > 0 THEN
  6886  (6201)                  IF screenOn THEN
  6887  (6202)                      % THE ROOM KEEPS SCROLLING BEHIND AN OPEN WINDOW.
  6888  (6203)                      %
  6889  (6204)                      % This whole block used to sit under "IF NOT panelUp", so a
  6890  (6205)                      % line arriving during /help or /who went into the cache and
  6891  (6206)                      % never onto the screen until the window was closed. That
  6892  (6207)                      % reads as a lost message. Its comment said repainting would
  6893  (6208)                      % draw over the window - true of the paint AS IT WAS, which
  6894  (6209)                      % had no clipping, so the guard was a workaround for the
  6895  (6210)                      % missing rectangle and not a decision about behaviour.
  6896  (6211)                      %
  6897  (6212)                      % paintChat clips now, so it is safe to call with a panel up
  6898  (6213)                      % and the covered strip is simply skipped. The three
  6899  (6214)                      % SINGLE-ROW draws below do NOT clip - each lays out a whole
  6900  (6215)                      % row at once - so each is asked whether the panel is over
  6901  (6216)                      % its row, and closePanel puts back whichever were skipped.
  6902  (6217)                      IF lastDrainCount > 0 THEN
  6903  (6218)                          paintChat
  6904  (6219)  
  6905  (6220)                          % THE BAR CARRIES THE NEWS ABOUT WINDOWS YOU ARE
  6906  (6221)                          % NOT LOOKING AT - a conversation that just
  6907  (6222)                          % opened, an unread count, the star. None of that
  6908  (6223)                          % is on the pane, so an arrival that never redrew
  6909  (6224)                          % the bar left a private message sitting in a
  6910  (6225)                          % window nothing on screen mentioned.
  6911  (6226)                          %
  6912  (6227)                          % Only on a real arrival, and drawBar itself is
  6913  (6228)                          % one short row.
  6914  (6229)                          IF NOT rowCovered(barPRow) THEN
  6915  (6230)                              drawBar
  6916  (6231)                          ENDIF
  6917  (6232)                      ENDIF
  6918  (6233)                      IF NOT rowCovered(statusPRow) THEN
  6919  (6234)                          drawStatus
  6920  (6235)                      ENDIF
  6921  (6236)                      IF NOT rowCovered(inputPRow) THEN
  6922  (6237)                          drawInput
  6923  (6238)                      ENDIF
  6924  (6239)                      flush
  6925  (6240)                  ELSE
  6926  (6241)                      showPrompt
  6927  (6242)                  ENDIF
  6928  (6243)              ENDIF
  6929  (6244)  
  6930  (6245)              % ---- NOTHING HAPPENED? THEN GIVE THE MACHINE A MOMENT -----------
  6931  (6246)              % With the terminal in no-wait, MON1 returns at once whether or not
  6932  (6247)              % anything was typed, so a pass that found no message AND no key is
  6933  (6248)              % a pass that would otherwise go straight round again at full speed.
  6934  (6249)              % That is a SPIN, and it is the same shape that burned the machine
  6935  (6250)              % under CHATSV when XMSG died - the note beside sleepUnits carries
  6936  (6251)              % the measurement.
  6937  (6252)              %
  6938  (6253)              % The sleep is skipped whenever ANYTHING happened, so a busy room
  6939  (6254)              % and a fast typist both stay immediate; only an idle client waits,
  6940  (6255)              % and then only a fifth of a second.
  6941  (6256)              IF lastDrainCount = 0 AND typedLen = 0 AND NOT lineReady THEN
  6942  (6257)                  idleSleep
  6943  (6258)              ELSE
  6944  (6259)                  % SOMETHING HAPPENED, so the next idle pass starts short again.
  6945  (6260)                  % This is the half of the back-off that makes it worth having:
  6946  (6261)                  % without it the client would still be in its long sleep at the
  6947  (6262)                  % exact moment the server's answer to the line just sent lands.
  6948  (6263)                  0 =: idleRuns
  6949  (6264)              ENDIF
  6950  (6265)  
  6951  (6266)              % /quit clears this. The continue-test goes LAST so the leave
  6952  (6267)              % message it sends is on its way before the loop ends.
  6953  (6268)              WHILE running
  6954  (6269)          ENDDO
  6955  (6270)  
  6956  (6271)          % PUT THE TERMINAL BACK. Anything that leaves this loop - /quit, or the
  6957  (6272)          % port going away when XMSG dies - hands the terminal back to the user's
  6958  (6273)          % shell, and it must be handed back in the state it was lent in. A
  6959  (6274)          % terminal left in no-wait is one every later program has to live with.
  6960  (6275)          terminalWait
  6961  (6276)  
  6962  (6277)          % AND ON A SCREEN, THREE MORE THINGS ARE OWED BACK.
  6963  (6278)          %
  6964  (6279)          % The echo, the escape key, and the terminal's own settings - in that
  6965  (6280)          % order, and BEFORE the screen is blanked, because blanking is what
  6966  (6281)          % clears the frame away and anything printed after it belongs to the
  6967  (6282)          % shell.
  6968  (6283)          %
  6969  (6284)          % LEAVING THE ECHO OFF IS THE WORST OF THE THREE. The user is returned
  6970  (6285)          % to a SINTRAN prompt that answers nothing they type, which reads as a
  6971  (6286)          % hung machine and is not one. resetscreen restores terminal STATE, not
  6972  (6287)          % content - blankscreen is what clears the picture.
  6973  (6288)          IF screenOn THEN
  6974  (6289)              MON3(1, 0, echoTab)
  6975  (6290)              MON72(1)
  6976  (6291)              blankscreen
  6977  (6292)              resetscreen
  6978  (6293)          ENDIF
  6979  (6294)      ENDROUTINE
  6980  (6295)  
  6981  (6296)  ENDMODULE
  6982  (6297)  
  6983  (6298)  % NO $EOF HERE, deliberately, and it is not an oversight - an earlier version
  6984  (6299)  % had one and it broke the build.
  6985  (6300)  %
  6986  (6301)  % $EOF belongs at the end of an INCLUDED source, where it stops the compiler
  6987  (6302)  % reading past the file's end and calling that the end of the whole
  6988  (6303)  % compilation. XMPIMP:INCL has one for exactly that reason, and so does the
  6989  (6304)  % shipped (UTILITY)XMSG-PL-VALUES-L:INCL.
  6990  (6305)  %
  6991  (6306)  % In the MAIN source it does something else: it ends the compiler SESSION. The
  6992  (6307)  % compiler links and returns to SINTRAN there and then, so every command after
  6993  (6308)  % the COMPILE - the EXIT, and any LOAD - is handed to SINTRAN instead, which
  6994  (6309)  % answers ILLEGAL COMMAND and aborts the batch job. Measured on D100: the LOAD
  6995  (6310)  % line arrived at the shell as "L".
  6996  (6311)  %
  6997  (6312)  % The library is brought in afterwards by BRF-LINKER-C01. See CHATCC:MODE.
  6998  (6313)  
  6999  (6314)  % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  7000  (6315)  % WHAT IS NOT FINISHED:
  7001  (6316)  %
  7002  (6317)  %  1. TWO ITEMS THAT USED TO BE ON THIS LIST WERE STALE, and they are recorded
  7003  (6318)  %     here rather than quietly deleted, because a list of open problems that
  7004  (6319)  %     keeps solved ones on it is worse than no list: it sends the next person
  7005  (6320)  %     to work that is already done.
  7006  (6321)  %
  7007  (6322)  %       "Nothing has been compiled."   It compiles and it runs. See the header.
  7008  (6323)  %
  7009  (6324)  %       "No way to leave. kLeave is declared and never sent."   /leave and
  7010  (6325)  %       /quit both send it - handleCommand, which was written after this line
  7011  (6326)  %       and never came back to cross it off.
  7012  (6327)  %
  7013  (6328)  %     The remaining items below were re-read against the code on 2026-08-18 and
  7014  (6329)  %     are still true.
  7015  (6330)  %
  7016  (6331)  %  2. THE LOOP IS A BUSY ONE. It reads the keyboard and the port as fast as the
  7017  (6332)  %     machine will go, which on a shared SINTRAN is antisocial. The server does
  7018  (6333)  %     not have this problem - it BLOCKS on XFWTF - but a client watching two
  7019  (6334)  %     things cannot block on either one.
  7020  (6335)  %
  7021  (6336)  %     THE EXPERIMENT TO RUN, which is one specific thing rather than a research
  7022  (6337)  %     project. Both halves have a documented wake:
  7023  (6338)  %
  7024  (6339)  %       the terminal   after a no-wait call, "SuspendProgram or WaitForRestart
  7025  (6340)  %                      may passivate the program afterwards. The program
  7026  (6341)  %                      restarts when input or output to the device is
  7027  (6342)  %                      completed."          (Monitor Calls, NoWaitSwitch)
  7028  (6343)  %
  7029  (6344)  %       the port       XMPFRCV with XFWAK and WITHOUT XFWTF returns at once and
  7030  (6345)  %                      ARMS a wake: "the next transmission to this port will
  7031  (6346)  %                      lead to a wake up of the receiver task". Note that "when
  7032  (6347)  %                      the wake up is done, the message is not received, and so
  7033  (6348)  %                      the receiving must be repeated."   (COSMOS guide, XFWAK)
  7034  (6349)  %
  7035  (6350)  %     So the shape to try is: arm the port with XFWAK, then WaitForRestart, and
  7036  (6351)  %     on waking re-issue the receive and re-read the keyboard, because either
  7037  (6352)  %     could be what woke us.
  7038  (6353)  %
  7039  (6354)  %     AND THE OTHER ROUTE IS OPEN AFTER ALL: MAC and NPL are BOTH installed on
  7040  (6355)  %     D100 (verified 2026-08-18 with LIST-REENTRANT, which is the command that
  7041  (6356)  %     answers this - subsystems live in shared memory, not as :PROG files, so
  7042  (6357)  %     LIST-FILES finding nothing proved nothing). So TNOWAIT (307B) can be reached
  7043  (6358)  %     the way the PLANC manual says: an interface routine assembled in MAC and
  7044  (6359)  %     loaded BEFORE PLANC-1BANK-F00. That is now a real option to weigh against the XFWAK
  7045  (6360)  %     experiment below, not a closed door.
  7046  (6361)  %
  7047  (6362)  %     WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED is whether an XMSG wake-up actually restarts a
  7048  (6363)  %     BACKGROUND PROGRAM passivated that way. The call that makes the wake-up
  7049  (6364)  %     context explicit, XMPFWDF, is for DRIVERS - the guide says so, and adds
  7050  (6365)  %     "not permitted for RT-programs". So this is one measurement, not a
  7051  (6366)  %     deduction, and until it is made the loop stays as it is: honest and rude.
  7052  (6367)  %     A short HOLD is the fallback if the wake does not arrive.
  7053  (6368)  %
  7054  (6369)  %  3. THE NAME IS NO LONGER FIXED AT RONNY, and this item is kept in corrected
  7055  (6370)  %     form because it went through a wrong shape on the way.
  7056  (6371)  %
  7057  (6372)  %     It was briefly a PROMPT at start-up, and that was a mistake: on a machine
  7058  (6373)  %     where you have already told SINTRAN who you are by logging in, being
  7059  (6374)  %     asked your name again on every run is nagging. It is now read from
  7060  (6375)  %     CHAT:CNFG in your own file area, changed with /nick, and saved only when
  7061  (6376)  %     the ROOM confirms the rename - never when the request is sent, because
  7062  (6377)  %     the room can refuse it.
  7063  (6378)  %
  7064  (6379)  %     WHAT IS STILL MISSING is the right fallback for somebody who has never
  7065  (6380)  %     renamed: it should be the SINTRAN user name and it is still RONNY.
  7066  (6381)  %     GetUserName is GUSNA 214B, and the PLANC runtime provides no MON214 -
  7067  (6382)  %     the same gap CreateFile 221B falls into, which is why creating CHAT:CNFG
  7068  (6383)  %     goes through MON70 instead. There is no command-level way round 214, so
  7069  (6384)  %     it needs the route the PLANC manual describes in as many words: an
  7070  (6385)  %     interface routine assembled in MAC and loaded BEFORE the PLANC runtime
  7071  (6386)  %     library. MAC is installed here - LIST-REENTRANT shows it - so this is
  7072  (6387)  %     work, not a wall.
  7073  (6388)  %
  7074  (6389)  %  4. The ON ROUTINEERROR form is taken from the Monitor Calls manual's own
  7075  (6390)  %     PLANC example for InByte. That ErrCode 3 specifically means "nothing was
  7076  (6391)  %     typed" is from the NoWaitSwitch entry, which says so for no-wait calls
  7077  (6392)  %     generally rather than for InByte in particular. If some other code turns
  7078  (6393)  %     up in practice, the handler above prints and carries on rather than
  7079  (6394)  %     mistaking it for a keystroke.
  7080  (6395)  % ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  7081  (6396)  
  7082  (6397)  